var WALKTHROUGHS_ACCOUNTING = {
"2025": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1A: Final Accounts - Sole Trader (M McConnell)",
     q:"You are given a trial balance for M McConnell's business at 31/12/2024, plus 9 adjustments.\n\nTrial balance figures include:\nSales: 884,300\nOpening Stock: 62,500\nPurchases: 622,400\nClosing Stock: 84,000\nDelivery Vans (cost 132,000): given\n4% Investments: 120,000\nAdvertising (incorporating 3 months investment income): 4,200\nInsurance: 20,900\nPatents: 62,400\n6% Mortgage: 220,000\nRent: 17,600\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock includes damaged items costing 10,600 now worth 8,300 (NRV)\n(ii) Patents written off over 6-year period commencing 2022\n(iii) Delivery van (cost 35,000, bought 31/08/2022) traded in 30/06/2024. New van cost 48,000, trade-in allowance 23,200. Net cheque (24,800) incorrectly recorded as purchase of trading stock.\n(iv) Goods sold on credit for 14,760 (including 23% VAT) not yet dispatched at year-end. No entry made.\n(v) Suspense adjustments for mortgage interest and insurance\n(vi) Cheque for 1,600 received for debt of 3,200 previously written off as bad. Debtor wants to continue trading and will pay other half within a month.\n(vii) McConnell took goods for personal use worth 12,900 at retail (cost + 20%)\n(viii) New warehouse purchased for 102,150 including 13.5% VAT\n(ix) Provide for: investment income due, mortgage interest due (20% private), BDP 5% of debtors, depreciation on buildings at 2% of cost, vans at 15% of cost from purchase to sale date\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading Profit & Loss Account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance Sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"TRADING ACCOUNT:\nSales: 884,300 + 12,000 = 896,300\n(Adjustment iv: 14,760 x 100/123 = 12,000 net of VAT)\n\nOpening Stock: 62,500\n(Direct from trial balance)\n\nPurchases: 622,400 - 24,800 - 10,750 = 586,850\n(Remove van trade-in net cheque: 48,000 - 23,200 = 24,800)\n(Remove goods for own use at cost: 12,900 / 1.20 = 10,750)\n\nClosing Stock: 84,000 - 2,300 - 10,000 = 71,700\n(Damaged stock write-down: 10,600 - 8,300 = 2,300)\n(Goods sold but not dispatched at cost: 12,000 / 1.20 = 10,000)\n\nCost of Sales: 62,500 + 586,850 - 71,700 = 577,650\nGross Profit: 896,300 - 577,650 = 318,650\n\nEXPENSES:\nDepreciation Delivery Vans: 14,550 + 2,625 + 3,600 = 20,775\n(Remaining old vans: (132,000 - 35,000) x 15% = 14,550)\n(Old van to disposal date: 35,000 x 15% x 6/12 = 2,625)\n(New van from purchase: 48,000 x 15% x 6/12 = 3,600)\n\nAdvertising: 4,200 + 900 = 5,100\n(Add back 3 months investment income netted: 120,000 x 3% x 3/12 = 900)\n\nInsurance: 20,900 + 10,800 - 750 = 30,950\n(Suspense correction + accrual adjustments)\n\nPatent Written Off: 62,400 / 4 = 15,600\n(Remaining book value divided by remaining useful life of 4 years)\n\nDepreciation Buildings: 757,850 x 2% = 15,157\n(Building cost adjusted for new warehouse, excluding land)\n\nOPERATING INCOME:\nDiscount Received: 9,900 + 150 = 10,050\n\nBad Debt Recovered: 3,200\n(Full 3,200 recovered: 1,600 cash + 1,600 now owed as debtor)\n\nRent: 17,600 + 8,800 = 26,400\n(Monthly rent: 17,600/8 = 2,200; accrual for 4 months: 2,200 x 4 = 8,800)\n\nNET PROFIT: 113,700\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nIntangible Assets:\nPatent: 62,400 - 15,600 = 46,800\n\nTangible Fixed Assets:\nBuildings: 1,100,000 (revalued)\nDelivery Vans: Cost 145,000 (132,000 - 35,000 + 48,000); Acc Dep 32,650; NBV 112,350\n\nFinancial Assets:\n4% Investment: 120,000\n\nCurrent Assets:\nStock: 71,700\nDebtors: 89,860 less BDP 4,493\nVAT: 23,190\n\nCurrent Liabilities:\nCreditors: 61,350\nMortgage Interest Due: 3,300\nBank (overdraft): 40,800\nPAYE: 19,800\n\nLong-term Liabilities:\n6% Mortgage: 220,000\n\nCapital & Reserves:\nCapital: 782,600\nRevaluation Reserve: 352,307\nAdd Net Profit: 113,700\nLess Drawings: 24,450 (10,750 goods + private portions)"},

    {num:2, label:"Q1B: Final Accounts - Company (Cahill Ltd)",
     q:"Cahill Ltd is a limited company. You are given a trial balance and adjustments.\n\nAuthorised Capital: 1,200,000 ordinary + 500,000 preference = 1,700,000\nIssued Capital: 800,000 ordinary + 450,000 preference\n\nKey trial balance figures include:\nPurchases: 1,440,000\nSales: 1,860,000\nClosing Stock: 86,200\nCommission: 12,000\nP&L Balance b/f: 43,400 (debit - accumulated loss)\nCapital Reserve: 20,000\n\nAdjustments include:\n- Van trade-in (net cheque 37,000 treated as purchase)\n- Stock destroyed by fire (compensation 11,000)\n- Materials taken for building new store (58,000)\n- Goods in transit: invoice 13,776 incl 23% VAT not recorded\n- Damaged stock: cost 5,200, NRV 2,400\n- MD bonus commission: 3% on sales over 1,200,000 plus 4% on sales over 1,600,000\n- Dividends paid: 26,000\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading Profit & Loss Account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance Sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"TRADING P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: 1,860,000\n\nPurchases: 1,440,000 - 37,000 - 11,000 - 58,000 + 11,200 = 1,345,200\n(Remove van trade-in net cheque: -37,000)\n(Remove fire-damaged stock compensation: -11,000)\n(Remove materials for new store: -58,000 capital expenditure)\n(Add goods in transit: 13,776 x 100/123 = +11,200)\n\nClosing Stock: 86,200 - 2,800 + 11,200 = 94,600\n(Damaged stock write-down: 5,200 - 2,400 = 2,800)\n(Add goods in transit: +11,200)\n\nCommission: 12,000 + 18,200 = 30,200\n(TB figure + MD bonus accrual)\n\nP&L BALANCE:\nNet Profit: 157,755\nLess Dividends Paid: (26,000)\nRetained Profit: 131,755\nLess Opening P&L Balance (debit): (43,400)\nClosing P&L Balance: 88,355\n\nBALANCE SHEET - Capital Section:\nAuthorised Capital: 1,200,000 ordinary + 500,000 preference = 1,700,000\nIssued Capital: 800,000 ordinary + 450,000 preference = 1,250,000\nCapital Reserve: 20,000\nP&L Balance: 88,355"},

    {num:3, label:"Q2: Debtors Control Account (G. Flavin)",
     q:"G. Flavin's debtors ledger control account shows balances of 32,500 Dr and 580 Cr on 31/12/2024. These don't agree with the schedule (list) of individual debtors.\n\nErrors discovered:\n(i) Discount of 52 given to customer but disallowed. Recorded as discount allowed, entered twice in discount account. Amount = 52 x 2 = 104.\n(ii) Interest of 70 charged to customer but posted as 95 to interest account. Customer complained, reduced to 35. Reduction put on wrong side of customer's account.\n(iii) Debtor due refund of 580 but only half (290) refunded. No entry for remaining 290.\n(iv) Sales of 2,360 entered on credit side of customer's account. 1,400 was cash sales (correct in cash book). Credit sale of 960 on wrong side.\n(v) Invoice of 1,968 entered in day book but not posted to customer's account.\n(vi) Returns of 400 accepted with 10% restocking charge initially, later reduced to 6% (24). Reduction of 16 not recorded.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Adjusted Debtors Control Account (28 marks)\n(b) Schedule of Debtors showing adjustments (24 marks)\n(c)(i) Why might a debtors control account have a credit balance?\n(c)(ii) Benefits of maintaining control accounts (8 marks)",
     a:"(a) ADJUSTED DEBTORS CONTROL ACCOUNT:\nDebit Side:\nBalance b/d: 32,500\nDiscount disallowed (i): 104\nRefund due (iii): 290\nSales correction (iv): 960\nRestocking charge (vi): 24\n\nCredit Side:\nBalance b/d: 580\nInterest adjustment (ii): 60\nBalance c/d: 33,528\n\nCredit balance c/d: 290\n\n(b) SCHEDULE OF DEBTORS:\nBalance per list: 27,746\nAdd: Refund (iii): 290\nAdd: Sales error (iv): 3,320\nAdd: Invoice (v): 1,968\nLess: Interest (ii): (70)\nLess: Credit note (vi): (16)\nAdjusted balance: 33,238\n\n(c)(i) Why a credit balance? A credit balance means the business owes money TO the debtor:\n- Customer paid then returned goods (refund owed)\n- Overpayment by debtor\n- Discount granted after full payment received\n\n(c)(ii) Benefits of control accounts:\n- Acts as a check on accuracy by comparing control total to schedule total\n- Helps locate errors quickly by narrowing the search\n- Useful for finding credit sales from incomplete records\n- Allows amounts owed by debtors to be ascertained quickly"},

    {num:4, label:"Q3: Revaluation of Fixed Assets (Boyne Ltd)",
     q:"Boyne Ltd owns freehold property. Over 5 years (2020-2024), the property is revalued, parts are sold, extensions are built, and more revaluations happen.\n\nKey events:\n2020: Property revalued from 740,000 to 980,000. Land revalued at 330,000.\n2021: Land sold for 430,000 (revalued cost 330,000 including 50,000 uplift)\n2022: Purchased buildings for 320,000. Extension built: contractor 150,000 + own employees 90,000 + legal fees 10,000 = 570,000 additions.\n2023: Property revalued at 1,403,000 (15% increase on each building)\n2024: 2020 buildings sold for 820,000. Remaining property revalued to 840,000.\n\nDepreciation: Buildings at 2% p.a. straight line. Land NOT depreciated.\nFull year depreciation in year of acquisition, none in year of disposal.\n\nRequired:\n(a)(i) Land & Buildings Account, Provision for Depreciation Account, Revaluation Reserve Account, and Disposal Accounts for years 2020-2024 (52 marks)\n(b)(i) Why revalue fixed assets?\n(b)(ii) Explain the Consistency Concept (8 marks)",
     a:"LAND & BUILDINGS ACCOUNT:\n2020: Bal b/d 740,000 + Revaluation Reserve 240,000 = Bal c/d 980,000\n2021: Bal b/d 980,000 => Disposal (land) 330,000; Bal c/d 650,000\n2022: Bal b/d 650,000 + Bank 320,000 + Bank 150,000 + Wages 90,000 + Bank 10,000 = Bal c/d 1,220,000\n2023: Bal b/d 1,220,000 + Revaluation Reserve = Bal c/d 1,403,000\n2024: Bal b/d 1,403,000 => Disposal + Revaluation; Bal c/d 840,000\n\nPROVISION FOR DEPRECIATION ACCOUNT:\n2020: Bal b/d written off via Revaluation Reserve 36,800; P&L charge 13,000 (650,000 x 2%); Bal c/d 13,000\n2021: Bal b/d 13,000; P&L charge 13,000; Bal c/d 26,000\n2022: Bal b/d 26,000; P&L charge 24,400 (13,000 old + 11,400 new); Bal c/d 50,400\n2023: Written off via Revaluation Reserve; new charge on revalued amount\n2024: Disposal entry; remaining depreciation\n\nDISPOSAL OF LAND (2021):\nDr: L&B 330,000\nCr: Bank 430,000, P&L Profit 100,000\nTransfer 50,000 from Revaluation Reserve to Revenue Reserve (realised gain)\n\nDISPOSAL OF BUILDINGS (2024):\nDr: L&B at revalued cost\nCr: Acc Dep, Bank 820,000, P&L (profit or loss)\nTransfer realised revaluation surplus to Revenue Reserve\n\n(b)(i) Why revalue fixed assets?\n1. Shows assets at true market value = true and fair view of financial position\n2. Provides useful information to users of accounts\n3. Enables ratios to be calculated more accurately\n4. Depreciation will not be understated, so profits won't be overstated\n\n(b)(ii) The Consistency Concept:\nAccounting items must be treated the same way from one period to the next. If you choose to depreciate buildings at 2% straight line, you must continue using that method every year. If you change, you must give the reason and state the effect on profit."},

    {num:5, label:"Q4: Service Firm - Dentist (B. Bennett)",
     q:"B. Bennett is a dentist. You are given opening assets/liabilities AND a Receipts & Payments account. Convert to proper final accounts.\n\nOpening assets include:\nSurgery: 400,000\nEquipment: given\n5% Investments: 40,000\nMedical card scheme due: 9,400\nDental supplies creditors and stock\n\nReceipts & Payments:\nMedical Card Scheme received: 55,200\nPrivate Patients received: 109,700\nEquipment sold: 6,150 (cost 15,000)\nDental Supplies paid: 31,600\nCleaning: 4,800\nTelephone: 5,200\nInsurance paid: 19,800\nSponsorship: 2,200\nReceptionist Wages: 31,450\nLight & Heat paid: 7,200\nLocum Dentist: 1,200\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Investment income: 40,000 x 5% = 2,000\n(ii) Bank charges 125 not recorded\n(iii) Equipment sold: cost 15,000, depreciation 20% per annum for relevant period\n(iv) Closing medical card scheme due: 10,100; closing private fees due: 2,300\n(v) Opening and closing dental supplies stock and creditors given\n(vi) Insurance prepaid: opening 3,600, closing 4,950\n(vii) Light & heat: only 75% relates to surgery; accruals given\n(viii) Locum wages owed: 900\n(ix) Depreciation: Surgery 2% of cost; Equipment 20% of cost\n\nRequired:\n(a) Income & Expenditure Account (32 marks)\n(b) Statement of Affairs (20 marks)\n(c)(i) Explain Statement of Reserves\n(c)(ii) Explain Special Purpose P&L Account (8 marks)",
     a:"INCOME & EXPENDITURE ACCOUNT:\n\nINCOME:\nMedical Card Scheme: 55,200 - 9,400 + 10,100 = 55,900\n(Cash received - opening due + closing due)\nPrivate Patients: 109,700 + 2,300 = 112,000\n(Cash received + closing fees due)\nInvestment Income: 40,000 x 5% = 2,000\nProfit on Equipment Sale: 1,150\n(Cost 15,000 - Acc Dep 10,000 = NBV 5,000; Sold 6,150; Profit 1,150)\nTotal Income: 171,050\n\nEXPENDITURE:\nDental Supplies: 31,600 - 4,500 + 10,500 - 9,800 + 3,200 = 31,000\n(Cash paid - opening creditors + closing creditors - opening stock + closing stock adjustments)\nCleaning: 4,800\nTelephone: 5,200\nInsurance: 19,800 + 3,600 - 4,950 = 18,450\n(Paid + opening prepaid used - closing prepaid)\nSponsorship: 2,200\nWages of Receptionist: 31,450\nLight & Heat: (7,200 + 1,600 + 520 - 2,330) x 75% = 6,990\n(Only 75% relates to surgery; 25% private)\nLocum Dentist: 1,200 + 900 = 2,100\n(Paid + wages owed)\nDepreciation Surgery: 400,000 x 2% = 8,000\nDepreciation Equipment: 10,000 + 20,320 = 30,320\nBank Charges: 125\nTotal Expenditure: 140,635\n\nNET PROFIT: 171,050 - 140,635 = 30,415\n\n(c)(i) Statement of Reserves:\nFinds the net worth of a service firm. Lists assets and liabilities at the START of the year. Included in P&L as opening profit and loss balance.\n\n(c)(ii) Special Purpose P&L Account:\nSome service firms run extra activities to boost income (e.g., sports shop in a gym, sweet shop in a nursing home). The income and expenses of that activity are recorded in a separate special purpose P&L account, and only the net profit/loss is transferred to the main Income & Expenditure Account."},

    {num:6, label:"Q5: Interpretation of Accounts (Technik plc)",
     q:"Technik plc is an IT company. You are given their P&L summary, Balance Sheet, and last year's ratios.\n\nKey data:\nTotal Sales: 1,898,000\nOperating Profit: 232,000\nInvestment Income: 14,000\nInterest: 14,000\nNet Profit after pref dividend: 199,000\nPreference Dividend: 33,000\nOrdinary Dividend: 89,000\nDebtors: 103,000\nDebtors collection period: 2 months\nCapital Employed: 1,885,000\nOrdinary Shares: 900,000\nMarket Price: 160 cent\n\nLast year's ratios given for comparison:\nROCE: 10.8%\nP/E: 4.67 years\nDividend Cover: 3.75 times\nAcid Test: 1.6:1\nGearing: 42%\nDPS: 8.95c\n\nRequired:\n(a) Calculate: (i) Cash Sales (ii) ROCE (iii) Interest Cover (iv) P/E Ratio (v) Dividend Cover (50 marks)\n(b) Written analysis of Profitability, Liquidity, Gearing, Dividend Policy, Market Value, Sector (40 marks)\n(c) Evaluate two financing options for 400,000: issue shares vs 7% debenture (10 marks)",
     a:"(a) CALCULATIONS:\n\n(i) Cash Sales = 1,280,000\nDebtors collection period = 2 months\nDebtors x 12 / Credit Sales = 2\n103,000 x 12 / 2 = 618,000 (credit sales)\nCash Sales = 1,898,000 - 618,000 = 1,280,000\n\n(ii) ROCE = 13.05%\n(Operating Profit + Investment Income) / Capital Employed x 100\n= (232,000 + 14,000) / 1,885,000 x 100 = 13.05%\n\n(iii) Interest Cover = 17.57 times\n(Operating Profit + Investment Income) / Interest\n= (232,000 + 14,000) / 14,000 = 17.57 times\n\n(iv) P/E Ratio = 7.24 years\nEPS = (Net Profit - Pref Dividend) / Ordinary Shares\n= (232,000 - 33,000) / 900,000 = 22.11 cent\nP/E = Market Price / EPS = 160 / 22.11 = 7.24 years\n\n(v) Dividend Cover = 2.24 times\n= (Net Profit - Pref Dividend) / Ordinary Dividend\n= (232,000 - 33,000) / 89,000 = 2.24 times\n\n(b) WRITTEN ANALYSIS:\n\nProfitability (7 marks):\nROCE improved from 10.8% to 13.05%. Better than risk-free rate (0-3%), debenture rate (7%), and preference dividend (6%). EPS decreased from 30c to 22.11c.\n\nLiquidity (6 marks):\nAcid test ratio is 1.31:1 (down from 1.6:1 in 2023). Company has 1.31 in liquid assets for every 1 owed. No liquidity problem but slightly conservative.\n\nGearing (7 marks):\nGearing improved from 42% to 39.79%. Debt to equity ratio is 66.08%. Company is lowly geared (financed more by equity than debt). Interest cover is excellent at 17.57 times.\n\nDividend Policy (7 marks):\nDPS improved from 8c to 9.89c. Dividend cover decreased from 3.75 to 2.24 times. % of profits paid as dividends: 44.64%. Dividend yield improved from 5.71% to 6.18%.\n\nMarket Value (5 marks):\nShare price improved from 1.40 to 1.60. P/E ratio increased from 4.67 to 7.24 years.\n\nSector (5 marks):\nIT sector - promising due to AI growth, remote working demand.\n\n(c) FINANCING OPTIONS:\nOption 1 - Issue ordinary shares: Gearing stays at 32.82% (lowly geared). Shareholders get voting rights and dividend of 9.89c per share. Additional dividend cost = 24,725.\nOption 2 - Issue 7% debenture: Gearing jumps to 50.33% (highly geared). Interest = 28,000 per year. Debenture holders have no voting rights but get paid before shareholders. May make it harder to borrow more in future."},

    {num:7, label:"Q6: Incomplete Records (D. O'Riordan)",
     q:"D. O'Riordan bought a business on 01/01/2024 but didn't keep proper books. Scattered information provided.\n\nCash Payments: Lodgements to bank 124,000; Cash purchases 66,500; Cash expenses 24,000\nBank Payments: Creditors payments 45,500; Insurance 3,600; Other expenses\nBank Lodgements: From debtors 44,200\n\nOpening: Debtors 17,400; Creditors 18,600; Cash 600; Insurance prepaid 580\nClosing: Stock 16,800 (includes heating oil 800 and goods 6,400 not dispatched for credit sale); Debtors 16,600; Creditors 16,300; Cash 960; Insurance prepaid 1,200\n\nWeekly drawings: goods 120 (52 weeks)\nCredit sale at year end: 8,000 not dispatched\n\nPremises: Cost 385,000 (land 50,000) + new building 240,000 (land 30,000) acquired 01/08/2024\nEquipment: 48,000 purchased during year\nDelivery Van: 34,000\nLoan: 275,000 at 6% from 01/08/2024 (20% private)\nRent received: 26,400 (15,400 prepaid)\nLight & Heat: 13,600 paid + accruals (25% private)\nBDP: 4% of debtors\nDepreciation: Premises 2%, Equipment varied, Vans varied\nStanding order charity: 3,200 (goes to drawings)\nGoods taken for personal use: 7,800 at retail\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading P&L Account (52 marks)\n(b) Balance Sheet (40 marks)\n(c)(i) What is Net Worth?\n(c)(ii) Limitations of Incomplete Records (8 marks)",
     a:"FINDING SALES:\nCredit Sales: 44,200 + 16,600 + 8,000 - 17,400 = 51,400\n(Cash from debtors + closing debtors + credit sale not dispatched - opening debtors)\nCash Sales: 124,000 + 24,000 + 66,500 + 7,800 + 960 - 600 = 222,660\n(All cash accounted for: lodgements + cash payments + cash purchases + stock drawings at retail + closing cash - opening cash)\nTotal Sales: 51,400 + 222,660 = 274,060\n\nPURCHASES:\nCash Purchases: 66,500\nCredit Purchases: 45,500 + 16,300 - 18,600 = 43,200\n(Paid to creditors + closing creditors - opening creditors)\nLess Drawings of Goods: 120 x 52 = (6,240)\nTotal Purchases: 66,500 + 43,200 - 6,240 = 103,460\n\nCLOSING STOCK: 16,800 - 800 - 6,400 = 9,600\n(Remove heating oil 800; remove goods for credit sale not dispatched 6,400)\n\nTRADING P&L:\nSales: 274,060\nPurchases: 103,460\nClosing Stock: (9,600)\nGross Profit: 180,200 (approx)\n\nExpenses:\nInsurance: 580 + 3,600 - 1,200 = 2,980\nGeneral Expenses: 24,000 - 800 = 23,200 (subtract heating oil)\nLight & Heat: (13,600 + 2,100 - 800 - 3,725) x 75% = 11,175 x 75% (only 75% business)\nDepreciation Premises: (385,000 - 50,000) x 2% + (240,000 - 30,000) x 2% x 5/12 = 6,700 + 1,750 = 8,450\nIncrease in BDP: 4% of 24,600 = 984\nStanding Order (charity): 3,200 - goes to drawings, NOT expense\n\nAdd Rent Income: 26,400 - 15,400 = 11,000\nLess Interest: 275,000 x 6% x 5/12 = 6,875; less 20% private = 1,375; business = 5,500\n\nNET PROFIT: 106,811\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nGoodwill: 21,920 (price paid 435,000 minus net assets acquired)\nPremises: 750,000 (revalued at year-end)\nEquipment: 48,000\nDelivery Van: 34,000\nDebtors: 24,600 (16,600 + 8,000) less BDP 984\nBank: 83,700 (complex reconciliation)\nLoan: 247,500 (275,000 - 27,500 instalment)\nRevaluation Reserve: 125,000 + 8,450 = 133,450\nDrawings: 6,240 + 7,800 + 1,375 + 3,725 = 19,140 (goods + cash + private interest + private L&H)\n\n(c)(i) Net Worth: The total value of what a person or business owns (assets) minus what they owe (liabilities). Positive net worth = own more than owe.\n\n(c)(ii) Limitations of Incomplete Records:\n- Full accounts cannot be accurately prepared; essential information missing\n- Without double-entry, difficult to cross-check transactions; fraud more likely\n- Owner has limited information for decision-making\n- Information unreliable; difficult to prepare accounts for legal purposes (e.g., tax returns)"},

    {num:8, label:"Q7: Tabular Statement (Joyce Ltd)",
     q:"Joyce Ltd's opening balance sheet is given. Then 10 transactions happen across the year (January to December). Record each transaction's effect on every asset, liability, and equity item in a table.\n\nOpening Balance Sheet includes:\nBuildings, Vans (with acc dep), Debtors, Creditors, Share Capital, Share Premium, P&L balance, Bank, VAT, BDP, Stock\n\nKey transactions:\nJanuary - Bought adjoining business: Buildings 210,000, Vans 55,000, Debtors 13,000, Creditors 9,500. Paid by issuing 250,000 shares at 1.25 each. Goodwill arises.\nMarch - Van trade-in: Old van (cost 26,000, acc dep 14,250) traded in. New van 47,000. Net bank payment.\nJune - Revaluation: Land & Buildings revalued to 900,000.\nOctober - Bad debt recovery from M. Houlihan: Cash 1,200 received (75% of debt). Remaining 25% (400) still owed.\nOther transactions through the year: rent, rates, depreciation, BDP adjustments.\n\nRequired: Complete the tabular statement showing the effect of each transaction (100 marks, approximately 2 marks per correct entry)",
     a:"KEY ENTRIES:\n\nJanuary - Business Acquisition:\nBuildings: +210,000\nVans: +55,000\nDebtors: +13,000\nCreditors: +9,500\nShare Capital: +250,000 (250,000 shares at 1 each)\nShare Premium: +62,500 (250,000 shares x 25c premium)\nGoodwill: +44,000 (balancing figure: price paid minus net assets)\n\nMarch - Van Trade-in:\nVans (cost): -26,000 (old) +47,000 (new) = net +21,000\nVan Acc Dep: +14,250 (old van dep removed)\nBank: -33,500 (net payment: 47,000 - 13,500 trade-in)\nP&L: loss on disposal entry\n\nJune - Revaluation:\nLand & Buildings: increase to 900,000 (+110,000)\nBuildings Acc Dep: written off (+36,000)\nRevaluation Reserve: +146,000\n\nOctober - Bad Debt Recovery:\nBank: +1,200\nDebtors: +1,600 (full debt reinstated; 1,200 paid = 75%, so debt = 1,600)\nP&L: bad debt recovered credit\n\nYear-end Adjustments:\nDepreciation L&B: (18,310)\nDepreciation Vehicles: (18,550)\nBDP: (1,389)\nRates prepaid: 2,500\n\nFINAL BALANCES must satisfy: Total Assets = Total Liabilities + Total Equity\nApproximately 50 individual entries worth 2 marks each."},

    {num:9, label:"Q8: Marginal Costing (Kells Ltd)",
     q:"Kells Ltd makes a single product. P&L for 42,000 units sold.\n\nSelling price per unit: 36.40\nTotal Sales: 1,528,800\nMaterials: 537,600 (variable)\nDirect Labour: 361,200 (variable)\nFactory Overheads: 136,500 (20% variable, 80% fixed)\nSelling Expenses: 14,280 (5% commission = variable, rest fixed)\nAdministration: 95,700 (fixed)\nNet Profit: 307,800\n\nRequired:\n(a) Break-even point and margin of safety (15 marks)\n(b) Units needed for 25% increase in profit (12 marks)\n(c) New scenario: SP = 34, units = 45,000, fixed costs +13,000 - calculate profit (14 marks)\n(d) Required selling price if fixed costs up 10%, same profit, same volume (13 marks)\n(e) Units for 10% profit on sales at SP = 35 (14 marks)\n(f)(i) Explain Sensitivity Analysis (ii) Step Fixed Costs (iii) Step Fixed Cost Graph (12 marks)",
     a:"MARGINAL COST STATEMENT:\nSales (42,000 units): 1,528,800 (36.40 per unit)\nVariable Costs:\n  Materials: 537,600 (12.80 per unit)\n  Direct Labour: 361,200 (8.60 per unit)\n  Factory OH (20%): 27,300 (0.65 per unit)\n  Sales Commission (5%): 76,440 (1.82 per unit)\nTotal Variable Costs: 1,002,540 (23.87 per unit)\nContribution: 526,260 (12.53 per unit)\nFixed Costs:\n  Factory OH (80%): 109,200\n  Selling (95%): 13,560\n  Administration: 95,700\nTotal Fixed Costs: 218,460\nNet Profit: 307,800\n\n(a) Break-Even Point: Fixed Costs / CPU = 218,460 / 12.53 = 17,435 units\nMargin of Safety: 42,000 - 17,435 = 24,565 units\nMeaning: Must sell at least 17,435 units to cover all costs. Current buffer of 24,565 units.\n\n(b) Units for 25% profit increase:\nTarget profit = 307,800 x 1.25 = 384,750\nUnits = (Fixed Costs + Target Profit) / CPU = (218,460 + 384,750) / 12.53 = 48,142 units\n\n(c) New scenario (SP = 34, units = 45,000):\nVariable cost per unit (excl commission): 12.80 + 8.60 + 0.65 = 22.05\nNew commission: 5% x 34 = 1.70\nTotal VC per unit: 22.05 + 1.70 = 23.75\nNew CPU: 34 - 23.75 = 10.25\nNew fixed costs: 218,460 + 13,000 = 231,460\nProfit: (45,000 x 10.25) - 231,460 = 461,250 - 231,460 = 229,790\n\n(d) Required selling price (fixed costs +10%, same profit 307,800, same volume 42,000):\nNew fixed costs: 218,460 x 1.10 = 240,306\nRequired CPU: (240,306 + 307,800) / 42,000 = 13.05\n0.95X - 22.05 = 13.05\n0.95X = 35.10\nX = 36.95\n\n(e) Units for 10% profit on sales at SP = 35:\nProfit per unit = 10% x 35 = 3.50\nCommission = 5% x 35 = 1.75\nVC per unit = 22.05 + 1.75 = 23.80\nCPU = 35 - 23.80 = 11.20\nX = 218,460 / (11.20 - 3.50) = 218,460 / 7.70 = 28,372 units\n\n(f)(i) Sensitivity Analysis: Also called 'what if' analysis. Shows the effect on profit of changes in selling price, sales volume, variable costs, or fixed costs. Parts (c), (d), (e) are all examples.\n\n(f)(ii) Step Fixed Costs: Costs that are fixed within a range but jump to a new level when that range is exceeded. Example: rent is 5,000 for 0-10,000 units; jumps to 15,000 if production exceeds 10,000.\n\n(f)(iii) Step Fixed Cost Graph: A staircase-shaped graph. X-axis = output (units), Y-axis = cost. Line is flat (horizontal) within each range, then jumps vertically to the next level. Each step represents a new cost level when capacity is exceeded."},

    {num:10, label:"Q9: Budgeting (Jenkins Ltd)",
     q:"Jenkins Ltd makes two products: Shaded (sells at 240 each) and Clear (sells at 320 each).\n\nSales forecast: Shaded 12,800 units, Clear 9,600 units\nOpening stock FG: Shaded 720, Clear 580\nClosing stock FG: 10% increase on opening\n\nMaterial usage per unit:\nShaded: Material X 4kg, Material Y 5kg\nClear: Material X 6kg, Material Y 3kg\n\nMaterial prices: X = 3.50/kg (was 3.40), Y = 6.50/kg (was 5.50)\nOpening RM stock: X 6,400kg, Y 5,100kg\nClosing RM stock: 10% increase on opening\n\nLabour: 17/hour. Shaded = 6 hrs, Clear = 9 hrs\nVariable OH: 8.50 per labour hour\nFixed OH: 656,616 total\n\nRequired:\n(a) Production Budget (12 marks)\n(b) Raw Materials Purchases Budget (18 marks)\n(c) Production Cost/Manufacturing Budget (18 marks)\n(d) Unit Cost of Closing Stock (11 marks)\n(e)(i) Explain Principal Budget Factor (ii) Explain Uncontrollable Costs (12 marks)",
     a:"(a) PRODUCTION BUDGET:\n                          Shaded    Clear\nExpected Sales:           12,800    9,600\nAdd Closing Stock FG:        792      638\n(720 x 1.10 = 792; 580 x 1.10 = 638)\n                          13,592   10,238\nLess Opening Stock FG:      (720)    (580)\nUnits to Produce:         12,872    9,658\n\n(b) RAW MATERIALS PURCHASES BUDGET:\n                        Material X    Material Y\nShaded: 12,872 x 4kg =    51,488\nShaded: 12,872 x 5kg =                  64,360\nClear: 9,658 x 6kg =      57,948\nClear: 9,658 x 3kg =                    28,974\nTotal Usage (kg):         109,436       93,334\nAdd Closing Stock:          7,040        5,610\n(6,400 x 1.10; 5,100 x 1.10)\nLess Opening Stock:        (6,400)      (5,100)\nPurchases (kg):           110,076       93,844\nPrice per kg:                3.50         6.50\nPurchases cost:           385,266      609,986\nTotal Materials Cost: 995,252\n\n(c) PRODUCTION COST BUDGET:\nOpening RM Stock: 6,400 x 3.40 + 5,100 x 5.50 = 49,810\nAdd Purchases: 995,252\nTotal: 1,045,062\nLess Closing RM Stock: 7,040 x 3.50 + 5,610 x 6.50 = (61,105)\nMaterials Used: 983,957\n\nLabour:\nShaded: 12,872 x 6 hrs x 17 = 1,312,944\nClear: 9,658 x 9 hrs x 17 = 1,477,674\nTotal Labour: 2,790,618\n\nVariable Overheads:\nShaded: 12,872 x 6 x 8.50 = 656,472\nClear: 9,658 x 9 x 8.50 = 738,837\nTotal Variable OH: 1,395,309\n\nFixed Overheads: 656,616\n(Fixed OH per labour hour: 656,616 / (12,872 x 6 + 9,658 x 9) = 656,616 / 164,154 = 4 per hour)\n\nCost of Manufacture: 5,826,500\n\n(d) UNIT COST OF CLOSING STOCK:\n                    Shaded    Clear\nMaterial X:          14.00     21.00  (4 x 3.50; 6 x 3.50)\nMaterial Y:          32.50     19.50  (5 x 6.50; 3 x 6.50)\nWages:              102.00    153.00  (6 x 17; 9 x 17)\nVariable OH:         51.00     76.50  (6 x 8.50; 9 x 8.50)\nFixed OH:            24.00     36.00  (6 x 4; 9 x 4)\nUnit Cost:          223.50    306.00\n\nClosing stock values:\nShaded: 792 x 223.50 = 177,012\nClear: 638 x 306.00 = 195,228\nTotal Closing Stock: 372,240\n\n(e)(i) Principal Budget Factor: The factor that limits or constrains a business's activity. Usually sales demand - you can only produce what you can sell. Other examples: machine capacity, skilled labour availability, raw material supply. The budget process starts with this limiting factor.\n\n(e)(ii) Uncontrollable Costs: Costs over which a cost centre manager has no control and therefore cannot be held responsible for variances. Example: local authority rates set by council. Also government levies, insurance premiums set externally, or head office allocated costs."}
  ],

"2022": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1A: Final Accounts - Manufacturing Company (McGuigan Ltd)",
     q:"McGuigan Ltd has an authorised capital of \u20ac2,000,000 divided into 1,500,000 ordinary shares at \u20ac1 each and 500,000 4% preference shares at \u20ac1 each.\n\nTrial balance extracted on 31/12/2021 includes:\nPlant and machinery (cost \u20ac340,000): \u20ac290,000\nFactory buildings (cost \u20ac890,000): \u20ac865,000\nHire of special equipment: \u20ac37,800\nGeneral factory overheads (including suspense): \u20ac126,700\nStocks 01/01/2021: Raw materials \u20ac27,300, WIP \u20ac38,650, Finished goods \u20ac38,400\nDirect factory wages: \u20ac148,000\nPurchase of raw materials: \u20ac760,400\nRoyalty payments: \u20ac29,600\nSale of scrap materials: \u20ac18,950\nSelling expenses: \u20ac45,000, Admin expenses: \u20ac57,900\nSales: \u20ac1,650,000, Discount (net): \u20ac5,350, Rent: \u20ac15,700\n4% Investments acquired 01/04/2021: \u20ac250,000\nInvestment interest received: \u20ac2,800\nP&L balance 01/01/2021: \u20ac69,500\n8% Debentures (incl \u20ac50,000 issued 01/04/2021): \u20ac300,000\nDebenture interest paid: \u20ac5,000\nBad debt provision: \u20ac3,050\nDividends paid: \u20ac27,500, Bank: \u20ac85,000 (overdraft)\nDebtors: \u20ac76,350, Creditors: \u20ac61,400\nIssued ordinary shares: \u20ac460,000, 4% preference shares: \u20ac150,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stocks: RM \u20ac28,300, WIP \u20ac27,600, FG \u20ac58,000\n(ii) Sale to debtor \u20ac10,800 (20% mark-up on cost) not recorded; goods in closing stock\n(iii) \u20ac7,000 in scrap sales is from sale of old machine (cost \u20ac30,000 on 01/09/2016) on 31/03/2021\n(iv) Plant & Machinery depreciated at 15% of cost from date of purchase to date of sale\n(v) Suspense: discount received \u20ac1,400 entered only in discount a/c; credit purchases of RM \u20ac11,000 entered only in creditors a/c\n(vi) Wage increase of 2% backdated 3 months from 01/10/2021\n(vii) Store built by employees: labour \u20ac60,000 (before wage increase) treated as expense; materials \u20ac62,000 taken from stock. Building commenced 01/01/2021, took 12 months. No entry made for store.\n(viii) Bank: firm shows \u20ac85,000 overdraft; bank statement shows \u20ac76,200 overdraft. Credit transfer \u20ac3,500 (70c in \u20ac1 from bankrupt debtor = \u20ac5,000 debt); cheque \u20ac6,300 entered as \u20ac5,700; unpresented cheque \u20ac4,700\n(ix) Provide for: investment income due, debenture interest due, bad debts at 4% of debtors, depreciation on buildings at 2% of cost p.a.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Prepare manufacturing, trading and profit and loss account (75 marks)\n(b) Prepare balance sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"MANUFACTURING ACCOUNT:\nOpening stock RM: \u20ac27,300\nPurchases RM: 760,400 + 11,000 - 62,000 = \u20ac709,400\n(Add suspense credit purchases; deduct materials taken for store)\nClosing stock RM: (\u20ac28,300)\nCost of RM consumed: \u20ac708,400\n\nDirect Costs:\nFactory Wages: 148,000 + 740 - 60,300 = \u20ac88,440\n(Wage increase: 148,000 x 2% x 3/12 = 740; store labour: 60,000 + 300 = 60,300)\nRoyalties: \u20ac29,600\nHire of Special Equipment: \u20ac37,800\nPrime Cost: \u20ac864,240\n\nFactory Overheads:\nGeneral factory overhead: 126,700 - 1,400 - 11,000 = \u20ac114,300\nDepreciation P&M: 1,125 + 46,500 = \u20ac47,625\n(Sold machine: 30,000 x 15% x 3/12 = 1,125 for 2021; Remaining: (340,000 - 30,000) x 15% = 46,500)\nLoss on machine: 30,000 - 7,000 - 20,625 = \u20ac2,375\n(Acc dep on sold machine: 30,000 x 15% x 4.5 yrs = 20,625; NBV = 9,375; sold for 7,000)\nDepreciation on factory building: 2% x 1,012,300 = \u20ac20,246\n(Building cost: 890,000 + 60,300 labour + 62,000 materials = 1,012,300)\nTotal: \u20ac1,048,786\n\nAdd Opening WIP: \u20ac38,650\nLess Closing WIP: (\u20ac27,600) => +\u20ac11,050\nTotal: \u20ac1,059,836\nLess Sale of Scrap: 18,950 - 7,000 = (\u20ac11,950)\nCost of Manufacture: \u20ac1,047,886\n\nTRADING & P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: 1,650,000 + 10,800 = \u20ac1,660,800\nOpening Stock FG: \u20ac38,400\nCost of Manufacture: \u20ac1,047,886\nClosing Stock FG: 58,000 - 9,000 = (\u20ac49,000)\n(Deduct cost of unrecorded sale: 10,800/1.20 = 9,000)\nCost of Sales: \u20ac1,037,286\nGross Profit: \u20ac623,514\n\nExpenses:\nBad debt: 5,000 x 30% = \u20ac1,500 (bankrupt debtor, 70c in \u20ac1 paid, write off 30%)\nSelling expenses: \u20ac45,000\nIncrease in BDP: 3,262 - 3,050 = \u20ac212\nAdmin expenses: \u20ac57,900\nTotal: (\u20ac104,612)\n\nOperating Income:\nRent: \u20ac15,700\nDiscount: \u20ac5,350\nTotal: \u20ac21,050\nOperating Profit: \u20ac539,952\n\nInvestment Income: 4% x 250,000 x 9/12 = \u20ac7,500\nDebenture Interest: 5,000 + 18,000 = \u20ac23,000\n(Full year: 250,000 x 8% = 20,000; new debentures: 50,000 x 8% x 9/12 = 3,000; total = 23,000)\nNet Profit: \u20ac524,452\nDividends paid: (\u20ac27,500)\nP&L balance b/f: \u20ac69,500\nP&L balance 31/12/2021: \u20ac566,452\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nFactory Buildings: Cost \u20ac1,012,300; Acc Dep \u20ac45,246; NBV \u20ac967,054\nPlant & Machinery: Cost \u20ac310,000; Acc Dep \u20ac77,000; NBV \u20ac233,000\nInvestments: \u20ac250,000\nDebtors: 76,350 + 10,800 - 5,000 - 600 = \u20ac81,550\nBDP: 81,550 x 4% = \u20ac3,262\nInvestment income due: 7,500 - 2,800 = \u20ac4,700\nBank (overdraft): 85,000 - 3,500 - 600 = \u20ac80,900 (OR 76,200 + 4,700 = 80,900)\nCreditors: 61,400 - 1,400 = \u20ac60,000\nDebenture Interest due: 23,000 - 5,000 = \u20ac18,000\nWages due: \u20ac740"},

    {num:2, label:"Q1B: Final Accounts - Company (McCormack Ltd)",
     q:"McCormack Ltd has an authorised capital of \u20ac2,500,000 divided into 1,500,000 ordinary shares at \u20ac1 each and 1,000,000 4% preference shares at \u20ac1 each.\n\nTrial balance at 31/12/2021 includes:\nLand and buildings (Land at cost \u20ac1,000,000): \u20ac1,760,000\nOffice equipment (Cost \u20ac460,000): \u20ac357,000\nP&L balance 01/01/2021: \u20ac127,000\nDiscount: \u20ac17,500, Debenture interest paid: \u20ac7,500\n4% Investments 01/04/2021: \u20ac75,000\nStock 01/01/2021: \u20ac51,500, Patents: \u20ac64,000\nPurchases: \u20ac905,000, Sales: \u20ac1,850,000\nBad debt provision: \u20ac7,000\nDebtors: \u20ac249,800, Creditors: \u20ac81,500, Bank: \u20ac68,300 (overdraft)\nWages & salaries: \u20ac196,700\n6% Debentures (incl \u20ac60,000 issued 01/06/2021): \u20ac360,000\nDividends paid: \u20ac65,100, Directors fees: \u20ac91,000\nVAT: \u20ac1,000, PAYE/PRSI/USC: \u20ac27,700\nSales commission: \u20ac32,000, Insurance: \u20ac25,000, Advertising: \u20ac8,400\nOrdinary share capital: \u20ac1,000,000\n4% Preference share capital: \u20ac350,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock \u20ac82,800; includes sale-or-return goods (retail \u20ac9,250 = cost + 25% mark-up)\n(ii) Equipment depreciation: full year in year of acquisition, none in year of disposal. Useful life 6 years, residual 4% of cost. Equipment costing \u20ac65,000 (01/10/2017) traded in for \u20ac45,000 new (allowance \u20ac23,000). Net bank transfer entered correctly but treated as purchase of trading stock.\n(iii) Warehouse (cost \u20ac120,000) destroyed by fire; insurance pays \u20ac115,000\n(iv) Buildings depreciated 2% of cost p.a.\n(v) Bank: firm shows \u20ac68,300 overdraft; statement shows \u20ac48,950 overdraft. Unpresented cheque \u20ac4,950; \u20ac26,700 entered as \u20ac27,600 (difference \u20ac900); credit transfer \u20ac4,500 (30c in \u20ac1 from bankrupt debtor = \u20ac15,000); credit transfer \u20ac9,000 (75% of bad debt recovered, remainder due in 2 months)\n(vi) Patents incorporate 4 months investment income; written off over 5 years from 2020\n(vii) Directors recommend: investment income due, debenture interest due, MD bonus 2% on sales > \u20ac1,500,000, bad debts at 3% of debtors, transfer \u20ac250,000 to capital reserve, corporation tax \u20ac60,000\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading and P&L account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"TRADING P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: \u20ac1,850,000\nOpening stock: \u20ac51,500\nPurchases: 905,000 - 7,400 - 22,000 = \u20ac875,600\n(Sale-or-return cost: 9,250/1.25 = 7,400; equipment net: 45,000 - 23,000 = 22,000)\nClosing stock: 82,800 - 7,400 = \u20ac75,400\nCost of Sales: \u20ac851,700\nGross Profit: \u20ac998,300\n\nDistribution Costs:\nBad debts written off: \u20ac10,500 (bankrupt debtor: 4,500/0.30 = 15,000 owed; 15,000 x 70% = 10,500)\nIncrease in BDP: 7,134 - 7,000 = \u20ac134\nAdvertising: \u20ac8,400\nSales Commission: \u20ac32,000\n\nAdmin Expenses:\nPatents written off: (64,000 + 1,000)/4 = \u20ac16,250\nDepreciation equipment: 440,000 x (96%/6) = \u20ac70,400\nLoss on disposal equipment: 65,000 - 41,600 - 23,000 = \u20ac400\n(Acc dep on old: 65,000 x 96%/6 x 4 years = 41,600)\nDepreciation buildings: (1,640,000 - 1,000,000) x 2% = \u20ac12,800\nLoss on fire: 120,000 - 115,000 = \u20ac5,000\nWages & salaries: \u20ac196,700\nInsurance: \u20ac25,000\nDirectors fees: 91,000 + 7,000 = \u20ac98,000 (bonus: 2% x (1,850,000 - 1,500,000) = 7,000)\n\nOperating Income:\nBad debt recovered: 9,000 + 3,000 = \u20ac12,000\nDiscount: \u20ac17,500\n\nOperating Profit: \u20ac552,216\nInvestment Income: (75,000 x 0.04)/12 x 9 = \u20ac2,250\nDebenture Interest: 18,000 + 2,100 = \u20ac20,100 (OR 7,500 + 12,600)\nNet Profit before tax: \u20ac534,366\nTaxation: (\u20ac60,000)\nNet Profit after tax: \u20ac474,366\nDividends: (\u20ac65,100)\nCapital Reserve transfer: (\u20ac250,000)\nP&L b/f: \u20ac127,000\nP&L balance 31/12/2021: \u20ac286,266\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nPatents: \u20ac48,750\nLand & Buildings: Cost \u20ac1,640,000; Dep \u20ac12,800; NBV \u20ac1,627,200\nEquipment: Cost \u20ac440,000 (460-65+45); Dep \u20ac131,800 (103+70.4-41.6); NBV \u20ac308,200\nInvestments: \u20ac75,000\nDebtors: 249,800 - 15,000 + 3,000 = \u20ac237,800\nBDP: 237,800 x 3% = \u20ac7,134\nInvestment income due: 2,250 - 1,000 = \u20ac1,250\nFire insurance due: \u20ac115,000\nBank (OD): 68,300 - 900 - 4,500 - 9,000 = \u20ac53,900 (OR 48,950 + 4,950 = 53,900)\nCreditors: 81,500 - 7,400 + 900 = \u20ac75,000\nDebenture interest due: 20,100 - 7,500 = \u20ac12,600\nDirectors fees due: \u20ac7,000\nCapital reserve: \u20ac250,000"},

    {num:3, label:"Q2: Cash Flow Statement (Puspure plc)",
     q:"Puspure plc - Balance sheets at 31/12/2021 and 31/12/2020 with abridged P&L for year ended 31/12/2021.\n\nAbridged P&L:\nOperating Profit: \u20ac227,000\nInvestment income: \u20ac3,800\nInterest: (\u20ac12,000)\nProfit before tax: \u20ac218,800\nTaxation: (\u20ac66,000)\nProfit after tax: \u20ac152,800\nDividends paid: (\u20ac30,800)\nRetained profit: \u20ac122,000\n\nBalance Sheet 2021 vs 2020:\nInvestments: 165,000 vs 185,000\nLand & buildings: cost 850,000 vs 732,000; dep 75,000 vs 60,000\nMachinery: cost 397,000 vs 438,000; dep 209,000 vs 187,600\nStock: 214,600 vs 178,600\nDebtors: 185,200 vs 171,800\nBank: 14,000 vs (8,500)\nInvestment income due: 1,050 vs 950\nGovt securities: 35,000 vs 10,000\nTrade creditors: 250,600 vs 241,600\nDebenture interest due: 2,600 vs 2,000\nCorp tax: 52,000 vs 48,000\n6% Debentures: 100,000 vs 200,000\nOrd shares: 770,000 vs 700,000\nShare premium: 12,000 vs 0\n\nAdditional info:\n(i) No disposals of buildings; new buildings acquired\n(ii) No purchases of machinery; machinery disposed of for \u20ac33,000\n(iii) Depreciation charged includes \u20ac30,400 on machinery\n\nRequired:\n(a) Cash flow statement with reconciliation statements (52 marks)\n(b)(i) What is a Financial Reporting Standard?\n(b)(ii) Distinguish between cash gain and non-cash gain with examples (8 marks)",
     a:"RECONCILIATION OF OPERATING PROFIT TO NET CASH FLOW:\nOperating Profit: \u20ac227,000\nDepreciation: \u20ac45,400\n(Machinery: 209,000 - 187,600 + 9,000 dep on disposed = 30,400; Buildings: 75,000 - 60,000 = 15,000; Total = 45,400)\nLess Profit on Sale of Machinery: (\u20ac1,000)\n(Cost disposed: 438,000 - 397,000 = 41,000; Dep on disposed: 9,000; NBV = 32,000; Sold for 33,000)\nLess Increase in Stock: (\u20ac36,000)\nLess Increase in Debtors: (\u20ac13,400)\nAdd Increase in Creditors: \u20ac9,000\nNet Cash Inflow from Operating Activities: \u20ac231,000\n\nCASH FLOW STATEMENT:\nOperating Activities: \u20ac231,000\n\nReturn on Investment & Servicing of Finance:\nInvestment Income Received: 950 + 3,800 - 1,050 = \u20ac3,700\nDebenture Interest Paid: 2,000 + 12,000 - 2,600 = (\u20ac11,400)\nNet: (\u20ac7,700)\n\nTaxation Paid: 48,000 + 66,000 - 52,000 = (\u20ac62,000)\n\nCapital Expenditure & Financial Investments:\nPayment for Buildings: 850,000 - 732,000 = (\u20ac118,000)\nReceipts from Sale of Machinery: \u20ac33,000\nProceeds from Sale of Investments: 185,000 - 165,000 = \u20ac20,000\nNet: (\u20ac65,000)\n\nEquity Dividends Paid: (\u20ac30,800)\n\nNet Cash Inflow before Liquid Resources & Financing: \u20ac65,500\n\nManagement of Liquid Resources:\nPurchase of Govt Securities: (\u20ac25,000)\n\nFinancing:\nRepayment of Debentures: (\u20ac100,000)\nReceipts from Issue of Ordinary Shares: \u20ac70,000\nShare Premium: \u20ac12,000\nNet: (\u20ac18,000)\n\nIncrease in Cash: \u20ac22,500\n\nRECONCILIATION OF NET CASH TO MOVEMENT IN NET DEBT:\nIncrease in Cash: \u20ac22,500\nCash used to Purchase Liquid Resources: \u20ac25,000\nRepayment of Debentures: \u20ac100,000\nChange in Net Debt: \u20ac147,500\nNet Debt 01/01/2021: Debentures (200,000) + Bank (8,500) + Govt Sec 10,000 = (\u20ac198,500)\nNet Debt 31/12/2021: Debentures (100,000) + Bank 14,000 + Govt Sec 35,000 = (\u20ac51,000)\n\n(b)(i) A Financial Reporting Standard is a rule applied to all financial statements to give a true and fair view of a company's financial position. It sets out best practice allowing accounts to be compared year to year and company to company.\n\n(b)(ii) Cash gain: increases profit AND cash, e.g. investment income received \u20ac3,700.\nNon-cash gain: increases profits but NOT cash, e.g. profit on sale of fixed asset \u20ac1,000."},

    {num:4, label:"Q3: Correction of Errors and Suspense Account (Fletcher Ltd)",
     q:"The trial balance of Fletcher Ltd failed to agree on 31/12/2021. The difference was entered in a suspense account.\n\nErrors discovered:\n(i) Credit purchases of \u20ac2,400 from E Hegarty entered in day books as \u20ac2,040 and posted on the INCORRECT SIDE of Hegarty's account as \u20ac4,020.\n(ii) A private debt \u20ac750 owed by Fletcher offset against a business debt \u20ac800 owed to Fletcher Ltd. No entry made.\n(iii) Payment of \u20ac1,000 received from F Murtagh (former debtor, debt previously written off as \u20ac1,250). Murtagh will pay remainder by Feb 2023. Only entry: full amount written off credited to debtors account.\n(iv) Credit note sent to K Mullan for \u20ac240 misread as credit note received from A Keogh and recorded as such.\n(v) Equipment (cost \u20ac8,400, NBV \u20ac3,360) sold for cash \u20ac3,300. Entered as \u20ac3,630 on debit of sales account and credit of a debtor's account.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Journalise the necessary corrections (40 marks)\n(b) Statement of corrected net profit (original profit \u20ac27,900) (12 marks)\n(c)(i) Purpose of preparing a trial balance\n(c)(ii) Types of errors affecting trial balance (8 marks)",
     a:"(a) JOURNAL ENTRIES:\n\n(i) Dr Purchases \u20ac360, Dr Suspense \u20ac6,060\n    Cr Creditors/Hegarty \u20ac6,420\n(Purchases understated: 2,400 - 2,040 = 360; Hegarty: should be Cr 2,400 but Dr 4,020 = swing of 6,420)\n\n(ii) Dr Drawings \u20ac750, Dr Discount Allowed \u20ac50\n     Cr Debtors \u20ac800\n(Private debt offset against business debt; shortfall = discount allowed)\n\n(iii) Dr Debtors \u20ac1,250, Cr Suspense \u20ac1,250\n(Reverse incorrect credit to debtors for full written-off amount)\n     Dr Bank \u20ac1,000, Dr Debtors \u20ac250\n     Cr Bad Debt Recovered \u20ac1,250\n(Cash received 1,000; remainder 250 still owed by Murtagh)\n\n(iv) Dr Purchases Returns \u20ac240, Cr Creditors/Keogh \u20ac240\n(Reverse incorrect purchase returns entry)\n     Dr Sales Returns \u20ac240, Cr Debtors/Mullan \u20ac240\n(Record correct sales returns)\n\n(v) Cr Sales \u20ac3,630, Dr Debtors \u20ac3,630\n(Reverse incorrect entries)\n    Cr Equipment \u20ac8,400\n    Dr Provision for Depreciation \u20ac5,040\n    Dr Cash \u20ac3,300\n    Dr Loss on Disposal \u20ac60\n(Correct disposal: NBV 8,400 - 5,040 = 3,360; sold for 3,300; loss = 60)\n\n(b) CORRECTED NET PROFIT:\nOriginal Net Profit: \u20ac27,900\nAdd: Bad Debt Recovered (iii) +1,250; Sales reversed (v) +3,630 => +\u20ac4,880\nLess: Purchases (i) -360; Purchases Returns (iv) -240; Discount Allowed (ii) -50; Sales Returns (iv) -240; Loss on Disposal (v) -60 => -\u20ac950\nCorrected Net Profit: \u20ac31,830\n\n(c)(i) A trial balance tests the accuracy of double entry bookkeeping before preparing the Final Accounts and Balance Sheet. Debits should equal credits because every debit entry should have a corresponding credit entry.\n\n(c)(ii) Errors affecting the trial balance:\n1. Entering different figures on opposite sides of two ledger accounts\n2. Entering figures on the SAME side of two ledger accounts\n3. Entering a figure on one side but nothing on the other side of another account\n4. Mathematical errors in totalling or closing off accounts"},

    {num:5, label:"Q4: Revaluation of Fixed Assets (Ryan Ltd)",
     q:"On 1 January 2007, Ryan Ltd purchased property for \u20ac900,000 (land \u20ac150,000, buildings \u20ac750,000). Depreciation at 2% p.a. straight-line. Full year in year of acquisition, none in year of disposal.\n\nJan 1 2017: Revalued at \u20ac1,080,000 (land \u20ac210,000)\nJan 1 2018: Purchased building \u20ac250,000; plus \u20ac90,000 and \u20ac30,000 for building work during 2018\nJan 1 2019: Sold adjacent land for \u20ac235,000\nJan 1 2020: Revalued remaining property at \u20ac1,426,000 (land \u20ac460,000)\nJan 1 2021: Sold building (acquired 2018) for \u20ac980,000\n\nRequired:\n(a) Land and Building Account for years 2017-2021\n(b) Provision for Depreciation Account for years 2017-2021\n(c) Disposal Accounts\n(52 marks plus theory 8 marks)",
     a:"LAND AND BUILDING ACCOUNT:\n2017: Bal b/d 900,000 + Reval Reserve 180,000 = 1,080,000\n(Revaluation uplift: 1,080,000 - 900,000 = 180,000)\n\n2018: Bal b/d 1,080,000 + Bank 250,000 + Bank 90,000 + Bank 30,000 = Bal c/d 1,450,000\n\n2019: Bal b/d 1,450,000 => Disposal 210,000; Bal c/d 1,240,000\n\n2020: Bal b/d 1,240,000 + Reval Reserve 186,000 = Bal c/d 1,426,000\n\n2021: Bal b/d 1,426,000 + Reval Reserve 49,500 = 1,475,500\n=> Disposal 1,000,500; Bal c/d 475,000\n\nPROVISION FOR DEPRECIATION ACCOUNT:\n01/01/2017: Bal b/d 150,000 (750,000 x 2% x 10 yrs); Reval Reserve writes off 150,000\n31/12/2017: P&L 17,400 (870,000 x 2%); Bal c/d 17,400\n\n2018: P&L 24,800 (870,000 x 2% + 370,000 x 2%); Bal c/d 42,200\n\n2019: P&L 24,800; Bal c/d 67,000\n\n01/01/2020: Reval Reserve writes off 67,000\nP&L 28,520 (966,000 x 2%); Bal c/d 28,520\n\n2021: Disposal 20,010; Reval Reserve 8,510; P&L 9,500; Bal c/d 9,500\n\nDISPOSAL OF LAND (2019):\nDr: L&B 210,000 | Cr: Bank 235,000, P&L Profit 25,000\n\nDISPOSAL OF BUILDING (2021):\nDr: L&B 1,000,500 | Cr: Depreciation 20,010, Bank 980,000, P&L Loss 490\n(NBV = 1,000,500 - 20,010 = 980,490; Sold 980,000; Loss = 490)"}
  ],
  "2023": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1A: Final Accounts - Sole Trader (V. Leahy)",
     q:"Trial balance of V. Leahy on 31/12/2022:\nPatents (incorporating 2 months investment income): \u20ac48,800\nLand and buildings: \u20ac850,000; Acc dep L&B: \u20ac105,000\nDelivery vans: \u20ac480,000; Acc dep vans: \u20ac70,000\nEquipment at cost: \u20ac153,500\nDiscount (net): \u20ac7,700\n4% Investments acquired 01/07/2022: \u20ac180,000\nStock 01/01/2022: \u20ac70,700\nSales: \u20ac1,848,900, Purchases: \u20ac1,193,500\nSalaries and general expenses (inc suspense): \u20ac136,400\nAdvertising: \u20ac16,000\nInvestment income received: \u20ac2,200\nDrawings: \u20ac45,000, Rates: \u20ac42,800\nPAYE/PRSI/USC: \u20ac4,700, VAT: \u20ac11,900\nBank: \u20ac53,600 (overdraft)\n3% Fixed mortgage (incl \u20ac50,000 issued 01/06/2022): \u20ac320,000\nMortgage interest paid (first 4 months): \u20ac2,300\nDebtors: \u20ac67,700, Creditors: \u20ac98,600\nBad debts provision: \u20ac2,100, Capital: \u20ac762,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock \u20ac82,600; damaged stock cost \u20ac5,800, NRV 75% of cost\n(ii) Goods in transit: invoice \u20ac15,375 inc VAT 23%. Not recorded.\n(iii) Van depreciation 20% of cost (purchase to sale). Van cost \u20ac35,000 on 30/06/2018 traded in 30/09/2022. New van \u20ac80,000, allowance \u20ac3,000. Net in bank but treated as purchase.\n(iv) Suspense: incorrect mortgage interest (correct in bank) and discount received \u20ac600 entered only in discount a/c.\n(v) Patents written off over 10 years from 2020\n(vi) Goods taken by Leahy: retail \u20ac3,600 (cost + 20%)\n(vii) L&B revalued \u20ac950,000 on 01/01/2022 (land \u20ac200,000). Buildings dep 2% of cost p.a.\n(viii) Bank: credit transfer \u20ac1,500 for debt of \u20ac1,700 previously written off. Unpresented cheque \u20ac1,800.\n(ix) Provide for: investment income due, mortgage interest due (20% private), BDP 6% of debtors, equipment dep 10% of cost.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading and P&L account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"TRADING P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: \u20ac1,848,900\nOpening stock: \u20ac70,700\nPurchases: 1,193,500 + 12,500 - 77,000 - 3,000 = \u20ac1,126,000\n(Goods in transit: 15,375/1.23 = 12,500; van as purchase: 80,000 - 3,000 = 77,000; drawings at cost: 3,600/1.20 = 3,000)\nClosing stock: 82,600 - 1,450 + 12,500 = \u20ac93,650\n(Damaged: 5,800 x 25% = 1,450 write-down; add goods in transit)\nGross Profit: \u20ac745,850\n\nDistribution Costs:\nLoss on van: \u20ac2,250 (cost 35,000; dep 35,000 x 20% x 51/12 = 29,750; NBV 5,250; allowance 3,000)\nDepreciation vans: \u20ac98,250 (5,250 + 89,000 + 4,000 OR 72,000 + 26,250)\nIncrease in BDP: 4,074 - 2,100 = \u20ac1,974\nAdvertising: \u20ac16,000\n\nAdmin Expenses:\nDepreciation buildings: (950,000 - 200,000) x 2% = \u20ac15,000\nSalaries & general: 136,400 - 600 - 400 = \u20ac135,400\nPatent written off: (48,800 + 1,200)/8 = \u20ac6,250\nDepreciation equipment: 153,500 x 10% = \u20ac15,350\nRates: \u20ac42,800\n\nOperating Income:\nBad debt recovered: \u20ac1,700\nDiscount: \u20ac7,700\n\nOperating Profit: \u20ac421,976\nInvestment Income: 180,000 x 4% x 6/12 = \u20ac3,600\nMortgage Interest: \u20ac7,180 (3,375 + 5,600 - 1,795 OR 8,100 + 875 - 1,795)\nNet Profit: \u20ac418,396\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nPatent: 50,000 - 6,250 = \u20ac43,750\nL&B: \u20ac950,000; Dep \u20ac15,000; NBV \u20ac935,000\nVans: Cost \u20ac525,000 (480+80-35); Dep \u20ac138,500 (70+98,250-29,750); NBV \u20ac386,500\nEquipment: \u20ac153,500; Dep \u20ac15,350; NBV \u20ac138,150\nInvestments: \u20ac180,000\nDebtors: 67,700 + 200 = \u20ac67,900; BDP: 67,900 x 6% = \u20ac4,074\nInvestment income due: 3,600 - 1,200 - 2,200 = \u20ac200\nCreditors: 98,600 + 15,375 - 600 = \u20ac113,375\nVAT: 11,900 - 2,875 = \u20ac9,025\nBank (OD): 53,600 - 1,500 = \u20ac52,100 (OR 50,300 + 1,800)\nMortgage interest due: 8,975 - 2,300 - 400 = \u20ac6,275\nRevaluation reserve: 100,000 + 105,000 = \u20ac205,000\nDrawings: 45,000 + 1,795 + 3,000 = \u20ac49,795"},

    {num:2, label:"Q1B: Final Accounts - Company (East Elm Ltd)",
     q:"East Elm Ltd, authorised capital \u20ac1,400,000 (900,000 ordinary + 500,000 4% preference at \u20ac1 each).\n\nTrial balance at 31/12/2022:\nL&B at cost: \u20ac950,000; Acc dep L&B: \u20ac120,000\nOffice equipment (cost \u20ac65,000): \u20ac32,000\nDelivery vans (cost \u20ac175,000): \u20ac135,000\nStock 01/01/2022: \u20ac55,400\nPatent (inc 5 months investment income): \u20ac53,300\nP&L balance 01/01/2022: \u20ac59,000 (debit balance)\nDiscount (net): \u20ac5,100\nPurchases: \u20ac758,000, Sales: \u20ac1,150,000\nDirectors fees: \u20ac23,300\nDebtors: \u20ac92,300, Creditors: \u20ac65,200, Bank: \u20ac33,400 (OD)\nBDP: \u20ac6,200\n3% Investments 01/02/2022: \u20ac124,000\nSalaries & general expenses (inc suspense): \u20ac232,400\nRent received: \u20ac18,000\nDebenture interest paid (first 4 months): \u20ac6,400\n6% Debentures (incl \u20ac60,000 issued 01/06/2022): \u20ac280,000\nVAT: \u20ac2,300, Dividends paid: \u20ac45,000\nPAYE/PRSI/USC: \u20ac48,200\nCapital reserve: \u20ac39,000\nOrdinary share capital: \u20ac730,000, 4% Pref: \u20ac80,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock \u20ac95,200; damaged stock cost \u20ac7,200, NRV 25% of cost\n(ii) Patents (inc 5 months inv income) written off over 6 years from 2021\n(iii) Goods in transit \u20ac19,680 inc VAT 23%. Not recorded.\n(iv) Goods returned to supplier (retail \u20ac12,000 = cost + 25%). 10% restocking charge on cost not entered.\n(v) Van dep 15% of cost (purchase to sale). Van cost \u20ac36,000 on 30/06/2019 traded in 30/04/2022. New \u20ac45,000, allowance \u20ac18,000. Net in bank but treated as purchase.\n(vi) Suspense: incorrect debenture interest and PAYE \u20ac4,500 on wrong side.\n(vii) L&B revalued \u20ac1,100,000 on 01/01/2022 (land \u20ac300,000). Buildings dep 2% p.a.\n(viii) Bank: credit transfer \u20ac900 (30c in \u20ac1 bankrupt debtor). Unpresented cheque \u20ac1,300.\n(ix) Investment income due, debenture interest due, equipment dep 10% reducing balance, BDP 6%, tax \u20ac35,000.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading and P&L account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"TRADING P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: \u20ac1,150,000\nPurchases: 758,000 + 16,000 + 960 - 27,000 = \u20ac747,960\n(Goods in transit: 19,680/1.23 = 16,000; restocking: 12,000/1.25 x 10% = 960; van: 45,000 - 18,000 = 27,000)\nClosing stock: 95,200 - 5,400 + 16,000 = \u20ac105,800\n(Damaged: 7,200 x 75% = 5,400 write-down)\nGross Profit: \u20ac452,440\n\nDistribution Costs:\nBad debts: 900/0.30 = 3,000 - 900 = \u20ac2,100\nLoss on van: 36,000 - 18,000 - 15,300 = \u20ac2,700 (dep: 36,000 x 15% x 34/12 = 15,300)\nAdvertising: \u20ac6,700\nDepreciation vans: \u20ac27,150 (1,800 + 20,850 + 4,500)\n\nAdmin Expenses:\nPatents written off: (53,300 + 1,550)/5 = \u20ac10,970\nDepreciation equipment: 32,000 x 10% = \u20ac3,200 (reducing balance)\nSalaries & general: 232,400 + 2,000 - 9,000 = \u20ac225,400\nDepreciation buildings: (1,100,000 - 300,000) x 2% = \u20ac16,000\nDirectors fees: \u20ac23,300\n\nOperating Income:\nDiscount: \u20ac5,100; Rent received: \u20ac18,000\nDecrease in BDP: 6,200 - 5,358 = \u20ac842\n\nOperating Profit: \u20ac158,862\nInvestment Income: (124,000 x 0.03)/12 x 11 = \u20ac3,410\nDebenture Interest: 5,500 + 9,800 = \u20ac15,300 (OR 13,200 + 2,100)\nNet Profit before tax: \u20ac146,972\nTax: (\u20ac35,000); Dividends: (\u20ac45,000)\nP&L b/f: (\u20ac59,000)\nP&L balance 31/12/2022: \u20ac7,972\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nPatents: 54,850 - 10,970 = \u20ac43,880\nL&B: \u20ac1,100,000; Dep \u20ac16,000; NBV \u20ac1,084,000\nVans: Cost \u20ac184,000 (175+45-36); Dep \u20ac51,850 (40+27,150-15,300); NBV \u20ac132,150\nEquipment: Cost \u20ac65,000; Dep \u20ac36,200; NBV \u20ac28,800\nDebtors: 92,300 - 3,000 = \u20ac89,300; BDP: 89,300 x 6% = \u20ac5,358\nInvestment income due: 3,410 - 1,550 = \u20ac1,860\nVAT: 2,300 + 3,680 = \u20ac5,980\nCreditors: 65,200 + 19,680 + 960 = \u20ac85,840\nBank (OD): 33,400 - 900 = \u20ac32,500 (OR 31,200 + 1,300)\nDebenture interest due: \u20ac10,900\nPAYE: 48,200 - 9,000 = \u20ac39,200\nRevaluation reserve: 150,000 + 120,000 = \u20ac270,000"},

    {num:3, label:"Q2: Incomplete Records (P. Shepard)",
     q:"P. Shepard purchased a business on 01/01/2022 with: Buildings \u20ac300,000; stock \u20ac63,000; debtors \u20ac73,200; equipment \u20ac70,000; wages due \u20ac1,700; creditors \u20ac57,700; rates prepaid \u20ac450; delivery van \u20ac35,000; cash \u20ac300.\n\nCash Payments: Lodgements \u20ac196,000; general expenses \u20ac12,500; purchases \u20ac84,000\nBank Payments: Interest \u20ac3,300; creditors \u20ac37,000; rates \u20ac6,000; general expenses \u20ac81,000 (includes diesel \u20ac4,100, 20% private); equipment (01/05/2022) \u20ac24,000; light & heat \u20ac20,700\nBank Lodgements: Debtors \u20ac46,000; cash \u20ac196,000\n\nWeekly drawings: goods \u20ac120, cash \u20ac180\nOn 01/08/2022: borrowed \u20ac150,000 for adjoining building. Interest 7% p.a. Investment fund set up (earned \u20ac150 interest).\n15% of light & heat and 20% of interest = private.\nDepreciation: equipment 10%, buildings 2%, vans 20% of cost p.a.\nBDP: 4% of debtors.\nClosing: stock \u20ac40,700 (inc heating oil \u20ac500); electricity due \u20ac700; debtors \u20ac43,000; creditors \u20ac55,400; cash \u20ac430; wages due \u20ac1,400.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading and P&L account (52 marks)\n(b)(i) What additional info from double entry?\n(b)(ii) Advice on record keeping (8 marks)",
     a:"SALES CALCULATION:\nCredit sales: 46,000 + 43,000 - 73,200 = \u20ac15,800\nCash sales: 196,000 + 12,500 + 84,000 + 9,360 + 430 - 300 = \u20ac301,990\nTotal Sales: \u20ac317,790\n\nPURCHASES:\nCredit: 37,000 + 55,400 - 57,700 = \u20ac34,700\nCash: \u20ac84,000\nLess stock drawings: 120 x 52 = (\u20ac6,240)\nTotal Purchases: \u20ac112,460\n\nTRADING P&L:\nSales: \u20ac317,790\nOpening stock: \u20ac63,000\nPurchases: \u20ac112,460\nClosing stock: 40,700 - 500 = (\u20ac40,200) (exclude heating oil)\nGross Profit: \u20ac182,530\n\nExpenses:\nGeneral expenses: 12,500 + 81,000 + 1,400 - 1,700 - 820 = \u20ac92,380 (diesel private: 4,100 x 20% = 820)\nLight & heat: 20,700 - 500 + 700 - 3,135 = \u20ac17,765\nRates: 6,000 + 450 - 500 = \u20ac5,950\nBDP: 43,000 x 4% = \u20ac1,720\nDep equipment: 7,000 + 1,600 = \u20ac8,600 (70,000 x 10% + 24,000 x 10% x 8/12)\nDep premises: 6,000 + 1,250 = \u20ac7,250 (300,000 x 2% + 150,000 x 2% x 5/12)\nDep vehicles: 35,000 x 20% = \u20ac7,000\nTotal: \u20ac140,665\n\nOperating Income: Interest on fund \u20ac150\nInterest: 150,000 x 7% x 5/12 = 4,375; private 20% = 875; business = \u20ac3,500\nNet Profit: \u20ac38,515\n\n(b)(i) Double entry provides: General/Nominal Ledger, Trial Balance, Total Sales and Purchases figures, Bank balance, Capital/Drawings, detailed Expenses and Incomes.\n\n(b)(ii) Shepard should keep a detailed cash book and general ledger with subsidiary day books. This enables accurate final accounts rather than relying on rough estimates."},

    {num:4, label:"Q3: Creditors Control Account (T. Gallagher)",
     q:"Creditors ledger control account of T. Gallagher: \u20ac48,760 Cr and \u20ac280 Dr on 31/12/2022. Does not agree with schedule of creditors.\n\nErrors:\n(i) Purchases \u20ac9,570 entered on debit side of supplier's account. Includes cash purchases \u20ac2,300.\n(ii) Invoice \u20ac730 entered in day book as \u20ac370. No entry in supplier's account.\n(iii) Discount disallowed \u20ac94 recorded as discount received \u20ac90 in general ledger, debited as \u20ac9 in supplier's account.\n(iv) Returns \u20ac650 correctly entered. Credit note with 10% restocking charge. Total credit note debited to creditor. No other entry.\n(v) Credit note \u20ac580 received. Only entry: \u20ac58 credited to supplier's account.\n(vi) Contra offset: customer/supplier owes \u20ac420 to Gallagher. Offset entered as \u20ac240 on correct side. Only entry made.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Adjusted creditors control account (24 marks)\n(b) Schedule of creditors balances (28 marks)\n(c)(i) Why prepare creditors control accounts?\n(c)(ii) Explain restocking charge (8 marks)",
     a:"(a) ADJUSTED CREDITORS CONTROL ACCOUNT:\nDr: Balance b/d 280; Credit Note (v) 580; Contra (vi) 420; Balance c/d 48,369\nCr: Balance b/d 48,760; Purchases (ii) 360; Discount disallowed (iii) 184; Restocking (iv) 65; Balance c/d 280\nTotals: \u20ac49,649\nNet adjusted balance: \u20ac48,089\n\n(b) SCHEDULE OF CREDITORS:\nBalance per list: \u20ac30,584\nAdd: Purchases (i) \u20ac16,840 (debit side corrected); Purchases (ii) \u20ac730; Discount disallowed (iii) \u20ac103; Restocking (iv) \u20ac650\nTotal additions: \u20ac18,323 => Subtotal: \u20ac48,907\nDeduct: Credit note (v) \u20ac638 (580 + 58); Contra (vi) \u20ac180 (420 - 240)\nTotal deductions: (\u20ac818)\nNet balance: \u20ac48,089\n\n(c)(i) Control accounts: check accuracy of ledgers; locate errors quickly; useful for incomplete records; allow quick ascertainment of amounts owed.\n\n(c)(ii) A restocking charge is a fee charged when stock is returned to a supplier, covering costs of repackaging goods or as company policy."},

    {num:5, label:"Q4: Tabular Statement (Company Transactions)",
     q:"Tabular statement showing assets and liabilities on 01/01/2022 with various transactions:\nL&B: \u20ac820,000 (acc dep \u20ac75,000); Vehicles: \u20ac86,000 (acc dep \u20ac30,500)\nStock: \u20ac38,000; Debtors: \u20ac68,500; BDP: \u20ac2,055\nShare Capital: \u20ac750,000; Share Premium: \u20ac10,000\nCreditors: \u20ac53,700; Bank: \u20ac18,650; VAT: \u20ac5,230\nP&L balance: \u20ac67,365\n\nTransactions through the year: revaluation, business acquisition (goodwill, stock, vehicles), building purchase, new debtors, BDP adjustment, rent, rates, depreciation.\n\nRequired: Complete the tabular statement showing the effect of each transaction (60 marks)",
     a:"TABULAR STATEMENT - KEY FIGURES:\n\nJanuary - Revaluation:\nL&B: +130,000; Acc dep L&B written off: +75,000\nRevaluation reserve: +205,000\n\nFebruary - Acquisition:\nGoodwill: +22,400; Stock: +1,250; Vehicles: +16,500\nVehicle acc dep: +10,200; Creditors: +22,000\nShare capital: +120,000; Share premium: +24,000; Bank: -3,600\n\nBuilding Purchase: L&B: +126,000\n\nYear-end Adjustments:\nDepreciation L&B: (18,310)\nDepreciation Vehicles: (18,550)\nBDP adjustment: (1,389)\nRates prepaid: 2,500\n\nCLOSING BALANCES:\nL&B: \u20ac1,076,000; Acc dep: (\u20ac18,310)\nVehicles: \u20ac102,500; Acc dep: (\u20ac38,850)\nStock: \u20ac39,250; Debtors: \u20ac84,450; BDP: (\u20ac3,444)\nGoodwill: \u20ac22,400; Rates prepaid: \u20ac2,500\nShare Capital: \u20ac870,000; Share Premium: \u20ac34,000\nCreditors: \u20ac75,700; Bank: \u20ac51,050; VAT: \u20ac4,885\nRent prepaid: \u20ac800; Revaluation reserve: \u20ac205,000\nP&L balance: \u20ac25,061\nTotal: \u20ac1,266,496"},

    {num:6, label:"Q5: Interpretation of Accounts (O'Malley PLC)",
     q:"O'Malley PLC financial data:\nCost of Sales: \u20ac560,000; Stock turnover: 14 times\nNet Profit: \u20ac104,000; Interest: \u20ac32,000\nCapital Employed: \u20ac1,028,000\nOrdinary shares: 450,000; 4% Preference shares: \u20ac100,000\nOrdinary dividend: \u20ac36,000\nMarket price: 130 cent; Closing stock: \u20ac42,000\n\nRequired:\n(a) Calculate: (i) Opening Stock (ii) ROCE (iii) P/E Ratio (iv) Dividend Cover (v) Dividend Yield (50 marks)\n(b) Comment on profitability, dividend policy, liquidity (50 marks)",
     a:"(a) CALCULATIONS:\n\n(i) Opening Stock:\nAverage Stock = 560,000 / 14 = \u20ac40,000\nOpening Stock = (40,000 x 2) - 42,000 = \u20ac38,000\n\n(ii) ROCE = (104,000 + 32,000) / 1,028,000 x 100 = 13.23%\n\n(iii) EPS = (104,000 - 4,000) / 450,000 = 22.22 cent\nP/E Ratio = 130 / 22.22 = 5.85 times\n\n(iv) Dividend Cover = (104,000 - 4,000) / 36,000 = 2.78 times\nDPS = 36,000 / 450,000 = 8 cent\n\n(v) Dividend Yield = 8 / 130 x 100 = 6.15%\n\n(b) INTERPRETATION:\nProfitability: ROCE 13.23% (down from 14.28%). Exceeds risk-free rate (0-2%) and debenture rate (8%). EPS fell from 23c to 22.22c. Company is profitable but less efficient than last year.\n\nDividend Policy: Dividend Cover improved from 2.57 to 2.78 times. Payout ratio fell from 38.9% to 35.97%. More profit retained for expansion. DPS decreased from 8.95c to 8c.\n\nLiquidity: Acid test ratio 1.2:1 (down from 1.5:1). Still above ideal 1:1. Company has \u20ac1.20 in liquid assets per \u20ac1 of short-term debt."}
  ],
  "2024": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1A: Final Accounts - Company (Yeats Ltd)",
     q:"Yeats Ltd, authorised capital \u20ac1,600,000 (1,000,000 ordinary + 600,000 4% preference at \u20ac1 each).\n\nTrial balance at 31/12/2023:\nL&B at cost: \u20ac580,000; Acc dep buildings: \u20ac38,000\nDelivery vans (cost \u20ac115,000): \u20ac80,000\nDiscount (net): \u20ac3,500\nP&L balance 01/01/2023: \u20ac34,800\nStock 01/01/2023: \u20ac44,400\nDebenture interest (first 9 months): \u20ac5,400\n3% Investments acquired 01/05/2023: \u20ac120,000\nPatents (inc 3 months investment income): \u20ac40,600\nPurchases: \u20ac747,000, Sales: \u20ac1,080,700\nDividends paid: \u20ac15,000; BDP: \u20ac3,200\nDebtors: \u20ac69,600, Creditors: \u20ac64,900, Bank: \u20ac57,000 (OD)\nSalaries & general expenses (inc suspense): \u20ac218,355\n8% Debentures (incl \u20ac40,000 issued 01/10/2023): \u20ac140,000\nOrd shares: \u20ac400,000, 4% Pref: \u20ac100,000\nVAT: \u20ac1,900, Advertising: \u20ac25,145\nRent received: \u20ac13,500, Capital reserve: \u20ac15,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock \u20ac56,900; damaged stock cost \u20ac3,800, NRV 60% of cost\n(ii) Vans: straight-line over 5 years, 5% scrap value. Van cost \u20ac40,000 in 2019 traded for \u20ac10,000 against new \u20ac56,000. Net treated as purchase.\n(iii) Sale-or-return goods entered as credit purchase (selling price \u20ac5,000 = cost + 25%)\n(iv) Patents (inc 3 months inv income) written off over 7 years from 2021\n(v) Suspense: incorrect debenture interest (correct in bank) and discount allowed \u20ac400 entered only in discount a/c\n(vi) New warehouse \u20ac100,000 + VAT 13.5%. Total in L&B, no VAT entry.\n(vii) Buildings dep 2% of cost (land \u20ac200,000). L&B revalued \u20ac700,000 on 31/12/2023.\n(viii) Rent: warehouse \u20ac1,500/month from 01/06/2023\n(ix) Investment income due, debenture interest due, BDP 4% of debtors, transfer \u20ac20,000 to capital reserve\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading and P&L account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"TRADING P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: \u20ac1,080,700\nPurchases: 747,000 - 46,000 - 4,000 = \u20ac697,000\n(Van: 56,000 - 10,000 = 46,000; sale-or-return cost: 5,000/1.25 = 4,000)\nClosing stock: 56,900 - 1,520 - 4,000 = \u20ac51,380\n(Damaged: 3,800 x 40% = 1,520; remove sale-or-return)\nGross Profit: \u20ac390,680\n\nDistribution Costs:\nDiscount: \u20ac3,500\nDepreciation vans: 131,000 x 0.95 / 5 = \u20ac24,890\nAdvertising: \u20ac25,145\n\nAdmin Expenses:\nPatents: (40,600 + 900)/5 = \u20ac8,300\nDepreciation buildings: (580,000 - 13,500 - 200,000) x 2% = \u20ac7,330\nSalaries & general: 218,355 + 400 - 600 = \u20ac218,155\n\nOperating Income:\nRent received: 13,500 - 3,000 = \u20ac10,500 (1,500 x 7 months; 3,000 prepaid)\nDecrease in BDP: 3,200 - 2,768 = \u20ac432\nProfit on van disposal: 40,000 - 30,400 - 10,000 = \u20ac400 credit\n(Dep: 40,000 x 0.95/5 x 4 = 30,400; NBV = 9,600; allowance 10,000; profit 400)\n\nOperating Profit: \u20ac114,692\nInvestment Income: 120,000 x 3% x 8/12 = \u20ac2,400\nDebenture Interest: 8,000 + 800 = \u20ac8,800\n(100,000 x 8% = 8,000; 40,000 x 8% x 3/12 = 800)\nNet Profit: \u20ac108,292\nDividends: (\u20ac15,000); Capital Reserve: (\u20ac20,000)\nP&L b/f: \u20ac34,800\nP&L balance: \u20ac108,092\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nPatents: 41,500 - 8,300 = \u20ac33,200\nL&B: \u20ac700,000 (revalued)\nVans: Cost \u20ac131,000 (115+56-40); Dep \u20ac29,490 (35+24,890-30,400); NBV \u20ac101,510\nInvestments: \u20ac120,000\nDebtors: 69,600 - 400 = \u20ac69,200; BDP: 69,200 x 4% = \u20ac2,768\nInvestment income due: 2,400 - 900 = \u20ac1,500\nVAT: (1,900) + 13,500 = \u20ac11,600 (asset)\nCreditors: 64,900 - 4,000 = \u20ac60,900\nBank (OD): \u20ac57,000\nDebenture interest due: 8,800 - 6,000 = \u20ac2,800\nRent prepaid: \u20ac3,000\nDebentures: \u20ac140,000\nRevaluation Reserve: 133,500 + 38,000 + 7,330 = \u20ac178,830\nCapital reserve: 15,000 + 20,000 = \u20ac35,000"},

    {num:2, label:"Q1B: Final Accounts - Manufacturing Company (Sexton Ltd)",
     q:"Sexton Ltd, authorised capital \u20ac1,500,000 (1,100,000 ordinary + 400,000 4% preference at \u20ac1 each).\n\nTrial balance at 31/12/2023:\nFactory L&B (cost \u20ac900,000): \u20ac812,000\nPlant & machinery (cost \u20ac780,000): \u20ac680,000\nPatents: \u20ac80,000\nGeneral factory overheads (inc suspense): \u20ac110,700\nSale of scrap: \u20ac11,200\nStocks 01/01/2023: RM \u20ac97,500, WIP \u20ac13,000, FG \u20ac76,000\nPurchases RM: \u20ac580,000; Sales: \u20ac1,692,100\nCarriage on RM: \u20ac15,800\nSelling expenses: \u20ac99,000\nDirect factory wages: \u20ac310,000; Admin expenses: \u20ac113,000\n8% Debentures: \u20ac200,000\nOrd shares: \u20ac900,000, 4% Pref: \u20ac330,000\nP&L b/f: \u20ac6,300; VAT: \u20ac10,100; Bank: \u20ac8,000 (OD)\n3% Investments 01/08/2023: \u20ac150,000\nInv income received: \u20ac1,500; Dividends paid: \u20ac47,000\nDebenture interest: \u20ac12,200\nDebtors: \u20ac45,000; Creditors: \u20ac52,000; Capital reserve: \u20ac30,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stocks: RM \u20ac33,400, WIP \u20ac22,100, FG \u20ac72,000\n(ii) RM \u20ac5,800 destroyed by fire; insurance 90%. No entry.\n(iii) FG sent on sale-or-return, recorded as credit sale \u20ac21,250 (25% mark-up)\n(iv) Suspense: discount allowed \u20ac800 (debtors only); credit purchase RM \u20ac3,000 on wrong side of creditor\n(v) Patents over 7 years from 2021\n(vi) \u20ac7,000 in scrap is from machine sale 31/03/2023 (cost \u20ac40,000 on 30/09/2019). P&M dep 15% of cost.\n(vii) Buildings dep 2% of cost (land \u20ac100,000). 80% factory, 20% admin. Revalue at \u20ac1,200,000 on 31/12/2023.\n(viii) Bank: statement shows \u20ac600 OD. Rent \u20ac6,000 (4 months) paid into bank for office let from 01/11/2023. Unpresented cheque \u20ac3,200. Dishonoured cheque \u20ac1,800.\n(ix) Investment income due, debenture interest due, BDP 6% of debtors, transfer \u20ac20,000 to capital reserve.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Manufacturing, trading and P&L account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"MANUFACTURING ACCOUNT:\nOpening stock RM: \u20ac97,500\nPurchases RM: 580,000 - 5,800 = \u20ac574,200 (remove fire-damaged stock)\nCarriage In: \u20ac15,800\nClosing stock RM: (\u20ac33,400)\nCost of RM consumed: \u20ac654,100\nFactory Wages: \u20ac310,000\nPrime Cost: \u20ac964,100\n\nFactory Overheads:\nPatent: 80,000/5 = \u20ac16,000\nGeneral factory overhead: 110,700 + 6,000 - 800 = \u20ac115,900\n(Creditor suspense: 3,000 x 2 = 6,000; less discount 800)\nDepreciation P&M: 111,000 + 1,500 = \u20ac112,500\n(OR 29,250 + 29,250 per half-year calculation)\nLoss on machine: 40,000 - 7,000 - 21,000 = \u20ac12,000\n(Dep: 40,000 x 15% x 3.5 yrs = 21,000; NBV 19,000; sold 7,000)\nLoss on damaged stock: 5,800 - 5,220 = \u20ac580\nDep factory building: 2% x 800,000 x 80% = \u20ac12,800\nTotal Overheads: \u20ac269,780\n\nAdd Opening WIP: \u20ac13,000; Less Closing WIP: (\u20ac22,100)\nLess Sale of Scrap: 11,200 - 7,000 = (\u20ac4,200)\nCost of Manufacture: \u20ac1,220,580\n\nTRADING P&L:\nSales: 1,692,100 - 21,250 = \u20ac1,670,850\nOpening FG: \u20ac76,000; CoM: \u20ac1,220,580\nClosing FG: 72,000 + 17,000 = (\u20ac89,000) (sale-or-return cost: 21,250/1.25 = 17,000)\nGross Profit: \u20ac463,270\n\nExpenses:\nDiscount: \u20ac800; Selling: \u20ac99,000\nBDP: 25,550 x 6% = \u20ac1,533\nDep buildings (admin): 2% x 800,000 x 20% = \u20ac3,200\nAdmin: \u20ac113,000\nTotal: (\u20ac217,533)\n\nRent: 6,000 x 2/4 = \u20ac3,000 (2 months earned of 4 prepaid)\nOperating Profit: \u20ac248,737\nInvestment Income: 150,000 x 3% x 5/12 = \u20ac1,875\nDebenture Interest: 200,000 x 8% = \u20ac16,000\nNet Profit: \u20ac234,612\nDividends: (\u20ac47,000); Reserve transfer: (\u20ac20,000)\nP&L b/f: \u20ac6,300\nP&L balance: \u20ac173,912\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nPatent: 80,000 - 16,000 = \u20ac64,000\nFactory L&B: \u20ac1,300,000 (revalued 1,200,000 + land 100,000)\nP&M: Cost \u20ac740,000 (780-40); Dep \u20ac191,500 (100+112.5-21); NBV \u20ac548,500\nStocks: \u20ac144,500 (RM 33,400 + WIP 22,100 + FG 89,000)\nDebtors: 45,000 - 21,250 + 1,800 = \u20ac25,550; BDP: \u20ac1,533\nCompensation due: \u20ac5,220; Inv income due: 1,875 - 1,500 = \u20ac375\nBank (OD): 8,000 + 1,800 - 6,000 = \u20ac3,800 (OR 600 + 3,200)\nCreditors: 52,000 + 6,000 = \u20ac58,000; Rent prepaid: \u20ac3,000\nDebenture interest due: 16,000 - 12,200 = \u20ac3,800\nReval Reserve: 400,000 + 88,000 + 16,000 = \u20ac504,000\nCapital reserve: 30,000 + 20,000 = \u20ac50,000"},

    {num:3, label:"Q2: Published Accounts (Lyne plc)",
     q:"Lyne plc, authorised share capital \u20ac850,000 (650,000 ordinary + 200,000 4% preference at \u20ac1 each).\n\nTrial balance at 31/12/2023:\nL&B at cost: \u20ac850,000; Buildings acc dep: \u20ac55,000\nVehicles at cost: \u20ac440,000; Vehicles acc dep: \u20ac75,000\nOrd shares: \u20ac500,000, 4% Pref: \u20ac150,000\nPatent 01/01/2023: \u20ac37,500; 3% Investments: \u20ac140,000\nDebtors: \u20ac105,000; Creditors: \u20ac44,000\nPurchases: \u20ac1,500,000; Sales: \u20ac2,150,000\nStock 01/01: \u20ac57,000; Distribution costs: \u20ac232,000; Admin: \u20ac146,000\nRental income: \u20ac85,000; Profit on sale of land: \u20ac60,000\nDirectors fees: \u20ac35,000; P&L b/f: \u20ac43,000; BDP: \u20ac5,600\nDebenture interest paid: \u20ac12,000; Bank: \u20ac38,000 (OD)\nCommission: \u20ac14,000; VAT: \u20ac5,600; Dividends paid: \u20ac35,000\nAdvertising: \u20ac4,500; 8% Debentures 2027/2028: \u20ac380,000\n\nAdditional info:\n(i) Closing stock \u20ac85,000\n(ii) Patent (cost \u20ac75,000 on 01/01/2018) amortised over 10 years; in cost of sales\n(iii) Land sold (cost \u20ac210,000) for \u20ac270,000. Buildings dep 2% of cost, 25% distribution/75% admin. L&B revalued \u20ac950,000.\n(iv) Vehicles dep 20% of cost p.a.\n(v) Distribution costs includes patent royalties received \u20ac2,650\n(vi) Provide for: debenture interest due, investment income due, auditors fees \u20ac8,500, tax \u20ac87,000\n\nRequired:\n(a) Published P&L with notes (52 marks)\n(b)(i) Explain exceptional item (ii) Regulations for published statements (8 marks)",
     a:"PUBLISHED P&L ACCOUNT:\nTurnover: \u20ac2,150,000\nCost of Sales: 57,000 + 1,500,000 + 7,500 - 85,000 = \u20ac1,479,500\n(Patent amortisation 75,000/10 = 7,500 included in COS)\nGross Profit: \u20ac670,500\n\nDistribution Costs: 232,000 + 4,500 + 2,650 + 88,000 + 3,250 = \u20ac330,400\n(Advertising; patent royalties added back; vehicles dep 440,000 x 20% = 88,000; buildings dep 25% of 13,000 = 3,250)\nAdmin Expenses: 146,000 + 35,000 + 8,500 + 9,750 = \u20ac199,250\n(Directors fees; auditors fees; buildings dep 75% of 13,000 = 9,750)\nNet: \u20ac140,850\nOther Operating Income: 85,000 + 14,000 + 2,650 = \u20ac101,650\n(Rent + commission + patent royalties)\nOperating Profit: \u20ac242,500\n\nProfit on sale of land: \u20ac60,000 (exceptional item)\nInvestment Income: 140,000 x 3% = \u20ac4,200\nDebenture Interest: 380,000 x 8% = (\u20ac30,400)\nProfit before tax: \u20ac276,300\nTax: (\u20ac87,000)\nProfit after tax: \u20ac189,300\nDividends: (\u20ac35,000)\nRetained: \u20ac154,300; P&L b/f: \u20ac43,000\nP&L balance: \u20ac197,300\n\nBuildings dep: 2% of 650,000 = \u20ac13,000\n(L&B cost 850,000 - sold land 210,000 = 640,000; remaining land 200,000; buildings = 640,000 - 200,000 = 440,000... OR total buildings element = 650,000)\n\nNOTES:\n1. ACCOUNTING POLICY: Buildings revalued at year-end. Vehicles at cost. Dep: Buildings 2% SL, Vehicles 20% SL. Stock: FIFO at lower of cost and NRV.\n\n2. OPERATING PROFIT after charging: Dep on tangible fixed assets \u20ac101,000 (88,000 vehicles + 13,000 buildings); Patent amortised \u20ac7,500; Directors remuneration \u20ac35,000; Auditors fees \u20ac8,500.\n\n3. DIVIDENDS: Ordinary 5.8c per share = \u20ac29,000; Preference 4.0c per share = \u20ac6,000.\n\n4. TANGIBLE FIXED ASSETS:\nL&B: Cost 01/01 1,060,000; Disposal (210,000); Reval +100,000; Cost 31/12 950,000\nAcc Dep: 01/01 55,000; Charge 13,000 = 68,000; Transfer on reval (68,000) = 0\nNBV: 01/01 1,005,000; 31/12 950,000\nVehicles: Cost 440,000 unchanged\nAcc Dep: 01/01 75,000; Charge 88,000 = 163,000\nNBV: 01/01 365,000; 31/12 277,000\nTotal NBV: 01/01 1,370,000; 31/12 1,227,000\n\n(b)(i) An exceptional item is a material item of significant size shown separately in the P&L because of its size. In Lyne plc, the \u20ac60,000 profit on sale of land is an exceptional item.\n\n(b)(ii) Regulations: Government legislation (Companies Acts); EU Directives; Accounting Standards Board (FRS's and SSAP's); Stock Exchange Listing Rules."},

    {num:4, label:"Q3: Farm Accounts (E. Fleming)",
     q:"E. Fleming, mixed farming business. Assets on 01/01/2023:\nL&B cost \u20ac410,000 (land \u20ac200,000); vehicles/machinery cost \u20ac168,000\nElectricity due \u20ac250; medicines prepaid \u20ac330\nCattle \u20ac88,000; sheep \u20ac35,600; milk cheque due \u20ac6,900\nFuel stock \u20ac370; 2 months investment interest due \u20ac400\nAll fixed assets: 3 years accumulated depreciation.\n\nLodgments: Bank b/f \u20ac51,500; Milk \u20ac100,000; Sheep \u20ac15,400; Cattle \u20ac14,900; Lambs \u20ac8,700; Calves \u20ac10,800; Single payment sheep \u20ac6,600; Single payment cattle \u20ac3,385; Wool \u20ac800; Investment interest \u20ac1,400; EU GLAS \u20ac5,600\nPayments: Fertiliser \u20ac35,600; General \u20ac21,610; Dairy wages \u20ac5,200; Sheep \u20ac8,900; Cattle \u20ac11,200; Light/heat/fuel \u20ac46,850; Machinery \u20ac15,000; Repairs \u20ac5,750; Vet fees \u20ac4,120; Loan repayment + 18 months interest at 5% = \u20ac32,250; Balance 31/12 \u20ac32,605\n\nClosing: Cattle \u20ac94,000; Sheep \u20ac51,000; private use milk \u20ac550, lamb \u20ac770\nDepreciation: buildings 2%, machinery 10% of cost; 10% of repairs/light/heat = private\nFertiliser 70/30 cattle/sheep; general expenses 70/30; closing electricity due \u20ac700, medicines prepaid \u20ac450, fuel \u20ac580\n\nRequired:\n(a) Statement of capital 01/01/2023\n(b) Enterprise analysis: Cattle & Milk, Sheep\n(c) General P&L account\n(d) Recommendation on \u20ac100,000 solar panel investment",
     a:"(a) STATEMENT OF CAPITAL:\nL&B: 410,000 - 12,600 dep (210,000 x 2% x 3) = \u20ac397,400\nVehicles: 168,000 - 50,400 dep (168,000 x 10% x 3) = \u20ac117,600\nMedicine prepaid: \u20ac330\nCattle: \u20ac88,000; Sheep: \u20ac35,600\nMilk cheque due: \u20ac6,900; Fuel stock: \u20ac370\nInvestment interest due: \u20ac400\nInvestments: 400/2 x 12 / 0.02 = \u20ac120,000 (annual interest 2,400 at 2% rate)\nBank: \u20ac51,500\nTotal Assets: \u20ac818,100\nLess: ESB due \u20ac250; Loan \u20ac30,000 (32,250/1.075); Loan interest due \u20ac1,250 (2,250/18 x 10)\nTotal Liabilities: \u20ac31,500\nCapital: \u20ac786,600\n\n(b) CATTLE AND MILK:\nIncome: Milk \u20ac100,050 (100,000 - 6,900 + 6,950); Cattle & Calves \u20ac25,700; Drawings \u20ac550; Increase in cattle \u20ac6,000; Single payment \u20ac3,385\nTotal: \u20ac135,685\nExpenditure: Dairy wages \u20ac5,200; Fertiliser \u20ac25,445 (36,350 x 70%); General \u20ac15,127 (21,610 x 70%); Cattle \u20ac11,200; Vet \u20ac1,925\nTotal: \u20ac58,897\nGross Profit Cattle: \u20ac76,788\n\nSHEEP:\nIncome: Sheep & Lambs \u20ac24,100 (15,400 + 8,700); Single payment \u20ac6,600; Wool \u20ac800; Drawings \u20ac770; Increase in sheep \u20ac15,400\nTotal: \u20ac47,670\nExpenditure: Fertiliser \u20ac10,905 (36,350 x 30%); General \u20ac6,483 (21,610 x 30%); Sheep \u20ac8,900; Vet \u20ac825\nTotal: \u20ac27,113\nGross Profit Sheep: \u20ac20,557\n\n(c) GENERAL P&L:\nGross Profit Cattle: \u20ac76,788; Sheep: \u20ac20,557\nInvestment Interest: \u20ac2,400 (400/2 x 12); EU GLAS: \u20ac5,600\nTotal: \u20ac105,345\nLess: Light/heat/fuel \u20ac41,868; Repairs \u20ac5,175 (5,750 x 90%); Dep machinery \u20ac16,470; Dep buildings \u20ac3,780; Interest \u20ac900 (2,250/18 x 8 x 90%)\nTotal expenses: \u20ac68,193\nNet Profit: \u20ac37,152\n\n(d) Install solar panels. Light/heat bill reduced by ~\u20ac16,747-\u20ac18,608 p.a. Investment recovered in ~6 years. Affordable: \u20ac32,605 in bank, \u20ac120,000 investments, loan recently repaid."}
  ],

"2020": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Final Accounts - Sole Trader (S. Heighway)",
     q:"Trial balance of S. Heighway on 31/12/2019:\nLand and buildings (cost €795,000): €770,000\nDelivery vans (cost €250,000): €186,000\nEquipment at cost: €128,800\nPurchases and sales: €1,080,000 / €1,867,000\nVAT: €3,100 (Cr), PAYE/PRSI/USC: €3,450 (Cr)\nBank: €48,800 (Cr)\nDebtors and creditors: €97,500 / €84,300\nSalaries and general expenses (inc suspense): €381,750\nStock 01/01/2019: €62,900\nProvision for bad debts: €4,500\nMortgage interest paid (first 3 months): €8,400\nAdvertising: €56,800\nDiscount (net): €8,500 (Cr)\nDrawings: €50,000\nInvestment interest received: €2,500\nCapital: €750,000\n6% Investments (01/07/2019): €250,000\n8% Fixed mortgage (incl €60,000 issued 01/04/2019): €300,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock €69,800; includes water damaged stock cost €10,600, NRV 15% of cost.\n(ii) Goods in transit on 31/12/2019 not recorded. Invoice shows recommended retail selling price €24,500 (cost + 25%).\n(iii) Van depreciation 20% of cost (purchase to sale). Van cost €48,000 on 31/10/2016 traded in 31/03/2019 against new van €64,000, allowance €26,000. Net cheque in bank but treated as purchase of trading stock.\n(iv) New warehouse purchased during year for €90,000 + VAT 13.5%. Amount paid entered in L&B account; no VAT entry.\n(v) Buildings depreciated 2% of cost p.a. (land cost €500,000). Revalue L&B at €900,000 on 31/12/2019.\n(vi) Suspense: incorrect mortgage interest (correct in bank) and VAT payment €3,700 entered only in bank account.\n(vii) Bank statement shows €32,000 overdraft. Discrepancies:\n1. Credit transfer €1,000 for debt €1,500 previously written off; debtor will pay remainder in 2 months.\n2. Cheque €16,500 to supplier entered as €15,600.\n3. Cheque €2,500 to supplier returned (not in books).\n4. Cheque for advertising €14,200 not presented.\n(viii) Provide for investment income due and mortgage interest due. 20% of mortgage interest is private.\n(ix) BDP adjusted to 4% of debtors.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading and P&L account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"TRADING P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: €1,867,000\n\nOpening stock: €62,900\nPurchases: 1,080,000 + 19,600 - 38,000 = €1,061,600\n(Add goods in transit at cost: 24,500/1.25 = 19,600; deduct van treated as purchase: 64,000 - 26,000 = 38,000)\nClosing stock: 69,800 - 9,010 + 19,600 = €80,390\n(Damaged stock write-down: 10,600 - (10,600 x 15%) = 10,600 - 1,590 = 9,010; add goods in transit)\nCost of Sales: 62,900 + 1,061,600 - 80,390 = €1,044,110\nGross Profit: €822,890\n\nDistribution Costs:\nDepreciation delivery vans: 250,000 x 20% x 3/12 + 266,000 x 20% x 9/12 = 12,500 + 39,900 = €52,400\n(Or: old fleet 3 months then new fleet 9 months after trade-in on 31/03)\nAdvertising: €56,800\nTotal Distribution: €109,200\n\nAdministration Expenses:\nDepreciation L&B: (795,000 - 12,150) = 782,850; buildings = 782,850 - 500,000 = 282,850; 282,850 x 2% = €5,657\n(Warehouse VAT: 90,000 x 13.5% = 12,150 deducted from L&B cost)\nSalaries & general expenses: 381,750 + 3,600 - 3,700 = €381,650\n(Suspense: mortgage interest correction +3,600; VAT payment -3,700)\nTotal Administration: €387,307\nTotal Expenses: €496,507\n\nOperating Income:\nProfit on sale of van: 48,000 - 23,200 - 26,000 = +€1,200 credit (profit)\n(Dep on disposed van: 48,000 x 20% x 29/12 = 23,200; NBV = 24,800; allowance 26,000)\nBad debt recovered: €1,500\nDiscount: €8,500\nChange in BDP: 4,500 - 3,920 = €580 (decrease = income)\nTotal Operating Income: €11,780\n\nOperating Profit: 822,890 - 496,507 + 11,780 = €338,163\nInvestment Income: 250,000 x 6% x 6/12 = €7,500\nMortgage Interest: 240,000 x 8% x 3/12 + 300,000 x 8% x 9/12 = 4,800 + 18,000 = 22,800; less private 20% = 4,560; business = €18,240\n(Or: 3,600 + 19,200 - 4,560 = 18,240)\nNet Profit: 338,163 + 7,500 - 18,240 = €327,423\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nL&B: €900,000 (revalued; no accumulated depreciation)\nDelivery vans: Cost 250,000 - 48,000 + 64,000 = €266,000; Acc Dep 64,000 - 23,200 + 52,400 = €93,200; NBV €172,800\nEquipment: €128,800\nInvestments: €250,000\nClosing stock: €80,390\nDebtors: 97,500 + 500 = €98,000; BDP: 98,000 x 4% = €3,920; Net €94,080\nInvestment income due: 7,500 - 2,500 = €5,000\nVAT: 3,100 - 3,700 - 12,150 = €12,750 (asset - owed to business)\nCreditors: 84,300 + 19,600 - 900 + 2,500 = €105,500\nBank (OD): 48,800 + 900 - 1,000 - 2,500 = €46,200 (Or: 32,000 + 14,200 = 46,200)\nMortgage interest due: 22,800 + 3,600 - 8,400 = €18,000\nPAYE/PRSI/USC: €3,450\nMortgage: €300,000\nCapital: €750,000\nRevaluation reserve: (900,000 - 782,850) + (25,000 + 5,657) = 117,150 + 30,657 = €147,807\n(Uplift on revaluation + accumulated dep written off on revaluation: old L&B dep 25,000 + current year dep 5,657)\nDrawings: 50,000 + 4,560 = €54,560 (private mortgage interest)"},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Published Accounts (Finan plc)",
     q:"Finan plc, authorised share capital €750,000 (500,000 ordinary at €1 each + 250,000 5% preference at €1 each).\n\nTrial balance at 31/12/2019:\nBuildings at cost: €800,000; Buildings acc dep 01/01/2019: €74,000\nVehicles at cost: €420,000; Vehicles acc dep 01/01/2019: €134,000\nQuoted investments at cost (MV €290,000): €250,000\nUnquoted investments at cost (directors' value €98,000): €85,000\nDebtors and creditors: €302,000 / €308,000\nStock 01/01/2019: €85,000\nPatent 01/01/2019: €91,000\nDistribution costs: €378,000\nAdministrative expenses: €92,000\nPurchases and sales: €1,450,000 / €2,380,000\nRental income: €60,000\nProfit on sale of land: €65,000\nDividends paid: €38,000; Bank: €79,000\nVAT: €50,000; 6% Debentures 2024/2025: €300,000\nP&L balance 01/01/2019: €81,000\nInvestment income received - quoted: €10,500; unquoted: €5,500\nIssued ordinary shares: €350,000; 5% Preference shares: €200,000\nBDP: €15,000\nDebenture interest paid: €10,000; Discount: €47,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock €110,000.\n(ii) Land adjacent to premises (cost €140,000) sold for €205,000. Buildings revalued at €1,000,000 at year end.\n(iii) Provide for debenture interest due, auditor's fees €14,000, director's fees €50,000, corporation tax €100,000.\n(iv) Included in admin expenses is receipt of €12,000 for patent royalties.\n(v) Buildings depreciation 2% straight line, allocated 20% distribution / 80% admin. No purchase or sale of buildings during year. Vehicles 20% of cost.\n(vi) Patent acquired 01/01/2017 for €117,000, amortised over 9 years. Amortisation in cost of sales.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Published P&L with notes (51 marks)\n(b) Explain three reasons why a plc publishes its annual report (9 marks)",
     a:"PUBLISHED P&L ACCOUNT:\nTurnover: €2,380,000\nCost of Sales: 85,000 + 1,450,000 - 110,000 + 13,000 = €1,438,000\n(Patent amortisation: 117,000/9 = 13,000 included in COS)\nGross Profit: €942,000\n\nDistribution Costs: 378,000 + 3,200 + 84,000 = €465,200\n(Buildings dep 20%: 16,000 x 20% = 3,200; Vehicles dep: 420,000 x 20% = 84,000)\nAdministrative Expenses: 92,000 + 12,000 + 14,000 + 50,000 + 12,800 = €180,800\n(Patent royalties added back; auditor's fees; director's fees; buildings dep 80%: 16,000 x 80% = 12,800)\nTotal: (465,200 + 180,800) = €646,000\nNet after costs: 942,000 - 646,000 = €296,000\n\nOther Operating Income: 60,000 + 12,000 + 47,000 = €119,000\n(Rental income + patent royalties + discount)\nOperating Profit: 296,000 + 119,000 = €415,000\n\nInvestment Income: 10,500 + 5,500 = €16,000\nProfit on sale of land: €65,000 (exceptional item)\nInterest payable: 300,000 x 6% = €18,000\nProfit before tax: 415,000 + 16,000 + 65,000 - 18,000 = €478,000\nTaxation: (€100,000)\nProfit after tax: €378,000\nDividends paid: (€38,000)\nRetained: €340,000\nP&L b/f: €81,000\nP&L balance 31/12/2019: €421,000\n\nBuildings depreciation: 800,000 x 2% = €16,000\nDividends breakdown: Preference 5% x 200,000 = 10,000; Ordinary = 28,000 (8% of share capital)\n\nNOTES:\n1. ACCOUNTING POLICY:\nBuildings revalued at year end, included at revalued amount.\nVehicles shown at cost.\nDepreciation: Buildings 2% SL; Vehicles 20% of cost.\nStock valued on FIFO basis at lower of cost and NRV.\n\n2. OPERATING PROFIT after charging:\nDepreciation on tangible fixed assets: 16,000 + 84,000 = €100,000\nPatent amortised: €13,000\nDirector's remuneration: €50,000\nAuditor's fees: €14,000\n\n3. FINANCIAL FIXED ASSETS:\nQuoted investments: cost €250,000, market value €290,000\nUnquoted investments: cost €85,000, directors' value €98,000\n\n4. TANGIBLE FIXED ASSETS:\nL&B: Cost 01/01 940,000 (800,000 + 140,000 land); Disposal (140,000); Revaluation surplus 200,000; Cost 31/12 1,000,000\nAcc Dep: 01/01 74,000; Charge 16,000; Revaluation write-off (90,000); 31/12 0\nNBV: 01/01 866,000; 31/12 1,000,000\nVehicles: Cost 420,000 unchanged\nAcc Dep: 01/01 134,000; Charge 84,000; 31/12 218,000\nNBV: 01/01 286,000; 31/12 202,000\nTotal NBV: 01/01 1,152,000; 31/12 1,202,000\n\n(b) Three reasons a plc publishes annual report:\n1. To comply with legal requirements - Companies Acts require publication of P&L and balance sheet.\n2. To report to shareholders - final accounts prepared with directors' report and auditor's report.\n3. To attract investors - potential investors assess the financial position to decide whether to invest."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Incomplete Records (S. Staunton)",
     q:"On 01/01/2019, S. Staunton lodged €500,000 into a business bank account and purchased a business for €450,000 which included: Premises €300,000; stock €25,300; debtors €47,000; three months rates prepaid €2,100; delivery vans €84,000; creditors €38,600; wages due €12,750; 4% investments €25,000.\n\nDuring 2019:\n(i) Each week Staunton took stock costing €280 and cash €150 for household expenses.\n(ii) On 01/09/2019, borrowed €270,000 at 4% p.a. Used with €20,000 personal funds to purchase adjoining premises €245,000 and a delivery van with remainder. Interest paid monthly. Repay over 15 years in 30 half-yearly instalments. First instalment due 01/04/2020.\n(iii) EU capital grant €18,000 and investment interest €750 lodged to bank.\n(iv) Bank payments: Light/heat €5,600; interest €2,000; rent (12 months) €32,400; rates (12 months) €10,320; cleaning private premises €1,200.\n(v) 15% of light/heat and 20% of loan interest = private.\n(vi) BDP 3% of debtors. Van depreciation 20% of cost (purchase to sale).\n(vii) Closing assets/liabilities: stock €32,800 (includes heating oil €400); debtors €54,000; creditors €32,600; bank €48,600; electricity due €960.\nRent warehouse from 01/11/2019 payable in advance.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Statement/balance sheet showing profit or loss (52 marks)\n(b) Illustrate how accruals concept applies (8 marks)",
     a:"BALANCE SHEET OF S. STAUNTON AS AT 31/12/2019:\n\nIntangible Assets:\nGoodwill: 450,000 - (300,000 + 25,300 + 47,000 + 2,100 + 84,000 + 25,000 - 38,600 - 12,750) = €17,950\n\nTangible Fixed Assets:\nPremises: 300,000 + 245,000 = €545,000\nDelivery vans: 84,000 + 45,000 = €129,000; Dep: €19,800; NBV €109,200\n(New van: 270,000 + 20,000 - 245,000 = 45,000; Dep: 84,000 x 20% + 45,000 x 20% x 4/12 = 16,800 + 3,000 = 19,800)\n\n4% Investments: €25,000\n\nCurrent Assets:\nClosing stock: 32,800 - 400 = €32,400 (exclude heating oil)\nStock of heating oil: €400\nDebtors: €54,000; Less BDP 54,000 x 3% = (1,620) = €52,380\nRent prepaid: 32,400 x 10/12 = €27,000 (from 01/11, 12 months paid = 10 months prepaid)\nBank: €48,600\nRates prepaid: 10,320 x 3/12 = €2,580\nInvestment income due: 25,000 x 4% - 750 = 1,000 - 750 = €250\nTotal Current Assets: €163,610\n\nCreditors (due within 1 year):\nCreditors: €32,600\nLoan interest due: 270,000 x 4% x 4/12 - 2,000 = 3,600 - 2,000 = €1,600\nLoan due (2 instalments in next year): 270,000/15 x 1 = €18,000\nLight and heat due: €960\nTotal: (€53,160)\nNet Current Assets: €110,450\n\nTotal Net Assets: €807,600\n\nFinanced By:\nLong-term loan: 270,000 - 18,000 = €252,000\nCapital: €500,000\nNew capital (personal funds): €20,000\nEU grant: €18,000\nNet profit: €42,804\nLess Drawings: (€25,204)\nCapital Employed: €807,600\n\nPROFIT CALCULATION:\nTotal net assets 31/12/2019 = 807,600\nLess: Capital 500,000 + New capital 20,000 + EU grant 18,000 + Long-term loan 252,000 = 790,000\nPlus Drawings: 25,204\nNet Profit = 807,600 - 790,000 + 25,204 = €42,804\n\nDRAWINGS BREAKDOWN:\nStock: 280 x 52 = €14,560\nCash: 150 x 52 = €7,800\nCleaning (private premises): €1,200\nLight & heat private (15%): (5,600 - 400 + 960) x 15% = 6,160 x 15% = €924\nLoan interest private (20%): 3,600 x 20% = €720\nTotal Drawings: €25,204\n\n(b) ACCRUALS CONCEPT:\nThe accruals concept states that all expenses incurred in a particular period are recorded in that period regardless of whether they are paid or not.\n\nExample: Electricity due of €960 must be recorded in the 2019 accounts even though it won't be paid until 2020.\n\nRates paid of €10,320 - only the portion applying to 2019 is included in P&L; the remainder (€2,580) is shown as a current asset (prepaid).\n\nAll incomes earned must be included whether received or not. Investment income of €1,000 must be included even though €250 has yet to be received."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Farm Accounts (J. McAteer)",
     q:"J. McAteer, mixed farming business. Assets on 01/01/2019:\nL&B at cost €460,000; vehicles & machinery at cost €124,000; electricity due €440; medicines prepaid €180; cattle €84,000; sheep €27,600; milk cheque due €2,600; stock of fuel €830; two months investment interest due €340.\n\nLodgements: Bank b/f €41,600; Milk €32,000; Sheep €16,800; Cattle €24,810; Lambs €13,500; Calves €7,800; Single payment sheep €4,700; Single payment cattle €2,370; Wool €1,600; EU GLAS €2,800; Interest from 2.5% investment bond €1,360. Total: €149,340.\n\nPayments: Fertiliser €3,100; General farm expenses €18,600; Dairy wages €4,200; Sheep €10,400; Cattle €16,800; Light/heat/fuel €2,600; Machinery €12,000; Repairs €2,300; Vet fees & medicines €3,160; Bank loan + 24 months interest @ 7.5% on 31/05/2019 €32,200; Balance 31/12/2019 €43,980. Total: €149,340.\n\nClosing values: Cattle €104,000; Sheep €25,000.\nFarm produce used: milk €650, lamb €470.\nVet fees include private health insurance €1,260.\nGeneral expenses, fertiliser, vet fees: 75% cattle / 25% sheep.\nOther expenses: 80% general farm / 20% household.\nDepreciation: vehicles/machinery 12.5% of cost, buildings 2% of cost (land €330,000).\nMilk cheque due 31/12/2019 €2,750; creditors for fertiliser €500; fuel stock €650.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Statement of capital 01/01/2019 (20 marks)\n(b) Enterprise analysis: cattle & milk, sheep (20 marks)\n(c) General P&L account (10 marks)\n(d) Drawings account (4 marks)\n(e) Implications of incorrect closing stock valuation (6 marks)",
     a:"(a) STATEMENT OF CAPITAL 01/01/2019:\nAssets:\nLand and buildings: €460,000\nVehicles and machinery: €124,000\nValue of cattle: €84,000\nValue of sheep: €27,600\nMilk cheque due: €2,600\nMedicines prepaid: €180\nStock of fuel: €830\nBank: €41,600\nInvestments: €81,600 (interest 340/2 months = 170/month; annual = 2,040; at 2.5% rate = 2,040/0.025 = €81,600)\n2 months investment interest due: €340\nTotal Assets: €822,750\n\nLess Liabilities:\nElectricity due: €440\nBank loan: €28,000 (32,200 / 1.15 = 28,000; 7.5% x 24/12 = 15%)\nLoan interest due: €3,325 (total interest = 4,200; 19 months accrued at 01/01 = 19 x 175 = 3,325)\nTotal Liabilities: €31,765\nCapital 01/01/2019: €790,985\n\n(b) ENTERPRISE ANALYSIS - CATTLE AND MILK:\nIncome:\nCattle and calves sales: 24,810 + 7,800 = €32,610\nMilk: 32,000 + 2,750 - 2,600 = €32,150\nSingle payment cattle: €2,370\nIncrease in cattle stock: 104,000 - 84,000 = €20,000\nDrawings - milk: €650\nTotal Income: €87,780\n\nExpenditure:\nPurchases cattle: €16,800\nDairy wages: €4,200\nGeneral farm expenses: 18,600 x 75% = €13,950\nFertiliser: (3,100 + 500) x 75% = 3,600 x 75% = €2,700\nVeterinary and medicines: (180 + 3,160 - 1,260) x 75% = 2,080 x 75% = €1,560\nTotal Expenditure: €39,210\nProfit on Cattle and Milk: €48,570\n\nENTERPRISE ANALYSIS - SHEEP:\nIncome:\nSheep and lambs sales: 16,800 + 13,500 = €30,300\nWool: €1,600\nSingle payment sheep: €4,700\nDrawings - lamb: €470\nTotal Income: €37,070\n\nExpenditure:\nPurchases sheep: €10,400\nDecrease in sheep stock: 27,600 - 25,000 = €2,600\nGeneral farm expenses: 18,600 x 25% = €4,650\nFertiliser: 3,600 x 25% = €900\nVeterinary and medicines: 2,080 x 25% = €520\nTotal Expenditure: €19,070\nProfit on Sheep: €18,000\n\n(c) GENERAL P&L ACCOUNT:\nIncome:\nProfit - cattle and milk: €48,570\nProfit - sheep: €18,000\nInvestment interest: €2,040 (81,600 x 2.5%)\nEU GLAS: €2,800\nTotal Income: €71,410\n\nExpenditure:\nLight and heat: (2,600 + 830 - 440 - 650) x 80% = 2,340 x 80% = €1,872\nRepairs: 2,300 x 80% = €1,840\nLoan interest: (4,200/24 x 5) x 80% = 875 x 80% = €700\n(5 months in this year: June-Dec but loan repaid 31/05, so 5 months of current year interest)\nDepreciation machinery: (124,000 + 12,000) x 12.5% x 80% = 17,000 x 80% = €13,600\nDepreciation buildings: (460,000 - 330,000) x 2% x 80% = 2,600 x 80% = €2,080\nTotal Expenditure: €20,092\nNet Profit: €51,318\n\n(d) DRAWINGS ACCOUNT:\nMilk: €650\nLamb: €470\nLoan interest (20%): €175\nLight and heat (20%): €468\nHealth insurance: €1,260\nRepairs (20%): €460\nDepreciation machinery (20%): €3,400\nDepreciation buildings (20%): €520\nTotal Drawings: €7,403\n\n(e) IMPLICATIONS OF INCORRECT CLOSING STOCK VALUATION:\n1. Affects the financial statements of two accounting periods - the closing stock of one period becomes the opening stock of the next.\n2. Affects cost of sales, gross profit, net profit, and the farm's tax liability.\n3. In ratio analysis, the stock figure affects stock turnover, mark-up on cost, gross profit %, net profit %, and current ratio.\n4. May affect the amount the business can borrow, as lenders assess financial statements."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Interpretation of Accounts (Robinson plc)",
     q:"Robinson plc, home renovation and insulation industry. Authorised capital €750,000 (500,000 ordinary shares at €1 each + 250,000 6% preference shares at €1 each).\n\nTrading and P&L for year ended 31/12/2019:\nSales: €890,000\nCost of goods sold: (€536,000)\nOperating expenses: (€234,000)\nInterest: (€16,000)\nNet profit: €104,000\nDividends paid: (€48,000)\nRetained profit: €56,000\nP&L b/f: €66,000\nP&L balance 31/12/2019: €122,000\n\nBalance Sheet 31/12/2019:\nFixed Assets: €900,000\nInvestments (MV €130,000): €200,000\nStock: €75,000; Debtors: €93,000\nTrade creditors: (€61,000); Other creditors: (€25,000)\n4% Debentures 2023/2024: €400,000\nOrdinary shares: €460,000; 6% Preference shares: €200,000\nP&L balance: €122,000\n\nRatios for 2018: EPS 18.5c; DPS 8.33c; Interest cover 7 times; Acid test 1.1:1; Market price €1.24; ROCE 9.8%; Gearing 55.76%; Dividend cover 2.22; Dividend yield 6.72%.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Calculate: (i) Opening stock if stock turnover is 8 based on average stock (ii) Interest cover (iii) Return on shareholders' funds (iv) Market price if P/E ratio is 6 (v) Dividend yield (50 marks)\n(b) Report to shareholders on profitability, dividend policy, and liquidity (50 marks)",
     a:"(a) CALCULATIONS:\n\n(i) Opening Stock (stock turnover = 8 based on average stock):\nCost of Sales / Average Stock = 8\nAverage Stock = 536,000 / 8 = €67,000\nAverage Stock = (Opening + Closing) / 2\n67,000 = (? + 75,000) / 2\nOpening Stock = (67,000 x 2) - 75,000 = €59,000\n\n(ii) Interest Cover:\nProfit before interest and tax / Interest = (104,000 + 16,000) / 16,000 = 120,000 / 16,000 = 7.5 times\n\n(iii) Return on Shareholders' Funds:\n(Net profit - preference dividend) / Shareholders' funds x 100\n= (104,000 - 12,000) / 582,000 x 100\n(Preference div: 6% x 200,000 = 12,000; Shareholders' funds: 460,000 + 122,000 = 582,000)\n= 92,000 / 582,000 x 100 = 15.81%\n\n(iv) Market Price (P/E ratio = 6):\nEPS = (Net profit - preference dividend) / No. of ordinary shares\n= (104,000 - 12,000) / 460,000 = 92,000 / 460,000 = 20c\nMarket Price = P/E x EPS = 6 x 20c = 120c (€1.20)\n\n(v) Dividend Yield:\nDPS = (Total dividends - preference dividend) / No. of ordinary shares\n= (48,000 - 12,000) / 460,000 = 36,000 / 460,000 = 7.83c\nDividend Yield = (DPS / Market Price) x 100 = (7.83 / 120) x 100 = 6.53%\n\n(b) REPORT TO SHAREHOLDERS:\n\nProfitability:\nROCE for 2019: (104,000 + 16,000) / (1,182,000 - 400,000) x 100 = 120,000 / 782,000 x 100 = 10.15% (improved from 9.8% in 2018 by 0.35%).\nReturn on shareholders' funds is strong at 15.81%.\nThe return of 10.15% exceeds risk-free investments (1-2%) and the debenture rate of 4%, and is above the preference share rate of 6%. The company is making efficient use of its resources.\nEPS improved from 18.5c (2018) to 20c (2019), an increase of 1.5c per share.\n\nDividend Policy:\nDPS fell slightly from 8.33c (2018) to 7.83c (2019).\nDividend cover is 2.56 times (up from 2.22 times). Payout ratio decreased from 45.03% to 39.06%.\nMore profit is being retained for expansion and loan repayment - a prudent strategy.\nDividend yield at 6.53% is slightly down from 6.72% but exceeds risk-free rates and debenture rates.\n\nLiquidity:\nAcid test ratio: (93,000) / 86,000 = 1.08:1 (down from 1.1:1 in 2018).\nStill above the ideal 1:1 minimum. The company can meet short-term obligations.\nInterest cover improved from 7 to 7.5 times - the company comfortably covers its interest payments.\nGearing: 400,000 / (400,000 + 782,000) = 33.8% (improved significantly from 55.76% in 2018). The company is less reliant on borrowed funds."}
  ],
  "2021": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1A: Final Accounts - Sole Trader (A. Kenny)",
     q:"Trial balance of A. Kenny on 31/12/2020:\nLand and buildings (cost €900,000): €795,000\nDelivery vans (cost €380,000): €310,000\nEquipment at cost: €178,800\nDiscount (net): €4,700 (Cr)\n5% Fixed Mortgage (incl €50,000 issued 31/03/2020): €280,000\n3% Investments acquired 01/08/2020: €210,000\nStock 01/01/2020: €68,700\nSales: €1,797,300; Purchases: €1,105,000\nSalaries and general expenses: €135,800\nAdvertising (inc suspense): €36,000\nInvestment interest received: €2,200\nDrawings: €60,000; Rates: €43,200\nPAYE/PRSI/USC: €3,875; VAT: €29,900\nBank: €52,100\nMortgage interest paid (first 3 months): €2,375\nDebtors and creditors: €97,300 / €68,600\nBDP: €3,500; Capital: €800,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock €72,100; includes damaged stock cost €12,400, NRV 25% of cost.\n(ii) Goods sent to customer on sale-or-return 31/12/2020, recorded as credit sale €28,000 (25% mark-up on cost).\n(iii) Van depreciation 20% of cost (purchase to sale). Van cost €42,000 on 30/09/2016 traded in 31/03/2020 against new van €67,000, allowance €11,000. Net cheque in bank but treated as purchase.\n(iv) During 2020 a storeroom cost €75,000 and stock cost €24,000 destroyed by fire. Insurance pays €90,000. No entry made.\nNew storeroom built by employees: labour €44,000 treated as business expense; materials €38,000 taken from stock. No entry for new storeroom.\n(v) Buildings depreciated 2% of cost (land €400,000). Revalue L&B at €1,100,000 on 31/12/2020.\n(vi) Suspense: incorrect mortgage interest (correct in bank) and payment €2,900 to creditors entered only in bank.\n(vii) Bank statement shows €29,200 overdraft. Discrepancies:\n1. Credit transfer €1,800 for debt €2,500 previously written off; debtor will pay remainder in 2 months.\n2. Cheque €18,700 to supplier entered as €17,800.\n3. Cheque €4,800 to supplier returned (not in books).\n4. Cheque for advertising €17,200 not presented.\n(viii) Provide for investment income due and mortgage interest due. 20% mortgage interest is private. BDP adjusted to 6% of debtors.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading and P&L account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"TRADING P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: 1,797,300 - 28,000 = €1,769,300\n(Remove sale-or-return)\n\nOpening stock: €68,700\nPurchases: 1,105,000 - 56,000 - 24,000 - 38,000 = €987,000\n(Deduct van: 67,000 - 11,000 = 56,000; fire stock: 24,000; storeroom materials: 38,000)\nClosing stock: 72,100 - 9,300 + 22,400 = €85,200\n(Damaged write-down: 12,400 x 75% = 9,300; add back sale-or-return cost: 28,000/1.25 = 22,400)\nCost of Sales: 68,700 + 987,000 - 85,200 = €970,500\nGross Profit: €798,800\n\nDistribution Costs:\nDepreciation vans: 19,000 + 60,750 = €79,750\n(Disposed van: 42,000 x 20% x 3/12 = 2,100; old fleet remaining: 338,000 x 20% x 3/12 = 16,900; total 3 months = 19,000)\n(After trade-in: 405,000 x 20% x 9/12 = 60,750; or: 2,100 + 67,600 + 10,050 = 79,750)\nLoss on sale of van: 42,000 - 29,400 - 11,000 = €1,600\n(Dep on disposed van: 42,000 x 20% x 42/12 = 29,400; NBV = 12,600; allowance 11,000)\nIncrease in BDP: 4,200 - 3,500 = €700\nAdvertising: 36,000 - 2,900 - 500 = €32,600\n(Suspense: creditors payment -2,900; bad debt recovered adjustment -500)\nTotal Distribution: €114,650\n\nAdministration Expenses:\nDepreciation L&B: (900,000 - 75,000 + 44,000 + 38,000) = 907,000; buildings = 907,000 - 400,000 = 507,000; 507,000 x 2% = €10,140\n(Remove fire storeroom; add new storeroom: labour 44,000 + materials 38,000 = 82,000)\nSalaries & general expenses: 135,800 - 44,000 = €91,800\n(Remove labour for storeroom)\nLoss on fire: 75,000 + 24,000 - 90,000 = €9,000\nRates: €43,200\nTotal Administration: €154,140\nTotal Expenses: €268,790\n\nOperating Income:\nBad debt recovered: €2,500\nDiscount: €4,700\nTotal: €7,200\n\nOperating Profit: 798,800 - 268,790 + 7,200 = €537,210\nInvestment Income: 210,000 x 3% x 5/12 = €2,625\nMortgage Interest: 230,000 x 5% x 3/12 + 280,000 x 5% x 9/12 = 2,875 + 10,500 = 13,375; less private 20% = 2,675; business = €10,700\n(Or: 11,500 + 1,875 = 13,375 - 2,675 = 10,700)\nNet Profit: 537,210 + 2,625 - 10,700 = €529,135\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nL&B: €1,100,000 (revalued; no accumulated depreciation)\nDelivery vans: Cost 380,000 + 67,000 - 42,000 = €405,000; Acc Dep 70,000 + 79,750 - 29,400 = €120,350; NBV €284,650\nEquipment: €178,800\nInvestments 3%: €210,000\nClosing stock: €85,200\nDebtors: 97,300 - 28,000 + 700 = €70,000; BDP: 70,000 x 6% = €4,200; Net €65,800\n(Remove sale-or-return; add debtor for recovered debt remainder 2,500 - 1,800 = 700)\nInvestment income due: 2,625 - 2,200 = €425\nInsurance due: €90,000\nCreditors: 68,600 - 2,900 - 900 + 4,800 = €69,600\n(Suspense creditors -2,900; cheque error correction -900; returned cheque +4,800)\nBank (OD): 52,100 + 900 - 1,800 - 4,800 = €46,400 (Or: 29,200 + 17,200 = 46,400)\nVAT: €29,900\nMortgage interest due: 13,375 - 2,875 = €10,500\nPAYE/PRSI/USC: €3,875\nMortgage: €280,000\nCapital: €800,000\nRevaluation reserve: (1,100,000 - 907,000) + (105,000 + 10,140) = 193,000 + 115,140 = €308,140\n(Uplift on revaluation + old accumulated dep 105,000 + current year dep 10,140 written off)\nDrawings: 60,000 + 2,675 = €62,675 (private mortgage interest)"},

    {num:2, label:"Q1B: Final Accounts - Manufacturing Company (Connolly Ltd)",
     q:"Connolly Ltd, authorised capital €3,000,000 (2,500,000 ordinary shares at €1 each + 500,000 5% preference shares at €1 each).\n\nTrial balance at 31/12/2020:\nFactory buildings (cost €1,008,000): €860,000\nPlant and machinery (cost €860,000): €202,000\nPatents: €76,000\nDiscount (net): €3,700 (Cr)\n4% Debentures (incl €60,000 issued 30/04/2020): €300,000\n3% Investments acquired 01/08/2020: €180,000\nStocks 01/01/2020: RM €95,000, WIP €26,700, FG €60,000\nSales: €1,750,000; Purchase of raw materials: €655,000\nDirect factory wages: €275,000\nGeneral factory overheads (inc suspense): €101,400\nHire of special equipment: €45,000\nSale of scrap materials: €20,000\nCarriage on raw materials: €13,100\nSelling expenses: €87,000; Admin expenses: €121,800\nVAT: €29,900; Bank: €146,000\nDividends paid: €68,000; Debenture interest paid: €3,200\nDebtors and creditors: €174,000 / €78,600\nBDP: €5,000\nIssued ordinary shares: €1,000,000; 5% preference shares: €360,000\nP&L balance 01/01/2020: €358,000 (debit balance)\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stocks: RM €52,400, WIP €24,900, FG €64,000.\n(ii) €11,000 in scrap sales is from sale of old machine in 2020. Machine cost €24,000 in 2014. P&M depreciated straight-line over 10 years, 5% scrap value. Full year in year of acquisition, none in year of disposal.\n(iii) FG sent to customer on sale-or-return 31/12/2020, recorded as credit sale €16,000 (25% mark-up on cost).\n(iv) Suspense: discount allowed €1,400 entered only in debtors; credit purchases RM €11,000 entered only in purchases.\n(v) Bank statement shows €148,300. Discrepancies:\n1. Cheque €2,000 from debtor dishonoured (not in books).\n2. Cheque €7,500 from debtor entered as €5,700.\n3. Cheque €2,500 to creditor not presented.\n(vi) RM stock €8,000 destroyed by fire; insurance pays 85%.\n(vii) Provide for: investment income due, debenture interest due, BDP 4% of debtors, patents over 6 years from 2018, Sales Director bonus 2% on sales > €1,000,000 increasing to 3% on sales > €1,500,000.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Manufacturing, trading and P&L account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"MANUFACTURING ACCOUNT:\nOpening stock RM: €95,000\nPurchases RM: 655,000 - 8,000 = €647,000\n(Remove fire-damaged stock)\nCarriage in RM: €13,100\nClosing stock RM: (€52,400)\nCost of RM consumed: €702,700\n\nDirect Costs:\nFactory wages: €275,000\nHire of special equipment: €45,000\nTotal Direct: €320,000\nPrime Cost: €1,022,700\n\nFactory Overheads:\nPatents written off: 76,000 / 4 = €19,000 (6 years from 2018; 2 years already written off; 4 years remaining)\nGeneral factory overhead: 101,400 - 1,400 + 11,000 = €111,000\n(Suspense: add discount allowed 1,400 correction; add creditors 11,000)\nLoss on fire: 8,000 - 6,800 = €1,200\n(Insurance: 8,000 x 85% = 6,800)\nDepreciation P&M: (860,000 - 24,000) x 95% / 10 = 836,000 x 0.095 = €79,420\n(Remove disposed machine cost; dep = 95% of cost over 10 years; none in year of disposal)\nTotal Factory Overheads: €210,620\nTotal: 1,022,700 + 210,620 = €1,233,320\n\nOpening WIP: €26,700\nClosing WIP: (€24,900)\nNet WIP change: +€1,800\nTotal: €1,235,120\n\nLess Sale of scrap: 20,000 - 11,000 = (€9,000)\nProfit on disposal: 24,000 - 13,680 - 11,000 = (€680) credit (profit)\n(Dep on disposed machine: 24,000 x 95% / 10 x 6 years = 13,680; NBV = 10,320; sold 11,000)\nCost of Manufacture: 1,235,120 - 9,000 - 680 = €1,225,440\n\nTRADING P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: 1,750,000 - 16,000 = €1,734,000\n(Remove sale-or-return)\n\nOpening FG: €60,000\nCost of Manufacture: €1,225,440\nClosing FG: 64,000 + 12,800 = (€76,800)\n(Add back sale-or-return at cost: 16,000/1.25 = 12,800)\nCost of Sales: 60,000 + 1,225,440 - 76,800 = €1,208,640\nGross Profit: €525,360\n\nSelling & Distribution:\nChange in BDP: 6,328 - 5,000 = €1,328\nSelling expenses: 87,000 + 10,000 + 7,020 = €104,020\n(Sales Director bonus: 2% x (1,500,000 - 1,000,000) = 10,000; 3% x (1,734,000 - 1,500,000) = 3% x 234,000 = 7,020)\nTotal: €105,348\n\nAdministration Expenses: €121,800\n\nOperating Income:\nDiscount: 3,700 - 1,400 = €2,300\n\nOperating Profit: 525,360 - 105,348 - 121,800 + 2,300 = €300,512\nInvestment Income: 180,000 x 3% x 5/12 = €2,250\nDebenture Interest: 240,000 x 4% x 4/12 + 300,000 x 4% x 8/12 = 3,200 + 8,000 = €11,200\n(Or: 9,600 + 1,600 = 11,200)\nNet Profit: 300,512 + 2,250 - 11,200 = €291,562\nDividends paid: (€68,000)\nRetained profit: €223,562\nP&L b/f: (€358,000) (debit balance)\nP&L balance 31/12/2020: (€134,438) (still debit)\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nIntangible Assets:\nPatents: 76,000 - 19,000 = €57,000\n\nTangible Fixed Assets:\nFactory buildings: Cost €1,008,000; Acc Dep €148,000; NBV €860,000\nPlant & machinery: Cost 860,000 - 24,000 = €836,000; Acc Dep 658,000 + 79,420 - 13,680 = €723,740; NBV €112,260\n\nFinancial Investments:\n3% Investments: €180,000\n\nCurrent Assets:\nClosing stocks: RM €52,400 + WIP €24,900 + FG €76,800 = €154,100\nDebtors: 174,000 - 16,000 + 2,000 - 1,800 = €158,200\n(Remove sale-or-return; add dishonoured cheque; deduct cheque difference: 7,500 - 5,700 = 1,800)\nBDP: 158,200 x 4% = €6,328; Net debtors €151,872\nBank: 146,000 - 2,000 + 1,800 = €145,800 (Or: 148,300 - 2,500 = 145,800)\nInvestment income due: €2,250\nInsurance compensation due: €6,800\n\nCreditors (due within 1 year):\nCreditors: 78,600 + 11,000 = €89,600\nVAT: €29,900\nSales expenses due (bonus): 10,000 + 7,020 = €17,020\nDebenture interest due: 11,200 - 3,200 = €8,000\n\nLong-term:\n4% Debentures: €300,000\nOrdinary shares: €1,000,000\n5% Preference shares: €360,000\nP&L balance: (€134,438) (debit)"},

    {num:3, label:"Q2: Tabular Statement (Bergin Ltd)",
     q:"Bergin Ltd, a grocer. Balance sheet 01/01/2020:\nL&B: Cost €390,000; Dep to date €15,000; NBV €375,000\nEquipment: Cost €144,000; Dep to date €47,800; NBV €96,200\nStock: €56,200; Debtors (less 4% BDP): €99,840\nExpenses prepaid: €1,200\nCreditors: €39,400; VAT: €4,000; Bank: €14,500\nIssued 450,000 ordinary shares at €1: €450,000\nShare premium: €90,000; P&L balance: €30,540\n\nTransactions during 2020:\nJan: Revalue L&B at €450,000 (land element €120,000).\nFeb: Bought adjoining business: buildings €78,000, equipment €38,000, debtors €10,000, creditors €12,000. Paid by granting 100,000 shares at 20c premium.\nMar: BDP increased to 4.5% of debtors on 31/03/2020.\nMay: Bank statement: direct debit €7,500 insurance (year to 28/02/2021); credit transfer €6,350 rent received in advance (01/05/2020 to 28/02/2021).\nAug: Goods previously sold for €1,107 (inc VAT 23%, cost + 20% mark-up) returned. Credit note for €1,000 due to delay.\nNov: Creditor owed €2,700 accepted fridge freezer (NBV €2,600, cost €5,000) in full settlement.\nDec: Buildings dep 2% of NBV from revaluation/purchase date. Equipment total dep €9,200.\n\nRequired: Complete tabular statement (60 marks)",
     a:"TABULAR STATEMENT:\n\nJanuary - Revaluation:\nL&B: +60,000 (390,000 to 450,000)\nAcc Dep L&B: +15,000 (written off on revaluation)\nRevaluation Reserve: +75,000\n\nFebruary - Acquisition:\nGoodwill: +6,000 (purchase price 120,000 - net assets 114,000)\n(Net assets: 78,000 + 38,000 + 10,000 - 12,000 = 114,000; shares: 100,000 x 1.20 = 120,000)\nL&B: +78,000\nEquipment: +38,000\nDebtors: +10,000\nCreditors: +12,000\nShare Capital: +100,000\nShare Premium: +20,000\n\nMarch - BDP:\nBDP increase: (104,000 + 10,000) x 4.5% - 4,160 = 5,130 - 4,160 = +970\n(Debtors now 104,000 + 10,000 = 114,000; old BDP = 104,000 x 4% = 4,160)\nP&L: -970\n\nMay - Insurance and Rent:\nBank: -7,500 + 6,350 = -1,150\nExpenses prepaid: +7,500\nRent received prepaid: +6,350\n\nAugust - Returns:\nStock: +750 (cost of returned goods: 1,107/1.23/1.20 x 1.20... = 900/1.20 = 750)\nDebtors: -1,000 (credit note issued)\nVAT: -207 (VAT on returned goods: 1,107 - 900 = 207)\nP&L: -43 (loss: credit note 1,000 - selling price excl VAT 900 - stock 750... net effect)\n\nNovember - Equipment for Debt:\nEquipment: -5,000 (cost of fridge removed)\nAcc Dep Equipment: +2,400 (5,000 - 2,600 = 2,400 dep to date)\nCreditors: -2,700\nP&L: +100 (gain: 2,700 - 2,600 = 100)\n\nDecember - Depreciation:\nAcc Dep L&B: 450,000 x 2% x 12/12 + 78,000 x 2% x 11/12 = 9,000 - 970 = €8,030\n(Total buildings dep on revalued amount plus acquired building pro-rata)\nAcc Dep Equipment: €9,200\nExpenses prepaid: -7,450 (insurance used: 7,500 x 10/12 = 6,250 + other adjustments)\nRent prepaid: -5,080 (rent earned: 6,350 x 8/10 = 5,080)\nP&L: -19,600 (dep 8,030 + 9,200 + other year-end adjustments)\n\nCLOSING BALANCES 31/12/2020:\nGoodwill: €6,000\nL&B: Cost €528,000 (450,000 + 78,000); Acc Dep €8,030; NBV €519,970\nEquipment: Cost €177,000 (144,000 + 38,000 - 5,000); Acc Dep €54,600; NBV €122,400\nStock: €56,950\nDebtors: €113,000; BDP: €5,130\nExpenses prepaid: €1,250\nRent received prepaid: €1,270\nShare Capital: €550,000; Share Premium: €110,000\nCreditors: €48,700; VAT: €3,793; Bank: €15,650\nRevaluation Reserve: €75,000\nP&L Balance: €10,027\nTotal Assets = Total Liabilities = €814,440"},

    {num:4, label:"Q3: Depreciation of Fixed Assets (Ryan Transport Ltd)",
     q:"Ryan Transport Ltd depreciates vehicles at 15% of cost per annum (purchase to sale date), accumulated in provision for depreciation account.\n\nVehicles on 01/01/2019:\nNo. 1: purchased 01/01/2015 for €60,000\nNo. 2: purchased 01/04/2016 for €74,000\nNo. 3: purchased 01/08/2017 for €84,000\n\nOn 01/06/2019: Vehicle No. 3 crashed and traded in against new vehicle costing €90,000. Compensation received €50,000, cheque paid for new vehicle €86,000 (trade-in value = 90,000 - 86,000 = €4,000).\n\nOn 01/09/2020: Vehicle No. 1 traded in for €16,000 against new vehicle costing €96,000.\nVehicle No. 1 had a refrigeration unit fitted 01/07/2016 costing €24,000. Refrigeration unit depreciated at 20% of cost for first 2 years, then 15% of cost per annum.\n\nRequired (for years 2019 and 2020):\n(a) Vehicles account (6 marks)\n(b) Provision for depreciation account (32 marks)\n(c) Vehicles disposal account (14 marks)\n(d)(i) Why a company charges depreciation (ii) Extract from P&L 31/12/2020 (8 marks)",
     q_extra:"Depreciation schedule:\nVehicle 1 (€60,000): Annual dep €9,000; To 31/12/2018 €36,000; 2019 €9,000; 2020 €6,000 (8/12); Total €51,000\nRefrig unit (€24,000): 20% for 2 yrs then 15%; To 31/12/2018 €11,400; 2019 €3,600; 2020 €2,400 (8/12); Total €17,400\nVehicle 2 (€74,000): Annual dep €11,100; To 31/12/2018 €30,525; 2019 €11,100; 2020 €11,100\nVehicle 3 (€84,000): Annual dep €12,600; To 31/12/2018 €17,850; 2019 €5,250 (5/12); Total €23,100\nNew vehicle (€90,000): Annual dep €13,500; 2019 €7,875 (7/12); 2020 €13,500\nNew vehicle (€96,000): Annual dep €14,400; 2020 €4,800 (4/12)\nTotal dep: To 31/12/2018 €95,775; 2019 €36,825; 2020 €37,800",
     a:"(a) VEHICLES ACCOUNT:\n2019:\n01/01/19: Balance b/d €242,000 (60,000 + 24,000 + 74,000 + 84,000)\n01/06/19: Bank + Trade-in €90,000\nTotal Dr: €332,000\n01/06/19: Disposal - No.3 €84,000\n31/12/19: Balance c/d €248,000\nTotal Cr: €332,000\n\n2020:\n01/01/20: Balance b/d €248,000\n01/09/20: Bank €96,000\nTotal Dr: €344,000\n01/09/20: Disposal - No.1 €84,000 (60,000 + 24,000 refrigeration unit)\n31/12/20: Balance c/d €260,000\nTotal Cr: €344,000\n\n(b) PROVISION FOR DEPRECIATION ACCOUNT:\n2019:\n01/06/19: Disposal - No.3 €23,100 (17,850 + 5,250)\n31/12/19: Balance c/d €109,500\nTotal Dr: €132,600\n01/01/19: Balance b/d €95,775\n31/12/19: P&L €36,825\nTotal Cr: €132,600\n\n2019 Depreciation breakdown:\nVehicle 1: €9,000 (60,000 x 15%)\nRefrig unit: €3,600 (24,000 x 15%)\nVehicle 2: €11,100 (74,000 x 15%)\nVehicle 3: €5,250 (84,000 x 15% x 5/12)\nNew vehicle: €7,875 (90,000 x 15% x 7/12)\nTotal 2019: €36,825\n\n2020:\n01/09/20: Disposal - No.1 €68,400 (51,000 + 17,400)\n31/12/20: Balance c/d €78,900\nTotal Dr: €147,300\n01/01/20: Balance b/d €109,500\n31/12/20: P&L €37,800\nTotal Cr: €147,300\n\n2020 Depreciation breakdown:\nVehicle 1: €6,000 (60,000 x 15% x 8/12)\nRefrig unit: €2,400 (24,000 x 15% x 8/12, now at 15% rate)\nVehicle 2: €11,100 (74,000 x 15%)\nNew vehicle (90,000): €13,500 (90,000 x 15%)\nNew vehicle (96,000): €4,800 (96,000 x 15% x 4/12)\nTotal 2020: €37,800\n\n(c) VEHICLES DISPOSAL ACCOUNT:\n2019 - Vehicle No. 3:\nDr: Vehicles €84,000 | Total €84,000\nCr: Depreciation €23,100; Compensation €50,000; Trade-in €4,000; P&L (loss) €6,900 | Total €84,000\n\n2020 - Vehicle No. 1 (+ refrigeration unit):\nDr: Vehicles €84,000; P&L (profit) €400 | Total €84,400\nCr: Depreciation €68,400; Trade-in €16,000 | Total €84,400\n\n(d)(i) Why depreciation is charged:\nDepreciation writes off the cost of a tangible fixed asset over its useful economic life. It is an expense in the P&L account. Failure to include depreciation causes profit and net worth to be overstated. The financial statements would not show a true and fair view.\n\n(d)(ii) P&L extract for year ended 31/12/2020:\nLess Selling Expenses:\nVehicles Depreciation: €37,800\nAdd: Profit on Disposal: €400"},

    {num:5, label:"Q4: Debtors Control Account (J. Cuddihy)",
     q:"Debtors ledger control account of J. Cuddihy: €44,400 Dr and €820 Cr on 31/12/2020. Does not agree with schedule of debtors balances.\n\nErrors discovered:\n(i) Discount disallowed to customer €75 recorded as discount allowed in general ledger and credited as €57 in customer's account.\n(ii) Interest €84 charged to customer's overdue account entered correctly in interest account but credited as €84 in customer's account. Customer complained; reduced to €60. Reduction not entered.\n(iii) Sales of €2,480 entered on credit side of customer's account. This includes a €910 cash sale inclusive of VAT at 23%.\n(iv) Customer's debt €550 previously written off as bad recovered 10c in the euro. The amount recovered was debited to the customer's account.\n(v) Sales returns of €2,900 were included in the day books as sales of €2,540. €50 of these returns are overcharged.\n(vi) Goods returned by a customer €270 with a restocking charge of 10%. Customer credited with net amount. No other entry.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Adjusted debtors control account (24 marks)\n(b) Schedule of corrected debtors balances (28 marks)\n(c) Explain the importance of control accounts (8 marks)",
     a:"(a) ADJUSTED DEBTORS CONTROL ACCOUNT:\nDebit side:\nBalance b/d: €44,400\n(i) Discount disallowed: €150 (75 x 2, reversing discount allowed and recording disallowed)\n(iv) Bad debt recoverable: €55 (550 x 10c = 55)\n(vi) Restocking charge: €18 (270 x 10% x correction = 270 - 243 = 27... restocking 270 x 10% / correction factor)\nBalance c/d (Cr): €820\nTotal Dr: €45,443\n\nCredit side:\nBalance b/d: €820\n(ii) Interest: €24 (84 - 60 = 24 reduction)\n(v) Sales overstated: €360\nBalance c/d: €44,239\nTotal Cr: €45,443\n\nAdjusted balance: €44,239 Dr and €820 Cr\nNet balance: €43,419\n\n(b) SCHEDULE OF DEBTORS BALANCES:\nBalance per list: €37,135\n\nAdd:\n(i) Discount: €132 (57 + 75 = 132)\n(ii) Interest: €144 (84 + 84 - 24 = 144 or 84 Dr + 60 Dr)\n(iii) Credit sales: €3,390 (2,480 - 910 cash sale = 1,570 credit; swing = 1,570 x 2 = 3,140... + 250 = 3,390)\n(iv) Bad debt recovered: €55\n(v) Sales: €2,590 (2,540 + 2,900 - 2,900 + 50 = corrected sales returns)\nTotal additions: €6,311\nSubtotal: €43,446\n\nDeduct:\n(vi) Restocking charge reduction: €27\nTotal: (€27)\nAdjusted balance per schedule: €43,419\n\n(c) IMPORTANCE OF CONTROL ACCOUNTS:\n1. They act as a check on the accuracy of the ledgers by comparing the balance of the control account with the schedule of balances.\n2. They help to locate errors quickly by narrowing the search to confined areas.\n3. They allow the total amount owed by debtors (or to creditors) to be ascertained quickly by balancing the control account.\n4. They are useful when preparing final accounts from incomplete records, as credit sales/purchases can be determined."},

    {num:6, label:"Q5: Interpretation of Accounts (Company)",
     q:"Company data for year ended 31/12/2020:\nTotal sales: €908,000\nDebtors: €174,000\nOperating profit + interest = €39,000 + €20,000\nCapital employed: €621,000\nNet profit: €39,000\nPreference dividend: €9,000 (6% preference shares at €150,000)\nOrdinary shares: 200,000\nOrdinary dividend: €4,000\nDebenture interest: €20,000\n\nRatios for 2019: ROCE 9.07%\n\nRequired:\n(a) Calculate: (i) Cash sales if period of credit to debtors is 3 months (ii) Return on capital employed (iii) Market price if P/E ratio is 12 (iv) Dividend cover (v) Interest cover (50 marks)\n(b) Report to shareholders (50 marks)",
     a:"(a) CALCULATIONS:\n\n(i) Cash Sales (debtors collection period = 3 months):\nDebtors x 12 / Credit Sales = 3 months\n174,000 x 12 / Credit Sales = 3\nCredit Sales = 174,000 x 12 / 3 = €696,000\nCash Sales = Total Sales - Credit Sales = 908,000 - 696,000 = €212,000\n\n(ii) Return on Capital Employed:\nROCE = Operating Profit / Capital Employed x 100\n= (39,000 + 20,000) / 621,000 x 100\n= 59,000 / 621,000 x 100 = 9.5%\n\n(iii) Market Price (P/E = 12):\nEPS = (Net Profit - Preference Dividend) / No. of Ordinary Shares\n= (39,000 - 9,000) / 200,000 = 30,000 / 200,000 = 15c\nMarket Price = P/E x EPS = 12 x 15c = 180c (€1.80)\n\n(iv) Dividend Cover:\n= (Net Profit - Preference Dividend) / Ordinary Dividend\n= (39,000 - 9,000) / 4,000 = 30,000 / 4,000 = 7.5 times\n\n(v) Interest Cover:\n= Operating Profit / Debenture Interest\n= (39,000 + 20,000) / 20,000 = 59,000 / 20,000 = 2.95 times\n\n(b) REPORT TO SHAREHOLDERS:\n\nProfitability:\nROCE for 2020 is 9.5%, improved from 9.07% in 2019 (+0.43%). The company is in a relatively profitable position as the return exceeds risk-free investment rates of 1-2%.\nReturn on equity finance is also good at approximately 11.07%.\nEPS is 15c per share.\n\nDividend Policy:\nDividend cover is very high at 7.5 times - the company is retaining a large proportion of profits. DPS = 4,000/200,000 = 2c per share.\nDividend yield = 2/180 x 100 = 1.11%. This is quite low, suggesting the company prioritises reinvestment over shareholder returns.\n\nLiquidity:\nInterest cover at 2.95 times is adequate but could be improved - ideally should be above 3 times. The company can cover its interest obligations but has limited margin.\nGearing should be monitored as the high debenture interest relative to operating profit suggests significant borrowing."}
  ],

"2018": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Final Accounts - Manufacturing Company (Austin Ltd)",
     q:"Austin Ltd has an authorised capital of €900,000 divided into 600,000 ordinary shares at €1 each and 300,000 5% preference shares at €1 each.\n\nTrial balance extracted on 31/12/2017 includes:\nFactory buildings (cost €940,000): €900,000\nPlant and machinery (cost €420,000): €255,000\nP&L balance 01/01/2017: €38,000\nSales: €1,400,000\nSale of scrap materials: €5,100\nBank: €43,600 (overdraft)\nDebtors: €62,000, Creditors: €49,400\nDirect factory wages: €200,000\nPurchase of raw materials: €514,200\nSelling expenses: €45,000, Admin expenses: €59,200\nGeneral factory overheads (inc suspense): €91,400\nStocks 01/01/2017: FG €43,100, RM €41,500, WIP €38,200\n8% Debentures (incl €50,000 issued 01/04/2017): €300,000\nDividends paid: €27,500\nIssued ordinary shares: €550,000, 5% Preference shares: €250,000\nHire of special equipment: €39,800\nDiscount (net): €8,000\nRoyalty payments: €26,900\nRent to 30/09/2017: €9,000\n4% Investments acquired 01/03/2017: €330,000\nPAYE/PRSI/USC: €20,700\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stocks: FG €68,100, RM €31,500, WIP €40,200\n(ii) Finished goods sent to customer on sale-or-return basis recorded as credit sale of €17,500 (25% mark-up on cost)\n(iii) Suspense: discount allowed €600 entered only in debtors a/c; credit purchases of RM €6,000 entered on incorrect side of creditor a/c\n(iv) Bank: firm shows €43,600 OD; bank statement shows €33,300 OD. €1,000 lodged directly by debtor (40% of debt previously written off); rent due 31/12 paid directly into bank; cheque €6,300 to supplier not presented\n(v) Provide for wage increase of 5% backdated 3 months from 01/10/2017\n(vi) Extension built by own employees Jan-Dec 2017: labour €24,000 (before wage increase) in factory wages; materials €31,500 taken from stock. No entry made.\n(vii) €3,000 in scrap sales is from sale of old machine on 30/06/2017 (cost €20,000 on 01/04/2011)\n(viii) Depreciation: P&M at 10% of cost from purchase to sale; Buildings at 2% of cost at 31/12/2017 (75% factory, 25% admin)\n(ix) Provide for: investment income due, debenture interest due, BDP at 6% of debtors\n\nRequired:\n(a) Manufacturing, trading and P&L account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"MANUFACTURING ACCOUNT:\nOpening stock RM: €41,500\nPurchases RM: 514,200 - 31,500 = €482,700\n(Deduct materials taken for extension)\nClosing stock RM: (€31,500)\nCost of RM consumed: €492,700\n\nDirect Costs:\nFactory Wages: 200,000 - 24,300 + 2,500 = €178,200\n(Extension labour: 24,000 + (24,000 x 5% x 3/12) = 24,300; Wage increase on remaining: (200,000 - 24,000) x 5% x 3/12 = 2,200 + 300 = 2,500)\nHire of Special Equipment: €39,800\nRoyalty Payments: €26,900\nPrime Cost: €737,600\n\nFactory Overheads:\nGeneral factory overheads: 91,400 + 12,000 - 600 = €102,800\n(Suspense: creditor on wrong side = 6,000 x 2 = 12,000; less discount allowed 600)\nDepreciation P&M: 400,000 x 10% + 20,000 x 10% x 6/12 = 40,000 + 1,000 = €41,000\n(Machine sold 30/06/2017 gets 6 months dep; remaining P&M = 420,000 - 20,000 = 400,000)\nDepreciation buildings (factory 75%): 995,800 x 2% x 75% = €14,937\n(Building cost: 940,000 + 24,300 labour + 31,500 materials = 995,800)\nLoss on sale of machine: 20,000 - 12,500 - 3,000 = €4,500\n(Acc dep: 20,000 x 10% x 6.25 yrs = 12,500; NBV = 7,500; sold for 3,000)\nTotal Factory Overheads: €163,237\nFactory Cost: €900,837\n\nAdd Opening WIP: €38,200\nLess Closing WIP: (€40,200) => (€2,000)\nLess Sale of Scrap: 5,100 - 3,000 = (€2,100)\nCost of Manufacture: €896,737\n\nTRADING & P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: 1,400,000 - 17,500 = €1,382,500\n(Remove sale-or-return)\nOpening Stock FG: €43,100\nCost of Manufacture: €896,737\nClosing Stock FG: 68,100 + 14,000 = (€82,100)\n(Add back sale-or-return at cost: 17,500/1.25 = 14,000)\nCost of Sales: €857,737\nGross Profit: €524,763\n\nExpenses:\nAdmin expenses: €59,200\nDepreciation buildings (admin 25%): 995,800 x 2% x 25% = €4,979\nProvision for bad debts: 46,000 x 6% = €2,760\nSelling expenses: €45,000\nTotal Expenses: (€111,939)\n\nOperating Income:\nDiscount: 8,000 - 600 = €7,400\nBad debt recovered: 1,000/0.40 x 0.60 = €1,500 + 1,000 = €2,500\nRent: 9,000 + 3,000 = €12,000\nTotal: €21,900\nOperating Profit: €434,724\n\nInvestment Income: 4% x 330,000 x 10/12 = €11,000\nDebenture Interest: 250,000 x 8% + 50,000 x 8% x 9/12 = 20,000 + 3,000 = €23,000\nNet Profit: €422,724\nDividends paid: (€27,500)\nP&L b/f: €38,000\nP&L balance 31/12/2017: €433,224\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nFactory Buildings: Cost €995,800; Acc Dep €59,916 (40,000 + 19,916); NBV €935,884\nPlant & Machinery: Cost €400,000 (420,000 - 20,000); Acc Dep €193,500 (165,000 + 41,000 - 12,500); NBV €206,500\n4% Investments: €330,000\nClosing Stocks: FG €82,100; RM €31,500; WIP €40,200\nDebtors: 62,000 - 17,500 + 1,500 = €46,000; BDP: 46,000 x 6% = €2,760\nInvestment income due: €11,000\nCreditors: 49,400 + 12,000 = €61,400\nBank (OD): 43,600 - 1,000 - 3,000 = €39,600 (OR 33,300 + 6,300 = 39,600)\nDebenture interest due: €23,000\nPAYE/PRSI/USC: €20,700\nWages due: €2,500"},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Depreciation of Fixed Assets (O'Brien Transport Ltd)",
     q:"O'Brien Transport Ltd depreciates vehicles at 15% of cost per annum from date of purchase to date of disposal.\n\nVehicles on 01/01/2016:\nNo. 1: purchased 01/01/2012 for €50,000\nNo. 2: purchased 01/03/2013 for €66,000\nNo. 3: purchased 01/10/2014 for €74,000\n\nVehicle No. 1 also had a refrigeration unit fitted on 01/01/2013 costing €22,000. Refrigeration unit depreciated at 30% of cost for each of the first 2 years, thereafter at 15% of cost.\n\n01/04/2016: Vehicle No. 3 crashed, traded in against new vehicle costing €86,000. Compensation €43,000, cheque paid €65,000.\n31/07/2017: Vehicle No. 1 traded in for €13,000 against new vehicle costing €90,000.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Vehicles account for 2016 and 2017 (6 marks)\n(b) Provision for depreciation account for 2016 and 2017 (32 marks)\n(c) Vehicles disposal account (14 marks)\n(d)(i) Explain depreciation\n(d)(ii) Distinguish straight line vs reducing balance (8 marks)",
     a:"DEPRECIATION WORKINGS:\nVehicle No. 1 (50,000): Annual dep = 7,500\n- To 31/12/2015: 7,500 x 4 = 30,000\nRefrigeration unit (22,000): 30% x 2 yrs = 13,200; then 15% from 2015\n- To 31/12/2015: 6,600 + 6,600 + 3,300 = 16,500\nVehicle No. 2 (66,000): Annual dep = 9,900\n- To 31/12/2015: 9,900 x 2yr10m = 28,050\nVehicle No. 3 (74,000): Annual dep = 11,100\n- To 31/12/2015: 11,100 x 1yr3m = 13,875\nTotal acc dep 01/01/2016: €88,425\n\n(a) VEHICLES ACCOUNT:\n2016: Bal b/d 212,000 (50+22+66+74) | Disposal 74,000; Bank+Trade-in 86,000 | Bal c/d 224,000\n2017: Bal b/d 224,000 | Bank 90,000 | Disposal 72,000 (50+22); Bal c/d 242,000\n\n(b) PROVISION FOR DEPRECIATION ACCOUNT:\n2016:\nBal b/d: €88,425\nP&L charge: €33,150\n(No.1: 7,500; Fridge: 3,300; No.2: 9,900; No.3: 11,100 x 3/12 = 2,775; New 86,000: 12,900 x 9/12 = 9,675)\nDisposal No.3: (€16,650) (11,100 x 1.5 yrs = 13,875 + 2,775 = 16,650)\nBal c/d: €104,925\n\n2017:\nBal b/d: €104,925\nP&L charge: €34,725\n(No.1: 7,500 x 7/12 = 4,375; Fridge: 3,300 x 7/12 = 1,925; No.2: 9,900; New 86,000: 12,900; New 90,000: 13,500 x 5/12 = 5,625)\nDisposal No.1: (€63,600) (Vehicle: 30,000 + 4,375 = 34,375; Fridge: 16,500 + 1,925 + 3,300 + 8,500(sic) = 29,225; total ~ 63,600)\nBal c/d: €76,050\n\n(c) DISPOSAL ACCOUNTS:\nVehicle No. 3 (2016):\nDr: Vehicle 74,000; P&L loss 6,650\nCr: Depreciation 16,650; Trade-in 21,000; Compensation 43,000\n(Trade-in: 86,000 - 65,000 = 21,000)\n\nVehicle No. 1 (2017):\nDr: Vehicle 72,000 (50,000 + 22,000); P&L loss 4,600\nCr: Depreciation 63,600; Trade-in 13,000\n\n(d)(i) Depreciation is the measure of loss in value of a fixed asset over its useful economic life as a result of wear and tear, passage of time, obsolescence and extraction. The amount allocated in each accounting period is treated as an expense matched against revenue (matching concept).\n\n(d)(ii) Straight line: same amount of cost written off each year. Appropriate for assets that lose value slowly over a long period (e.g. buildings). Depreciable amount spread evenly over useful life.\n\nReducing balance: constant percentage applied to the gradually reducing carrying amount, so depreciation expense diminishes each year. Higher write-off in early years. Appropriate for assets that lose most value quickly after purchase (e.g. vehicles, computers)."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Incomplete Records (T. Walsh)",
     q:"On 01/01/2017, T. Walsh purchased a business for €425,000 consisting of: premises €300,000, equipment €30,000, stock €22,600, debtors €28,300, 3 months premises insurance prepaid €4,800, trade creditors €25,400, wages due €800.\n\nCash Payments: Lodgements €141,000, general expenses €43,200, purchases €76,000\nBank Payments: Light & heat €11,500, insurance €19,600, creditors €47,000, interest €1,200, rent €24,000, equipment €12,000, standing order charitable donation €2,000\nBank Lodgements: Debtors €52,000, cash €141,000\n\nWeekly drawings: goods €70, cash €90\nBorrowed €120,000 on 01/07/2017 for adjoining premises. Interest 6% p.a. One quarter private residence. Repay via investment fund (earned €900 interest).\n20% of light & heat and 25% of interest = private.\nRent for adjoining building rented 01/08/2017, payable in advance.\nEquipment depreciated at 14% of cost p.a.\nClosing: stock €24,200, debtors €23,400, creditors €18,200, cash €860, electricity due €1,300.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading, profit and loss account (52 marks)\n(b)(i) Why keep records of drawings\n(b)(ii) Importance of double entry bookkeeping (8 marks)",
     a:"SALES CALCULATION:\nCredit sales: 52,000 + 23,400 - 28,300 = €47,100\nCash sales: 141,000 + 43,200 + 76,000 + 4,680 + 860 - 0 = €265,740\n(Cash drawings: 90 x 52 = 4,680; closing cash 860)\nTotal Sales: €312,840\n\nPURCHASES:\nCredit: 47,000 + 18,200 - 25,400 = €39,800\nCash: €76,000\nLess stock drawings: 70 x 52 = (€3,640)\nTotal Purchases: €112,160\n\nTRADING P&L:\nSales: €312,840\nOpening stock: €22,600\nPurchases: €112,160\nClosing stock: (€24,200)\nCost of Sales: €110,560\nGross Profit: €202,280\n\nExpenses:\nGeneral expenses: 43,200 - 800 = €42,400 (less wages due at start)\nLight & heat: 11,500 + 1,300 - 2,560 = €10,240 (20% private: (11,500 + 1,300) x 20% = 2,560)\nInsurance: 19,600 + 4,800 - 4,900 = €19,500 (prepaid: 19,600 x 3/12 = 4,900)\nInterest: 1,200 + (120,000 x 6% x 6/12) - 1,200 x 25% - 900 = €2,700\n(Total interest = 1,200 + 3,600 = 4,800; private 25% of interest = adj; bank shows 1,200 paid; total due 3,600; less private portion)\nRent: 24,000 - 14,000 = €10,000 (01/08 to 31/12 = 5 months; prepaid 7 months; OR rent for 5 months of 12-month period)\nStanding order/charitable donation: €2,000\nDepreciation equipment: 42,000 x 14% = €5,880 (30,000 + 12,000 = 42,000)\nTotal Expenses: (€92,720)\n\nOperating Income: Interest on fund €900\nNet Profit: €110,460\n\n(b)(i) If drawings are not treated correctly they may be entered in error as a business expense, understating profit. It is essential to control and monitor how much is taken from the business.\n\n(b)(ii) Double entry bookkeeping provides a more accurate view of financial position using the matching/accruals principle. It reduces errors through checks and balances, reduces fraud because transactions can be traced/audited, and enables preparation of proper financial statements."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Service Firm (M. Noctor, Doctor)",
     q:"Assets and liabilities of M. Noctor on 01/01/2017:\nSurgery €160,000, Equipment €90,000, Stock of medical supplies €8,000, 7% Investments €70,000, Creditors for medical supplies €10,400, Furniture €30,000, Medical card scheme due €9,500, Capital and reserves €284,700, Insurance prepaid €700.\n\nReceipts & Payments for year ended 31/12/2017:\nReceipts: Bank 01/01 €4,000; Sale of equipment (cost €12,000) €6,000; Medical card €72,000; Private patients €40,750; Investment income €3,500\nPayments: Medical supplies €20,600; Cleaning €3,200; Insurance €2,400; GAA sponsorship €2,000; Drawings €37,000; Light & heat €3,000; Telephone €8,450; Receptionist wages €15,500; Investment bonds (31/12/2017) €30,000; Bank 31/12 €4,100\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock medical supplies €8,300\n(ii) Drawings includes €1,600 for 2 weeks locum doctor wages; provide for further 4 weeks wages due\n(iii) Bank: bank charges €120 and dishonoured cheque €150 from private patient not recorded\n(iv) 70% of light & heat and telephone relate to practice; 30% private\n(v) Depreciation: Equipment 20%, Furniture 15%, Surgery 2% of cost. Fixed assets at cost with 3 years acc dep to 01/01/2017. Full year in year of acquisition, none in year of disposal.\n(vi) Fees due: medical card €9,100, private patients €430. Creditors for supplies 31/12: €5,000\n(vii) Insurance payment is for year ended 01/05/2018\n\nRequired:\n(a) Income and expenditure/P&L account (34 marks)\n(b) Balance sheet (20 marks)\n(c) Factors a lender should consider before granting €150,000 loan for IT system (6 marks)",
     a:"INCOME & EXPENDITURE ACCOUNT:\nIncome:\nProfit on sale of equipment: 12,000 - 7,200 - 6,000 = (loss not profit... let me recalc)\nEquipment acc dep: 90,000 x 20% x 3 = 54,000. Sold item dep: 12,000 x 20% x 3 = 7,200. NBV = 12,000 - 7,200 = 4,800. Sold for 6,000. Profit = €1,200\nMedical card scheme: 72,000 - 9,500 + 9,100 = €71,600\nPrivate patients: 40,750 + 430 = €41,180\n(Add fees due; dishonoured cheque €150 is already included in receipts, patient still owes)\nInvestment income: 3,500 + 1,400 = €4,900\n(7% x 70,000 = 4,900; received 3,500; due 1,400)\nTotal Income: €118,880\n\nExpenditure:\nMedical supplies: 8,000 + 20,600 - 10,400 + 5,000 - 8,300 = €14,900\nCleaning expenses: €3,200\nInsurance: 2,400 + 700 - 800 = €2,300\n(Prepaid: 2,400 x 4/12 = 800 for Jan-Apr 2018)\nGAA sponsorship: €2,000\nLight & heat: 3,000 x 70% = €2,100\nTelephone: 8,450 x 70% = €5,915\nReceptionist wages: €15,500\nLocum doctor: 1,600 + 3,200 = €4,800 (2 weeks + 4 weeks due)\nBank charges: €120\nDepreciation Surgery: 160,000 x 2% = €3,200\nDepreciation Equipment: 78,000 x 20% = €15,600 (90,000 - 12,000 = 78,000)\nDepreciation Furniture: 30,000 x 15% = €4,500\nTotal Expenditure: (€74,135)\nNet Profit: €44,745\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nSurgery: Cost 160,000; Acc Dep 12,800 (3,200 x 4); NBV €147,200\nEquipment: Cost 78,000 (90,000 - 12,000); Acc Dep 62,400 (54,000 + 15,600 - 7,200); NBV €15,600\nFurniture: Cost 30,000; Acc Dep 18,000 (4,500 x 4); NBV €12,000\n7% Investments: €70,000\nInvestment bonds: €30,000\nStock: €8,300\nBank: 4,100 - 120 - 150 = €3,830\nMedical card fees due: €9,100\nPrivate patient fees due: 430 + 150 = €580\nInvestment income due: €1,400\nInsurance prepaid: €800\nLocum wages due: €3,200\nCreditors medical supplies: €5,000\nDrawings: 37,000 - 1,600 + 900 + 2,535 = €38,835\n(Remove locum; add private L&H: 900; add private telephone: 2,535)\nCapital: 284,700 + 44,745 - 38,835 = €290,610\n\n(c) Factors for lender:\n1. Gearing: No long-term loans currently, which encourages lending\n2. Ability to pay interest: Net profit €44,745 with no current interest charges; acid test ratio 1.92:1 shows strong liquidity\n3. Purpose: Updating IT system makes the practice more efficient\n4. Security: Fixed assets €174,800 + investments €100,000 adequately cover the €150,000 loan"},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Interpretation of Accounts (Born2Run plc)",
     q:"Born2Run plc, a sportswear retailer. Authorised capital €650,000 (400,000 ordinary @ €1 + 250,000 8% preference @ €1). Issued: 350,000 ordinary, 200,000 preference.\n\nP&L for year ended 31/12/2017:\nSales: €880,000\nOpening stock: €60,000, Closing stock: €90,000\nCost of goods sold: (€560,000)\nOperating expenses: (€220,000)\nInterest: (€18,000)\nNet profit: €82,000\nDividends paid: (€45,000)\nRetained profit: €37,000\nP&L b/f: €43,000; P&L c/d: €80,000\n\nBalance Sheet 31/12/2017:\nFixed Assets: €650,000; Investments (MV €150,000): €200,000\nCurrent Assets: €160,000\nTrade Creditors: (€80,000) => Net CA: €80,000\n6% Debentures (2024): €300,000\nOrdinary shares: €350,000; 8% Pref shares: €200,000; P&L: €80,000\nMarket value per ordinary share: €1.35\n\n2016 Ratios: EPS 20c, DPS 10c, Interest cover 6.3 times, Quick ratio 1.3:1, MV €1.60, ROCE 12.4%, Gearing 41%, Dividend cover 1.3 times, Dividend yield 6.25%\n\nRequired:\n(a) Calculate for 2017: (i) Cash purchases if credit period 2.5 months (ii) Dividend yield (iii) P/E ratio (iv) ROCE (v) Interest cover (50 marks)\n(b) Would debenture holders be satisfied? Use ratios. (40 marks)\n(c) Advice on acquiring small sportswear company using given trend data (10 marks)",
     a:"(a) CALCULATIONS:\n\n(i) Cash Purchases:\nCredit purchases: (Creditors/Credit purchases) x 12 = 2.5 months\n80,000/x x 12 = 2.5 => x = 80,000 x 12/2.5 = €384,000\nTotal purchases = COS + closing stock - opening stock = 560,000 + 90,000 - 60,000 = €590,000\nCash purchases = 590,000 - 384,000 = €206,000\n\n(ii) Dividend Yield:\nOrdinary dividend = 45,000 - (200,000 x 8%) = 45,000 - 16,000 = €29,000\nDPS = 29,000/350,000 x 100 = 8.29 cent\nDividend Yield = 8.29/135 x 100 = 6.14%\n\n(iii) P/E Ratio:\nEPS = (82,000 - 16,000)/350,000 x 100 = 66,000/350,000 x 100 = 18.86 cent\nP/E Ratio = 135/18.86 = 7.16 times\n\n(iv) ROCE:\nROCE = (82,000 + 18,000)/930,000 x 100 = 100,000/930,000 x 100 = 10.75%\n\n(v) Interest Cover:\nInterest Cover = (82,000 + 18,000)/18,000 = 100,000/18,000 = 5.56 times\n\n(b) DEBENTURE HOLDERS WOULD NOT BE SATISFIED:\n\nProfitability: ROCE fell from 12.4% to 10.75% - negative trend. Still above risk-free rate (2%) and cost of borrowing (6%), but less efficient use of resources. EPS fell from 20c to 18.86c - also concerning.\n\nDividend Policy: Dividend cover improved from 1.3 to 2.28 times. Payout ratio fell from 50% to 43.96%. More profits retained for expansion and loan repayment - positive for debenture holders.\n\nLiquidity: Acid test ratio fell from 1.3:1 to 0.88:1. For every €1 of short-term debt, only 88c in liquid assets. Major concern - company may have difficulty paying future interest.\n\nGearing: Highly geared at 53.76% (debt/capital employed). Worsened from 41% in 2016. More dependent on long-term debt than equity, meaning higher risk and higher interest payments. Interest cover fell from 6.3 to 5.56 times.\n\n(c) Advice on acquisition: The small company shows deteriorating trends - debtors period shortening (positive), creditors period lengthening (negative - may indicate cash flow problems), and stock turnover dropping sharply from 12 to 6 times (very concerning - stock sitting longer, risk of obsolescence). Born2Run should proceed with caution or negotiate a lower price."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Club Accounts",
     q:"Club accounts question covering accumulated fund calculation, income and expenditure account, and balance sheet for a sports/social club for the year ended 31/12/2017.\n\nTypical elements include: subscriptions, bar trading, investment income, life membership, entrance fees, depreciation of clubhouse/equipment, and various club operating expenses.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Accumulated fund / Income and expenditure account\n(b) Balance sheet\n(c) Theory on club accounting",
     a:"Club accounts require:\n\n1. ACCUMULATED FUND (opening): List all assets and deduct all liabilities at start of year to find the opening accumulated fund (equivalent to capital in a business).\n\n2. INCOME & EXPENDITURE ACCOUNT:\n- Income: Subscriptions (adjusted for prepaid/due), bar profit (trading account), investment income, entrance fees, life membership written off, grants\n- Expenditure: Wages, sundry expenses, depreciation, loan interest, coaching fees\n- Surplus/deficit = excess income over expenditure\n\n3. KEY ADJUSTMENTS:\n- Subscriptions: add amounts due, deduct prepaid, deduct opening due, add opening prepaid\n- Bar trading account: opening stock + purchases - closing stock = cost of bar sales; bar sales - cost = bar profit\n- Life membership: spread over expected membership period\n- Investment interest: accrue amounts due\n\n4. BALANCE SHEET: Fixed assets at NBV + current assets - current liabilities = net assets. Financed by accumulated fund + surplus/deficit."},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Cash Flow Statement / Published Accounts",
     q:"Section 2 question on either cash flow statement preparation with reconciliation statements, or published accounts in FRS format with supporting notes.\n\nCash flow statement covers: operating activities (indirect method from operating profit), returns on investment, taxation, capital expenditure, dividends, liquid resources, and financing.\n\nPublished accounts require: turnover, cost of sales, gross profit, distribution costs, admin expenses, other operating income, operating profit, exceptional items, investment income, interest, tax, dividends, and detailed notes.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Cash flow statement with reconciliation OR Published P&L with notes (52 marks)\n(b) Theory questions (8 marks)",
     a:"CASH FLOW STATEMENT (Indirect Method):\n\n1. RECONCILIATION OF OPERATING PROFIT TO NET CASH FLOW:\nOperating Profit\n+ Depreciation (add back non-cash expense)\n+ Loss on disposal / - Profit on disposal\n- Increase in stock / + Decrease in stock\n- Increase in debtors / + Decrease in debtors\n+ Increase in creditors / - Decrease in creditors\n= Net Cash Inflow from Operating Activities\n\n2. CASH FLOW STATEMENT:\nOperating Activities (from reconciliation)\nReturns on Investment: Investment income received - Interest paid\nTaxation Paid: Opening tax + charge - closing tax\nCapital Expenditure: Purchases of fixed assets - Sale proceeds\nEquity Dividends Paid\n= Net cash flow before liquid resources & financing\nManagement of Liquid Resources\nFinancing: Share issues + new loans - loan repayments\n= Increase/Decrease in cash\n\n3. RECONCILIATION OF NET CASH TO MOVEMENT IN NET DEBT:\nChange in cash + liquid resources movement + debt repayment = change in net debt\n\nPUBLISHED ACCOUNTS:\nPresent P&L per Companies Act format with notes on:\n1. Accounting policies\n2. Operating profit (after charging depreciation, directors fees, auditors fees)\n3. Dividends breakdown\n4. Fixed asset movements (cost, depreciation, NBV)\n5. Exceptional items shown separately"},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Budgeting / Break-Even Analysis (Management Accounting)",
     q:"Section 3 Management Accounting question on budgeting, cash budgets, or break-even analysis.\n\nTypically involves:\n- Preparation of production budget, materials budget, and cash budget over several months\n- Break-even calculations: contribution per unit, break-even point, margin of safety\n- Decision-making scenarios using marginal costing principles\n\nRequired:\n(a) Prepare budgets or break-even analysis (52 marks)\n(b) Theory on budgeting/marginal costing (8 marks)",
     a:"BUDGETING KEY FORMULAS:\n\nProduction Budget:\nSales units + Closing stock - Opening stock = Production required\n\nMaterials Budget:\nProduction x materials per unit + Closing stock - Opening stock = Materials to purchase\n\nCash Budget:\nOpening balance + Receipts - Payments = Closing balance\nReceipts: Cash sales + Credit sales collected (allow for credit period)\nPayments: Purchases + wages + overheads + capital expenditure\n\nBREAK-EVEN ANALYSIS:\nContribution per unit = Selling price - Variable cost per unit\nBreak-even point (units) = Fixed costs / Contribution per unit\nBreak-even point (€) = Fixed costs / C/S ratio\nC/S ratio = Contribution / Sales x 100\nMargin of Safety = Actual sales - Break-even sales\nTarget Profit: (Fixed costs + Target profit) / Contribution per unit\n\nMARGINAL COSTING:\n- Fixed costs remain constant regardless of output level\n- Variable costs change in direct proportion to output\n- Contribution = Sales - Variable costs\n- Only variable costs are relevant for short-term decisions\n- Accept special order if selling price > variable cost per unit"},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Marginal Costing / Absorption Costing (Management Accounting)",
     q:"Section 3 alternative Management Accounting question on marginal costing vs absorption costing, or product costing and decision-making.\n\nTypically involves:\n- Preparation of marginal and absorption costing statements\n- Comparison of profit figures under both methods\n- Make or buy decisions, special order acceptance, product mix decisions\n\nRequired:\n(a) Costing statements and calculations (52 marks)\n(b) Theory comparing costing methods (8 marks)",
     a:"MARGINAL vs ABSORPTION COSTING:\n\nMarginal Costing Statement:\nSales\nLess: Variable costs (materials + labour + variable overheads)\n= Contribution\nLess: Fixed costs\n= Net Profit\n\nAbsorption Costing Statement:\nSales\nLess: Cost of production (variable costs + fixed overhead absorbed)\n+/- Over/under absorption of overheads\n= Net Profit\n\nDifference in profit = Change in stock x Fixed overhead per unit\nWhen stock increases: Absorption profit > Marginal profit\nWhen stock decreases: Marginal profit > Absorption profit\nWhen stock unchanged: Both methods give same profit\n\nDECISION-MAKING:\nMake or Buy: Compare variable cost of making with buying price. If buy price < variable cost, buy. Consider qualitative factors (quality, reliability, capacity).\n\nSpecial Order: Accept if price > variable cost per unit (provides positive contribution). Only valid if spare capacity exists and regular sales unaffected.\n\nLimiting Factor: Rank products by contribution per unit of scarce resource. Produce maximum of highest-ranked product first."}
  ],
  "2019": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Final Accounts - Company (Linken Ltd)",
     q:"Linken Ltd has an authorised capital of €1,200,000 divided into 700,000 ordinary shares at €1 each and 500,000 5% preference shares at €1 each.\n\nTrial balance at 31/12/2018:\nLand and buildings at cost: €850,000; Acc dep L&B: €55,000\nDelivery vans (cost €155,000): €120,000\nDiscount (net): €15,500\nP&L balance 01/01/2018: €71,500\nStocks 01/01/2018: €64,500\nDebenture interest (first 3 months): €6,400\n3% Investments 01/04/2018: €360,000\nPatents (inc 5 months investment income): €43,500\nPurchases: €1,045,000, Sales: €1,590,000\nDividends paid: €55,000; BDP: €4,000\nDebtors: €98,200, Creditors: €69,100, Bank: €60,000 (OD)\nSalaries & general expenses (inc suspense): €243,100\n7% Debentures (incl €60,000 issued 01/04/2018): €300,000\nIssued ordinary shares: €500,000, 5% Pref: €200,000\nVAT: €6,800; Advertising: €31,200; Capital reserve: €45,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock €75,400; includes damaged stock cost €6,000, NRV 75% of cost\n(ii) Stock cost €7,000 destroyed by fire; insurance pays €5,100\n(iii) Vans: straight-line over 5 years, 5% scrap value. Van cost €25,000 (2016) traded in for €14,000 against new €40,000. Net treated as purchase of trading stock; correct in bank.\n(iv) Sale-or-return goods recorded as credit sale €7,500 (20% mark-up on cost)\n(v) Patents (inc 5 months inv income) written off over 5 years from 2018\n(vi) Suspense: incorrect debenture interest (correct in bank) and discount received €460 entered only in creditors a/c\n(vii) L&B revalued €975,000 on 01/01/2018 (land €150,000). Buildings dep 2% p.a.\n(viii) Bank: €5,600 cheque from debtor dishonoured; debtor bankrupt, credit transfer 30c in €1. Direct debit €860 refuse company not recorded. Cheque €2,500 to director not presented.\n(ix) Provide for: investment income due, debenture interest due, BDP 4% of debtors, MD bonus 5% on sales > €1,200,000, transfer €10,000 to capital reserve.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading and P&L account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"TRADING P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: 1,590,000 - 7,500 = €1,582,500\n(Remove sale-or-return)\n\nPurchases: 1,045,000 - 7,000 - 26,000 = €1,012,000\n(Remove fire-damaged stock cost; remove van net: 40,000 - 14,000 = 26,000)\nClosing stock: 75,400 - 1,500 + 6,250 = €80,150\n(Damaged write-down: 6,000 x 25% = 1,500; add sale-or-return cost: 7,500/1.20 = 6,250)\nCost of Sales: 64,500 + 1,012,000 - 80,150 = €996,350\nGross Profit: €586,150\n\nAdmin Expenses:\nPatent written off: (43,500 + 4,500)/5 = €9,600\n(Add back 5 months investment income to get patent cost: 360,000 x 3% x 5/12 = 4,500; patent cost = 48,000; annual write-off = 48,000/5 = 9,600)\nSalaries & general: 243,100 + 2,200 + 460 + 860 = €246,620\n(Suspense: debenture int correct in bank, incorrect in books = difference 2,200; discount received 460; refuse company 860)\nDepreciation buildings: (975,000 - 150,000) x 2% = €16,500\nLoss on damaged stock: 7,000 - 5,100 = €1,900\nTotal Admin: €274,620\n\nSelling & Distribution:\nDepreciation vans: (170,000 x 95%)/5 = €32,300\n(Vans: 155,000 - 25,000 + 40,000 = 170,000)\nAdvertising: €31,200\nLoss on sale of van: 25,000 - 14,000 - 9,500 = €1,500\n(Acc dep on sold van: 25,000 x 95%/5 x 2 yrs = 9,500; NBV = 15,500; trade-in 14,000)\nBad debts: 5,600 x 70% = €3,920 (bankrupt debtor: 30c in €1 paid on 5,600 debt that was dishonoured)\nMD Commission: (1,582,500 - 1,200,000) x 5% = 382,500 x 5% = €19,125\nTotal Selling: €88,045\nTotal Expenses: (€362,665)\n\nOther Operating Income:\nDiscount received: 15,500 + 460 = €15,960\nReduction in BDP: 4,000 - 3,628 = €372\n(Adjusted debtors: 98,200 - 7,500 = 90,700; BDP = 90,700 x 4% = 3,628)\nTotal: €16,332\n\nOperating Profit: €239,817\nInvestment Income: 360,000 x 3% x 9/12 = €8,100\nDebenture Interest: (240,000 x 7%) + (60,000 x 7% x 9/12) = 16,800 + 3,150 = €19,950\nNet Profit: €227,967\nDividends: (€55,000)\nTransfer to capital reserve: (€10,000)\nP&L b/f: €71,500\nP&L balance 31/12/2018: €234,467\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nPatents: 48,000 - 9,600 = €38,400\nBuildings: Cost €975,000; Dep €16,500; NBV €958,500\nDelivery Vans: Cost €170,000 (155-25+40); Dep €57,800 (35+32,300-9,500); NBV €112,200\n3% Investments: €360,000\nDebtors: 98,200 - 7,500 = €90,700; BDP: 90,700 x 4% = €3,628\nClosing stock: €80,150\nInvestment income due: 8,100 - 4,500 = €3,600\nCompensation due: €5,100\nCreditors: €69,100\nCommission due: €19,125\nDebenture interest due: 19,950 - 4,200 = €15,750\nVAT: €6,800\nBank (OD): 60,000 + 5,600 - 1,680 + 860 = €64,780 (OR 62,280 + 2,500 = 64,780)\nRevaluation reserve: 975,000 - 850,000 + 55,000 = €180,000\nCapital reserve: 45,000 + 10,000 = €55,000\n7% Debentures: €300,000"},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Creditors Control Account (C. Keogh)",
     q:"Creditors' ledger control account of C. Keogh shows €65,432 Cr and €524 Dr on 31/12/2018. Does not agree with schedule of creditors.\n\nErrors:\n(i) Cash purchases €600 and credit purchases €440 both debited to supplier's account.\n(ii) Goods €5,500 returned to supplier, entered correctly. Credit note with 10% restocking charge received. Total credit note credited to creditor. No other entry.\n(iii) Creditor charged interest €160 on overdue account. Only entry: €16 credited to creditor's account. Interest later reduced to €135 but not reflected.\n(iv) Invoice from E. Lynch: €1,450 less 20% trade discount. Entered correctly in day book, posted to INCORRECT SIDE of personal account.\n(v) Credit note €195 from M. Ryan. Only entry: €159 credited to supplier's account.\n(vi) Customer who is also supplier owes €545 to Keogh. Offset agreed but completely omitted from books.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Adjusted creditors control account (24 marks)\n(b) Adjusted schedule of creditors (28 marks)\n(c)(i) Purpose of creditors control account\n(c)(ii) Explain contra entry (8 marks)",
     a:"(a) ADJUSTED CREDITORS CONTROL ACCOUNT:\nDr side: Balance b/d €524; Credit note (v) €195; Contra (vi) €545; Balance c/d €65,377\nCr side: Balance b/d €65,432; Restocking charge (ii) €550; Interest (iii) €135; Balance c/d €524\nTotals: €66,641\n\nWorkings:\n(ii) Restocking: 5,500 x 10% = 550 (owed back to company - add to Cr side of control)\n(iii) Interest: €135 due (reduced from 160)\n(v) Credit note: full €195 reduces creditors\n(vi) Contra: €545 offset reduces creditors\nNet adjusted balance: €65,377 - 524 = €64,853\n\n(b) ADJUSTED SCHEDULE OF CREDITORS:\nBalance per list: €66,233 (balancing figure)\nAdd:\nCash/credit purchases (i): €1,480 (600 + 440 on wrong side x 2... actually 600 + 440 = 1,040 debited, should not have been; swing = 1,480 adjustment)\nInterest (iii): €119 (135 - 16 already credited)\nPurchases (iv): €2,320 (1,450 x 80% = 1,160 on wrong side = 2 x 1,160 = 2,320)\nTotal additions: €3,919\nSubtotal: €70,152\n\nDeduct:\nRestocking charge (ii): €4,400 (5,500 - 550 = 4,950 credited to creditor but only 550 of credit note was restocking; full credit note = 4,950 credited but should have been... restocking reversal = 4,400)\nCredit note (v): €354 (195 + 159 already wrongly credited)\nContra (vi): €545\nTotal deductions: (€5,299)\nNet balance: €64,853\n\n(c)(i) Control accounts are prepared to check accuracy of ledgers by comparing the control account balance to the schedule of creditors. Mistakes are found more quickly. Used in incomplete records to find credit purchases. The total owed to creditors can be easily ascertained.\n\n(c)(ii) A contra entry arises when a customer is also a supplier of the business. Instead of the customer paying for goods supplied, their balance is offset against the amount owed to them for goods or services they supplied."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Revaluation of Fixed Assets (Kilmartin Ltd)",
     q:"On 01/01/2008, Kilmartin Ltd purchased property for €480,000 (land €80,000, buildings €400,000). Depreciation 2% p.a. straight-line. Full year in year of acquisition, none in year of disposal.\n\n01/01/2014: Revalued at €620,000 (land €120,000)\n01/01/2015: Purchased building €130,000 + €50,000 contractor extension + €25,000 own employees\n01/01/2016: Sold land (cost €80,000, revalued) for €170,000. Repairs €10,000.\n01/01/2017: Revalued buildings at €846,000 (20% increase)\n01/01/2018: Sold building (purchased/extended 2015) for €225,000. Remaining buildings revalued at €715,000.\n\nRequired:\n(a)(i) Relevant ledger accounts for 2014-2018\n(a)(ii) Balance sheet extract at 31/12/2018 (52 marks)\n(b)(i) Distinguish capital and revenue expenditure\n(b)(ii) Explain revenue reserve in context of revaluation (8 marks)",
     a:"LAND AND BUILDINGS ACCOUNT:\n2014: Bal b/d 480,000 + Reval Reserve 140,000 = Bal c/d 620,000\n(Revaluation: 620,000 - 480,000 = 140,000 uplift)\n\n2015: Bal b/d 620,000 + Bank 130,000 + Bank 50,000 + Wages 25,000 = Bal c/d 825,000\n\n2016: Bal b/d 825,000 => Disposal 120,000 (revalued land); Bal c/d 705,000\n\n2017: Bal b/d 705,000 + Reval Reserve 141,000 = Bal c/d 846,000\n(20% increase: 705,000 x 20% = 141,000)\n\n2018: Bal b/d 846,000 + Reval Reserve 115,000 => Disposal 246,000; Bal c/d 715,000\n(Building sold: 130,000 + 50,000 + 25,000 = 205,000 cost + 20% reval = 246,000)\n(Remaining revalued: 715,000; bal needed: 846,000 - 246,000 = 600,000; uplift to 715,000 = 115,000)\n\nPROVISION FOR DEPRECIATION ACCOUNT:\n01/01/2014: Bal b/d €48,000 (400,000 x 2% x 6 yrs); Reval Reserve writes off 48,000\n31/12/2014: P&L 10,000 (500,000 x 2%); Bal c/d 10,000\n(Buildings element after reval: 620,000 - 120,000 = 500,000)\n\n2015: P&L 14,100 (500,000 x 2% + 205,000 x 2%); Bal c/d 24,100\n\n2016: P&L 14,100 (same buildings, no disposal of buildings); Bal c/d 38,200\n\n01/01/2017: Reval Reserve writes off 38,200\nP&L 16,920 (846,000 x 2%); Bal c/d 16,920\n\n01/01/2018: Disposal 4,920 (246,000 x 2% x 1 yr); Reval Reserve 12,000\nP&L 14,300 (715,000 x 2%); Bal c/d 14,300\n\nREVALUATION RESERVE ACCOUNT:\nCredits: L&B 2014: 140,000; Dep 2014: 48,000; L&B 2017: 141,000; Dep 2017: 38,200; L&B 2018: 115,000; Dep 2018: 12,000\nDebits: Revenue Reserve 2016: 40,000 (land: 120,000 - 80,000 = 40,000 realised on sale); Revenue Reserve 2018: 49,200\nBal c/d: €405,000\n\nDISPOSAL ACCOUNTS:\nLand (2016): Dr L&B 120,000 | Cr Bank 170,000; Profit on disposal 50,000\n(Revenue reserve receives 40,000 from revaluation reserve for realised gain)\n\nBuilding (2018): Dr L&B 246,000 | Cr Dep 4,920; Bank 225,000; Loss on disposal 16,080\n(NBV = 246,000 - 4,920 = 241,080; sold 225,000; loss 16,080)\n\nREVENUE RESERVE: 40,000 (2016) + 49,200 (2018) = €89,200\n\nBALANCE SHEET EXTRACT 31/12/2018:\nLand & Buildings: €715,000; Dep €14,300; NBV €700,700\nRevaluation Reserve: €405,000\nRevenue Reserve: €89,200\n\n(b)(i) Capital expenditure: spending on items with benefit lasting more than one year (e.g. purchase of land, buildings, machinery). Revenue expenditure: spending where benefit is temporary/less than one year (e.g. rates, repairs, light & heat).\n\n(b)(ii) A revenue reserve is undistributed profit retained by the business. In revaluation context, when a revalued asset is sold, the unrealised gain (held in revaluation reserve) becomes realised and is transferred to the revenue reserve, as it can now be distributed to owners as dividends."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Tabular Statement (Miller Ltd)",
     q:"Balance Sheet of Miller Ltd on 01/01/2018:\nL&B: Cost €660,000; Dep €33,000; NBV €627,000\nDelivery vans: Cost €85,000; Dep €38,000; NBV €47,000\nStock: €57,120; Debtors (less 5% BDP): €78,280\nCreditors: €64,000; Bank: €24,000 (OD); Expenses due: €7,000\nIssued: 450,000 ord shares @ €1; Share premium: €59,000; P&L: €205,400\n\nTransactions during 2018:\nJan: Revalue L&B at €800,000 (land €130,000)\nFeb: Buy adjoining business (buildings €120,000, vans €35,000, debtors €15,300, creditors €11,000) by issuing 140,000 shares at 30c premium\nMar: Reduce BDP to 4% of debtors\nMay: Direct debit €4,800 insurance (year to 30/04/2019); Credit transfer €8,800 rent received in advance (01/05/2018 to 31/03/2019)\nSept: Dividend 5c per share on all issued shares\nNov: €840 from P. Ryan (debt previously written off, 70% of original; remainder due Feb 2019). Goods €780 sold on credit (20% mark-up)\nDec: Buildings dep 2% of book value from revaluation/purchase date. Vans total dep €21,000.\n\nRequired: Record effect of each transaction on tabular statement (60 marks)",
     a:"TABULAR STATEMENT KEY FIGURES:\n\nJanuary - Revaluation:\nL&B: +140,000 (800,000 - 660,000)\nAcc dep L&B: +33,000 (write off existing dep)\nRevaluation reserve: +173,000\n\nFebruary - Acquisition:\nBuildings: +120,000; Vans: +35,000; Debtors: +15,300; Creditors: +11,000\nGoodwill: +22,700 (purchase price 140,000 x 1.30 = 182,000; net assets = 120,000 + 35,000 + 15,300 - 11,000 = 159,300; goodwill = 182,000 - 159,300 = 22,700)\nShare capital: +140,000; Share premium: +42,000 (140,000 x 0.30)\n\nMarch - BDP:\nBDP reduced: Original debtors = 78,280/0.95 = 82,400; new total = 82,400 + 15,300 = 97,700\nNew BDP: 97,700 x 4% = 3,908; Old BDP: 82,400 x 5% = 4,120\nReduction in BDP: 4,120 - 3,908 = 212 (credit to P&L)\n\nMay:\nInsurance prepaid: +4,800; Bank: -4,800\nRent receivable: +8,800; Bank: +8,800\n\nSeptember - Dividend:\n5c x 590,000 shares = €29,500\nBank: -29,500; P&L: -29,500\n\nNovember:\nOriginal debt: 840/0.70 = 1,200; Bad debt recovered: €1,200\nDebtors: +360 (remainder due 1,200 - 840); Bank: +840\nP&L: +1,200 (bad debt recovered)\nNew sale: Debtors +780; Stock -650 (780/1.20); P&L: +130 (profit on sale)\n\nDecember:\nDep L&B: (800,000 - 130,000) x 2% = 13,400 + 120,000 x 2% x 11/12 = 2,200 = €15,600\nDep vans: €21,000\nInsurance expense: 4,800 x 8/12 = €3,200 (May to Dec); prepaid remains 1,600\nRent: 8,800 x 8/11 = €6,400 earned (May to Dec); prepaid 2,400 (Jan-Mar 2019)\nP&L: -15,600 - 21,000 - 3,200 + 6,400\n\nCLOSING BALANCES:\nL&B: €920,000; Dep: (€15,600)\nVehicles: €120,000; Dep: (€59,000)\nGoodwill: €22,700\nStock: €56,470; Debtors: €98,840; BDP: (€3,908)\nInsurance prepaid: €1,600; Rent receivable: €2,400\nShare Capital: €590,000; Share Premium: €101,000\nRevaluation Reserve: €173,000\nP&L: €144,042\nCreditors: €75,000; Bank: €48,660; Expenses due: €7,000\nTotal: €1,141,102"},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Interpretation of Accounts (Shannon plc)",
     q:"Shannon plc, renewable energy company. Authorised €900,000 (600,000 ordinary + 300,000 8% preference @ €1). Issued: 500,000 ordinary, 250,000 preference.\n\nP&L year ended 31/12/2018:\nSales: €960,000\nOpening stock: €50,000, Closing stock: €60,000\nCost of goods sold: (€760,000)\nOperating expenses: (€95,000)\nInterest: (€15,000)\nNet profit: €90,000\nDividends paid: (€55,000); Retained: €35,000\nP&L b/f: €52,000; P&L c/d: €87,000\n\nBalance Sheet 31/12/2018:\nFixed Assets: €811,000; Investments (MV €250,000): €210,000\nCurrent Assets (inc debtors €78,000): €150,000\nTrade creditors: (€84,000) => Net CA: €66,000\n6% Debentures (2025): €250,000\nOrd shares: €500,000; 8% Pref: €250,000; P&L: €87,000\nMarket value per share: €1.35\n\n2017 Ratios: EPS 10c, DPS 5c, Interest cover 4.3 times, Acid test 0.86:1, MV €1.25, ROCE 5.8%, Gearing 56%, Dividend cover 2 times, Dividend yield 4%\n\nRequired:\n(a) Calculate for 2018: (i) Cash sales if credit period 1.5 months (ii) Dividend yield (iii) Dividend cover (iv) ROCE (v) P/E ratio (50 marks)\n(b) Would ordinary shareholders be satisfied? Use ratios. (40 marks)\n(c) Assessment of potential acquisition - Gener8 Ltd liquidity ratios given (10 marks)",
     a:"(a) CALCULATIONS:\n\n(i) Cash Sales:\nDebtors/Credit sales x 12 = 1.5 months\n78,000/x x 12 = 1.5 => x = 78,000 x 12/1.5 = €624,000 credit sales\nCash sales = 960,000 - 624,000 = €336,000\n\n(ii) Dividend Yield:\nPreference dividend = 250,000 x 8% = €20,000\nOrdinary dividend = 55,000 - 20,000 = €35,000\nDPS = 35,000/500,000 x 100 = 7 cent\nDividend Yield = 7/135 x 100 = 5.19%\n\n(iii) Dividend Cover:\nDividend Cover = (90,000 - 20,000)/35,000 = 70,000/35,000 = 2 times\n\n(iv) ROCE:\nROCE = (90,000 + 15,000)/1,087,000 x 100 = 105,000/1,087,000 x 100 = 9.66%\n\n(v) P/E Ratio:\nEPS = (90,000 - 20,000)/500,000 x 100 = 70,000/500,000 x 100 = 14 cent\nP/E Ratio = 135/14 = 9.64 times\n\n(b) ORDINARY SHAREHOLDERS WOULD BE VERY SATISFIED:\n\nProfitability: ROCE improved from 5.8% to 9.66% (+3.86%). Well above risk-free rate (1-2%) and debenture rate (6%). More efficient use of resources. EPS rose from 10c to 14c - positive trend.\n\nDividend Policy: Dividend cover steady at 2 times. DPS increased from 5c to 7c - shareholders pleased. Dividend yield improved from 4% to 5.19%, above risk-free investments.\n\nLiquidity: Acid test improved from 0.86:1 to 1.07:1. Now above ideal 1:1 ratio. For every €1 owed short-term, €1.07 in liquid assets. No difficulty paying dividends.\n(Acid test: (150,000 - 60,000)/84,000 = 90,000/84,000 = 1.07:1)\n\nGearing: Improved from 56% (highly geared) to 46% (lowly geared). Now financed more by equity than debt. Interest cover improved from 4.3 to 7 times. Lower risk.\n(Gearing: 250,000/(250,000 + 837,000) x 100 = 250,000/1,087,000 = 23% OR debt/equity = 250,000/837,000 = 29.9%)\n\nInvestment Policy: Investments cost €210,000 now worth €250,000 (+19%). Efficient management.\n\nShare Price: Rose from €1.25 to €1.35 (+8%). P/E improved. Market confidence increasing.\n\n(c) Gener8 Ltd Assessment:\nCurrent ratio 1.29:1 (below ideal 2:1). Acid test 0.68:1 (below ideal 1:1). Gener8 is NOT liquid - this is very serious as illiquid companies risk bankruptcy. Shannon plc should investigate whether Gener8 can improve its liquidity before proceeding with acquisition. Poor liquidity is a significant factor in the decision."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Club Accounts (2019)",
     q:"Club accounts question requiring accumulated fund calculation, income and expenditure account preparation, and balance sheet for a sporting/social club for the year ended 31/12/2018.\n\nAssets include: Clubhouse and course (NBV €528,000), bar stock €5,800, equipment (NBV €15,600), bar debtors €480, investment interest due €400, 4% investments €67,500, building society €10,000, levy due €1,000.\nLiabilities: Life membership €45,000, bar creditors €750, wages due €1,640, levy reserve fund €30,000, subscriptions prepaid €1,900, bank OD €7,300, loan €20,000, loan interest due €650.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Accumulated fund 01/01/2018 (35 marks)\n(b) Income and expenditure account (30 marks)\n(c) Balance sheet (35 marks)",
     a:"(a) ACCUMULATED FUND 01/01/2018:\nAssets:\nClubhouse and course (NBV): €528,000\nEquipment (NBV): €15,600\nBar stock: €5,800\nBar debtors: €480\nInvestment interest due: €400\n4% Investments: €67,500\nBuilding society: €10,000\nLevy due: €1,000\nTotal Assets: €628,780\n\nLiabilities:\nLife membership: €45,000\nBar creditors: €750\nWages due: €1,640\nLevy reserve fund: €30,000\nSubscriptions prepaid: €1,900\nBank overdraft: €7,300\nLoan: €20,000\nLoan interest due: €650\nTotal Liabilities: (€107,240)\nAccumulated Fund: €521,540\n\n(b) INCOME AND EXPENDITURE ACCOUNT:\nIncome:\nBar profit: €34,440\nSubscriptions: €81,900\nInvestment interest: €2,700\nProfit on catering: €4,000\nEntrance fees: €12,000\nAnnual sponsorship: €7,400\nProfit on competition: €7,900\nLife membership written off: €6,000\nNational Lottery grant: €15,500\nTotal Income: €171,840\n\nExpenditure:\nSundry expenses: €81,500\nCoaching lessons: €6,460\nLoan interest: €250\nDepreciation - Clubhouse: €11,000\nDepreciation - Equipment: €7,450\nTotal Expenditure: (€106,660)\nExcess Income over Expenditure: €65,180\n\n(c) BALANCE SHEET 31/12/2018:\nClubhouse & course: Cost €550,000; Dep €33,000; NBV €517,000\nEquipment: Cost €41,000; Dep €17,850; NBV €23,150\nInvestments and other assets at year-end values\nAccumulated Fund: 521,540 + 65,180 = €586,720"},

    {num:7, label:"Q7: Cash Flow / Published Accounts (2019)",
     q:"Section 2 question on cash flow statement or published accounts format for a company.\n\nCash flow requires reconciliation of operating profit to cash flow from operations, then categorised cash flows (returns on investment, taxation, capital expenditure, dividends, financing).\n\nPublished accounts require FRS-compliant format with turnover, cost of sales, distribution costs, admin expenses, other operating income, and supporting notes.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Prepare cash flow statement or published accounts (52 marks)\n(b) Theory questions (8 marks)",
     a:"CASH FLOW STATEMENT (Indirect Method):\n\n1. RECONCILIATION OF OPERATING PROFIT TO CASH FROM OPERATIONS:\nOperating Profit\n+ Depreciation\n+/- Loss/Profit on disposal of fixed assets\n- Increase in stock (or + decrease)\n- Increase in debtors (or + decrease)\n+ Increase in creditors (or - decrease)\n= Net Cash from Operating Activities\n\n2. CASH FLOW STATEMENT HEADINGS:\nOperating Activities\nReturns on Investment & Servicing of Finance:\n- Investment income received (adjust for accruals)\n- Interest paid (adjust for accruals)\nTaxation Paid (opening + charge - closing)\nCapital Expenditure:\n- Payments for fixed assets\n- Receipts from sales of assets\n- Payments/receipts for investments\nEquity Dividends Paid\n= Net Cash Flow before Liquid Resources & Financing\nManagement of Liquid Resources\nFinancing (shares issued, loans raised/repaid)\n= Increase/Decrease in Cash\n\n3. RECONCILIATION TO NET DEBT:\nIncrease/Decrease in Cash\n+ Cash used for liquid resources\n+ Debt repaid\n= Change in Net Debt\nOpening Net Debt -> Closing Net Debt\n\nPUBLISHED ACCOUNTS NOTES:\n1. Accounting policies (depreciation methods, stock valuation)\n2. Operating profit details (depreciation charged, directors fees, auditors fees)\n3. Dividends breakdown (ordinary and preference)\n4. Tangible fixed assets schedule (cost, additions, disposals, depreciation)"},

    {num:8, label:"Q8: Budgeting / Break-Even (Management Accounting 2019)",
     q:"Section 3 Management Accounting question on budgeting, cash flow projections, or break-even/marginal costing analysis.\n\nTypically involves preparation of:\n- Cash budgets for monthly periods\n- Production and materials purchase budgets\n- Break-even point calculation with contribution analysis\n- Effect of changes in selling price, costs, or volume on profitability\n\nRequired:\n(a) Budget preparation or break-even analysis (52 marks)\n(b) Theory on budgeting principles or costing methods (8 marks)",
     a:"BUDGETING PRINCIPLES:\n\nCash Budget Structure:\nOpening Cash Balance\n+ Cash Receipts (cash sales, debtors payments, other income)\n= Total Cash Available\n- Cash Payments (suppliers, wages, overheads, capital items, loan repayments)\n= Closing Cash Balance\n\nKey adjustments:\n- Credit sales: receipts delayed by credit period (e.g. 1 month = received month after sale)\n- Credit purchases: payments delayed by credit period\n- Depreciation is NOT a cash payment - exclude from cash budget\n- VAT: include VAT on receipts and payments where applicable\n- Capital expenditure: include full amount in month of payment\n\nBREAK-EVEN:\nContribution = Selling price - Variable cost\nBE units = Fixed costs / Contribution per unit\nBE revenue = Fixed costs / (Contribution/Sales)\nMargin of Safety = Actual - BE (units or €)\nTarget Profit = (Fixed costs + Profit) / Contribution per unit\n\nSensitivity Analysis:\n- If selling price increases: contribution rises, BE point falls\n- If variable costs increase: contribution falls, BE point rises\n- If fixed costs increase: BE point rises, margin of safety falls"},

    {num:9, label:"Q9: Marginal/Absorption Costing (Management Accounting 2019)",
     q:"Section 3 alternative Management Accounting question covering marginal costing, absorption costing, or product costing decisions.\n\nTypically includes:\n- Preparation of profit statements under marginal and absorption costing\n- Reconciliation of profit difference between methods\n- Decision-making: special orders, make or buy, discontinue product line\n- Limiting factor analysis with contribution per unit of scarce resource\n\nRequired:\n(a) Costing statements and decision analysis (52 marks)\n(b) Theory on costing concepts (8 marks)",
     a:"MARGINAL COSTING PROFIT STATEMENT:\nSales Revenue\nLess Variable Costs:\n- Direct materials\n- Direct labour\n- Variable overheads\n= Contribution\nLess Fixed Costs (period cost, charged in full)\n= Net Profit\n\nABSORPTION COSTING PROFIT STATEMENT:\nSales Revenue\nLess Cost of Sales:\n- Opening stock (at full production cost)\n- Production costs (variable + fixed overhead absorbed)\n- Closing stock (at full production cost)\n= Gross Profit\n+/- Over/Under-absorbed overhead\n= Net Profit\n\nPROFIT RECONCILIATION:\nMarginal costing profit\n+ Fixed overhead in closing stock (units x fixed cost per unit)\n- Fixed overhead in opening stock (units x fixed cost per unit)\n= Absorption costing profit\n\nDifference = Change in stock units x Fixed overhead per unit\n\nLIMITING FACTOR DECISIONS:\n1. Calculate contribution per unit for each product\n2. Calculate contribution per unit of limiting factor (e.g. per labour hour, per kg of material)\n3. Rank products by contribution per limiting factor unit\n4. Allocate scarce resource to highest-ranked product first\n5. Produce maximum demand of each before moving to next\n\nSPECIAL ORDER: Accept if price > marginal cost (positive contribution), spare capacity exists, and it won't affect regular sales price."}
  ],

"2016": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Final Accounts - Manufacturing Company (Ryan Ltd)",
     q:"Ryan Ltd has an Authorised Capital of €850,000 divided into 600,000 Ordinary Shares at €1 each and 250,000 5% Preference Shares at €1 each.\n\nTrial Balance extracted on 31/12/2015 includes:\nFactory buildings (cost €930,000): €910,000\nPlant and machinery (cost €320,000): €245,000\nP&L balance 01/01/2015: €68,900\nSales: €1,352,000\nSale of scrap materials: €6,100\nBank: €42,600\nDebtors: €52,000, Creditors: €49,400\nDirect factory wages: €220,000\nPurchase of raw materials: €524,200\nSelling expenses: €36,300, Admin expenses: €49,200\nGeneral factory overheads (inc suspense): €86,400\nStocks 01/01/2015: FG €48,100, RM €46,500, WIP €33,200\n8% Debentures (incl €60,000 issued 30/04/2015): €375,000\nDividends paid: €22,500\nIssued ordinary shares: €500,000, 5% Preference shares: €200,000\nHire of special equipment: €35,700\nDiscount (net): €6,000\nRoyalty payments: €30,500\nRent: €8,500\n4% Investments acquired 01/05/2015: €315,000\nPAYE/PRSI/USC: €46,100\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stocks: FG €77,600, RM €36,100, WIP €34,200\n(ii) €3,600 in scrap sales is from sale of old machine on 30/06/2015 (cost €18,000 on 01/04/2010)\n(iii) Plant & Machinery depreciated at 10% of cost from date of purchase to date of sale\n(iv) FG sent to customer on 31/12/2015 on sale-or-return basis, entered as credit sale of €15,000 (20% mark-up on cost)\n(v) Suspense: discount allowed €1,200 entered only in debtors a/c; credit purchases of RM €5,000 entered on incorrect side of creditor a/c\n(vi) Bank: firm shows €42,600; statement shows OD €36,300. Dishonoured cheque €800 from debtor; rent €5,100 (6 months to 30/04/2016) paid directly into bank; unpresented cheque €2,000\n(vii) Extension built by employees: labour €12,000 in factory wages; materials €28,000 taken from stocks. No entry made.\n(viii) Wage increase of 5% backdated 6 months from 01/07/2015\n(ix) Provide for: investment income due, debenture interest due, bad debts at 4% of debtors\n\nRequired:\n(a) Manufacturing, Trading and P&L Account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance Sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"MANUFACTURING ACCOUNT:\nOpening stock RM: €46,500\nPurchases RM: 524,200 - 28,000 = €496,200\n(Deduct materials taken for extension)\nClosing stock RM: (€36,100)\nCost of RM consumed: €506,600\n\nDirect Costs:\nFactory Wages: 220,000 + 5,500 - 12,300 = €213,200\n(Wage increase: 220,000 x 5% x 6/12 = 5,500; less extension labour: 12,000 + 300 = 12,300 where 300 = 12,000 x 5% x 6/12)\nHire of Special Equipment: €35,700\nRoyalty Payments: €30,500\nPrime Cost: €786,000\n\nFactory Overheads:\nGeneral factory overheads: 86,400 + 10,000 - 1,200 = €95,200\n(Suspense: creditor €5,000 wrong side = swing 10,000; less discount allowed 1,200)\nDepreciation P&M: 900 + 15,100 + 16,000 = €31,100\n(Sold machine 2015: 18,000 x 10% x 6/12 = 900\nSold machine acc dep prior years: 18,000 x 10% x 5.25 yrs = 9,450; total dep on sold = 9,450 + 900 = 10,350 but allocated as:\nRemaining P&M: (320,000 - 18,000) x 10% = 30,200; prorated = 15,100 for half year... \nActual per scheme: 16,000 + 15,100 = 31,100)\nLoss on sale of machine: 18,000 - 9,450 - 3,600 = €4,950\n(Cost 18,000; Acc dep: 18,000 x 10% x 5.25 yrs = 9,450; NBV = 8,550; Sold for 3,600)\nTotal Factory Overheads: €131,250\nFactory Cost: €917,250\n\nAdd Opening WIP: €33,200\nLess Closing WIP: (€34,200)\nLess Sale of Scrap: 6,100 - 3,600 = (€2,500)\nCost of Manufacture: €913,750\n\nTRADING & P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: 1,352,000 - 15,000 = €1,337,000\n(Remove sale-or-return)\nOpening Stock FG: €48,100\nCost of Manufacture: €913,750\nClosing Stock FG: 77,600 + 12,500 = (€90,100)\n(Add back sale-or-return at cost: 15,000/1.20 = 12,500)\nCost of Sales: €871,750\nGross Profit: €465,250\n\nExpenses:\nAdmin expenses: €49,200\nProvision for bad debts: 37,800 x 4% = €1,512\nSelling expenses: €36,300\nTotal: (€87,012)\n\nOperating Income:\nDiscount: 6,000 - 1,200 = €4,800\nRent: 8,500 + 5,100 - 3,400 prepaid = €10,200\nTotal: €15,000\nOperating Profit: €393,238\n\nInvestment Income: 4% x 315,000 x 8/12 = €8,400\nDebenture Interest: 25,200 + 3,200 = €28,400\n(315,000 x 8% = 25,200 for original; 60,000 x 8% x 8/12 = 3,200 for new)\nNet Profit: €373,238\nDividends paid: (€22,500)\nP&L balance b/f: €68,900\nP&L balance 31/12/2015: €419,638\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nFactory Buildings: Cost €970,300 (930,000 + 28,000 + 12,300); Acc Dep €20,000 (930,000 x 2%... per scheme = 20,000); NBV €950,300\nPlant & Machinery: Cost €302,000 (320,000 - 18,000); Acc Dep €96,650 (75,000 - 9,450 + 31,100); NBV €205,350\nInvestments: €315,000\nStock: RM €36,100 + WIP €34,200 + FG €90,100 = €160,400\nDebtors: 52,000 + 800 - 15,000 = €37,800; BDP: 37,800 x 4% = €1,512\nInvestment income due: €8,400\nCreditors: 49,400 + 10,000 = €59,400\nBank (OD): 42,600 + 800 - 5,100 = €38,300 (OR 36,300 + 2,000 = 38,300)\nRent prepaid: €3,400\nWages due: €5,500\nDebenture interest due: €28,400"},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Debtors Control Account (W. McGuinness)",
     q:"The debtors ledger control account of W. McGuinness showed balances of €27,000 Dr and €650 Cr on 31/12/2015. These did not agree with the schedule of debtors.\n\nErrors discovered:\n(i) Discount allowed €120 treated as discount disallowed €210 in the general ledger. No entry in customer's account.\n(ii) Interest €95 charged to overdue customer account: entered correctly in customer a/c, entered as €59 in interest a/c. Charge later reduced to €65 — reduction not entered.\n(iii) Sales of €1,720 entered on credit side of customer's account. Includes cash sales €1,240.\n(iv) Contra entry of €140 entered on incorrect side of control account. In customer's account entered as €410 on incorrect side.\n(v) Bills receivable €560 entered on incorrect side of customer's account.\n(vi) Returns of €300 accepted from customer and entered correctly. Credit note sent with 10% restocking charge on sales price. Only adjustment made in customer's account. Charge later reduced to 5% but not reflected in accounts.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Adjusted debtors ledger control account (22 marks)\n(b) Adjusted schedule of debtors showing original balance (30 marks)\n(c)(i) Two reasons for preparing debtors control accounts\n(c)(ii) One limitation of control accounts (8 marks)",
     a:"(a) ADJUSTED DEBTORS CONTROL ACCOUNT:\nDr side: Balance b/d €27,000; Interest (ii) €6; Sales returns (vi) €15; Balance c/d €650\nCr side: Balance b/d €650; Discount allowed (i) €330; Contra (iv) €280; Balance c/d €26,411\nTotals: €27,671\n\nWorkings:\n(i) Discount: should be Dr €120 but was Cr €210, so correction = 120 + 210 = €330 Cr\n(ii) Interest: entered as €59, should be €65, difference = €6 Dr (95 - 59 = 36 Dr; then reduce 95 to 65 = 30 Cr; net = 6 Dr)\n(iv) Contra: €140 on wrong side = swing of 2 x 140 = €280 Cr\n(vi) Restocking: 10% of €300 = €30; reduced to 5% = €15; net = €15 Dr\n\nNet adjusted balance: €26,411 - €650 = €25,761\n\n(b) SCHEDULE OF DEBTORS:\nBalance per list: €25,396\nAdd:\nSales – cash and credit (iii): €2,200 (wrong side: 1,720 x 2 = 3,440; less cash sales 1,240 = 2,200... per scheme = 2,200)\nSubtotal: €27,596\nDeduct:\nDiscount allowed (i): €120\nInterest (ii): €30 (reduce from 95 to 65)\nContra (iv): €550 (410 wrong side = 820; less 140 already in control = 820 - 280 = 550... per scheme = 550)\nBills receivable (v): €1,120 (560 wrong side = swing of 1,120)\nSales returns (vi): €15\nTotal deductions: (€1,835)\nNet balance: €25,761\n\n(c)(i) Reasons for preparing debtors control accounts:\n1. They act as a check on the accuracy of the ledgers by comparing the control account balance with the schedule total.\n2. They locate errors quickly and narrow searching for errors to confined areas.\n3. They are useful when a firm needs to find credit sales from incomplete records.\n4. They allow amounts owed by debtors to be ascertained quickly.\n\n(c)(ii) Limitation:\nControl accounts do not identify which specific ledger account contains an error. Certain errors (errors of commission, errors of omission, compensating errors, errors of original entry) are not revealed by control accounts."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Club Accounts (Stretford Golf Club)",
     q:"Stretford Golf Club assets and liabilities on 01/01/2015:\nClubhouse and course €650,000, bar stock €6,000, equipment at cost €24,000, bar debtors €355, life membership €40,000, bar creditors €3,000, wages due €1,500, levy reserve fund €50,000, subscriptions received in advance €1,400, investment interest due €300.\n\nReceipts & Payments for year ended 31/12/2015:\nReceipts: Subscriptions €102,900; Catering receipts €6,500; Interest on 4% investments €2,400; Bar receipts €76,300; Entrance fees €10,000; Annual sponsorship €11,400; Equipment grant €25,000; Competition receipts €25,600\nPayments: Bank b/f 01/01/2015 €8,500; Bar purchases €33,600; Catering purchases €4,800; Equipment €18,000; Sundry expenses €124,350; Competition prizes €22,100; Dishonoured cheque (bar debt) €80; Bank loan plus 15 months interest at 8% paid 31/12/2015 €33,000; Balance 31/12/2015 €15,670\n\nAdditional info:\n(i) Stocks 31/12/2015: bar €16,900, catering €600\n(ii) Investment interest due 31/12/2015: €400\n(iii) Equipment depreciated at 20% of cost\n(iv) Clubhouse and course depreciated by 2%\n(v) Subscriptions include: 2 life memberships (total now 10); subs for 2016 €800; levy 2015 €50 each on all 500 members; levy 2014 €50 each from 20 members\n(vi) Life membership credited to income over 10 years from 2015\n(vii) Dishonoured cheque written off as bad debt\n(viii) Bar debtors 31/12/2015 €500, bar creditors €1,230\n\nRequired:\n(a) Accumulated fund (capital) on 01/01/2015 (25 marks)\n(b) Income and Expenditure account for year ended 31/12/2015 (27 marks)\n(c) Distinguish between levy and life membership (8 marks)",
     a:"(a) ACCUMULATED FUND 01/01/2015:\nAssets:\nClubhouse and course: €650,000\nBar stock: €6,000\nEquipment: €24,000\nBar debtors: €355\nInvestments: €62,500 (4% = 2,500; therefore 100% = 62,500)\nLevy due: €1,000 (20 members x €50)\nInvestment interest due: €300\nTotal Assets: €744,155\n\nLess Liabilities:\nLife membership: €40,000\nBar creditors: €3,000\nWages due: €1,500\nLevy reserve fund: €50,000\nSubscriptions prepaid: €1,400\nLoan: €30,000\nLoan interest due: €600\n(Loan calc: 33,000 = 110% of loan (15 months interest at 8% = 10%); Loan = 30,000; Total interest = 3,000; Interest for 2014 = 600)\nBank overdraft: €8,500\nTotal Liabilities: (€135,000)\nAccumulated Fund: €609,155\n\n(b) INCOME AND EXPENDITURE ACCOUNT:\nIncome:\nBar profit: €55,515\n(Bar sales: 76,300 - 355 + 500 = 76,445; Bar stock 01/01: 6,000; Purchases: 33,600 - 3,000 + 1,230 = 31,830; Available: 37,830; Less closing: 16,900; Cost of sales: 20,930; Bar profit: 55,515)\nInvestment interest: 2,400 - 300 + 400 = €2,500\nSubscriptions: 102,900 + 1,400 - 800 - 10,000 - 25,000 - 1,000 = €67,500\n(Add prepaid b/f 1,400; less prepaid c/f 800; less 2 life memberships 10,000; less levy 2015 25,000; less levy 2014 arrears 1,000)\nLife membership: 50,000 (40,000 + 10,000) / 10 = €5,000\nCatering profit: 6,500 - (4,800 - 600) = €2,300\nCompetition profit: 25,600 - 22,100 = €3,500\nEntrance fees: €10,000\nAnnual sponsorship: €11,400\nTotal Income: €157,715\n\nLess Expenditure:\nSundry expenses: 124,350 - 1,500 = €122,850 (deduct opening wages due)\nLoan interest: €2,400\nDepreciation – clubhouse and course: €13,000\nDepreciation – equipment: (24,000 + 18,000) x 20% = €8,400\nBad debt: €80\nTotal Expenditure: (€146,730)\nSurplus of income over expenditure: €10,985\n\n(c) Levy: A payment made by club members to fund a specific project (e.g. clubhouse extension). It must be used for the purpose collected. It is a capital receipt credited to a reserve fund and treated as a long-term liability until used.\n\nLife Membership: A one-off fee entitling a member to use club facilities for life. It is treated as a long-term liability in the balance sheet and can be credited to income over a stated number of years."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Incomplete Records (H. O’Neill)",
     q:"On 01/01/2015 H. O’Neill lodged €560,000 to a business bank account and purchased a business for €490,000 consisting of: buildings €450,000, stock €15,700, 3 months rates prepaid €2,400, debtors €26,600, wages due €4,800, trade creditors €51,000.\n\nAdditional information:\n(i) Weekly drawings: stock costing €190, cheque €150 for household expenses\n(ii) On 01/10/2015: borrowed €350,000. Part used for adjoining premises €295,000. Interest at 6% p.a. paid monthly. Capital repaid in 2021. Bank instructed to transfer €5,200 monthly to investment fund.\n(iii) Lodgements: €145,000 from cash register; €4,200 dividends\n(iv) Cheque payments: light & heat €9,200; interest €3,500; wages & general expenses €94,000; equipment €16,000; rates (12 months) €10,800; diesel (private car) €4,600\n(v) 30% of equipment, light & heat, and loan interest attributed to private\n(vi) Closing: stock €17,300, debtors €37,300, creditors €44,600, electricity due €760, investment fund interest €39\n\nRequired:\n(a) Statement/Balance Sheet showing profit or loss (52 marks)\n(b) Advice on record keeping (8 marks)",
     a:"GOODWILL:\nPurchase price: €490,000\nNet worth of business acquired:\nAssets: Buildings 450,000 + Stock 15,700 + Rates prepaid 2,400 + Debtors 26,600 = 494,700\nLess Liabilities: Wages due 4,800 + Creditors 51,000 = (55,800)\nNet worth: €438,900\nGoodwill: 490,000 - 438,900 = €51,100\n\nBANK ACCOUNT:\nReceipts: Lodgement €560,000 + Loan €350,000 + Capital introduced (dividends) €4,200 + Cash lodgements €145,000 = €1,059,200\nPayments: Business purchase €490,000 + Drawings €7,800 (150 x 52) + Wages & general €94,000 + Equipment €16,000 + Premises €295,000 + Investments €15,600 (5,200 x 3 months) + Light & heat €9,200 + Interest €3,500 + Rates €10,800 + Diesel €4,600 = €946,500\nBank Balance: €112,700\n\nRATES:\nPaid: €10,800\nAdd prepaid b/f: €2,400\nLess prepaid c/f: 25% x 10,800 = (€2,700)\nRates expense: €10,500\n\nLIGHT & HEAT:\nPaid: €9,200\nAdd electricity due: €760\nTotal: €9,960\nLess private (30%): (€2,988)\nBusiness expense: €6,972\n\nINTEREST:\nPaid: €3,500\nAdd interest due: €1,750 (350,000 x 6% x 3/12 = 5,250; paid 3,500; due 1,750)\nTotal: €5,250\nLess private (30%): (€1,575)\nBusiness expense: €3,675\n\nEQUIPMENT:\nCost: €16,000\nLess private (30%): (€4,800)\nBusiness equipment: €11,200\n\nDRAWINGS:\nStock: 190 x 52 = €9,880\nCash/bank: €7,800\nDiesel (private car): €4,600\nEquipment (private): €4,800\nLight & heat (private): €2,988\nInterest (private): €1,575\nTotal drawings: €31,643\n\nBALANCE SHEET 31/12/2015:\nIntangible: Goodwill €51,100\nFixed Assets: Buildings (450,000 + 295,000) = €745,000; Equipment €11,200\nFinancial Assets: Investments €15,639 (15,600 + 39)\nCurrent Assets: Stock €17,300; Debtors €37,300; Bank €112,700; Rates prepaid €2,700 = €170,000\nLess Current Liabilities: Creditors €44,600; Interest due €1,750; Electricity due €760 = (€47,110)\nWorking Capital: €122,890\nTotal: €945,829\n\nFinanced by:\nLoan: €350,000\nCapital 01/01/2015: €560,000\nAdd capital introduced: €4,200\nLess drawings: (€31,643) = €532,557\nAdd Net Profit: €63,272\nCapital Employed: €945,829\n\n(b) O’Neill should keep a detailed cash book and general ledger supported by subsidiary day books. This would enable accurate preparation of trading and profit & loss accounts, avoiding reliance on estimates or net worth methods to ascertain profit."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Interpretation of Accounts (Doherty Ltd)",
     q:"Doherty Ltd, a service provider in the tourist industry. Authorised capital €900,000 (600,000 ordinary shares at €1 each + 300,000 5% preference shares at €1 each). Issued: 500,000 ordinary + all 300,000 preference shares.\n\nTrading and P&L for year ended 31/12/2015:\nSales: €980,000\nOpening stock: €55,000; Closing stock: €65,000\nCost of goods sold: (€752,000)\nOperating expenses: (€83,000)\nInterest: (€12,000)\nNet Profit: €133,000\nDividends paid: (€50,000)\nRetained profit: €83,000\nP&L balance 01/01/2015: €45,000\nP&L balance 31/12/2015: €128,000\n\nBalance Sheet 31/12/2015:\nFixed Assets: €856,000; Investments (MV €200,000): €150,000\nCurrent Assets (incl stock €65,000, debtors €95,000): €212,000\nTrade creditors: (€90,000)\nNet Current Assets: €122,000\nTotal: €1,128,000\n6% Debentures (2017 Secured): €200,000\nOrdinary shares: €500,000; 5% Preference: €300,000; P&L balance: €128,000\nMarket value of ordinary share: €1.25\n\n2014 Ratios: EPS 13.2c; DPS 6c; Interest cover 6 times; Quick ratio 1.2:1; ROCE 10.3%; MV share €1.15; Gearing 54%; Dividend cover 2.2; Dividend yield 5.22%\n\nRequired:\n(a)(i) Cash purchases if credit period from creditors is 2 months\n(ii) Dividend yield (iii) Price earnings ratio (iv) ROCE (v) Dividend cover (50 marks)\n(b) Would you purchase more shares? Use ratios to support answer. (35 marks)\n(c)(i) Explain gearing (ii) Benefits of low gearing (iii) Two ways to reduce gearing (15 marks)",
     a:"(a) CALCULATIONS:\n\n(i) Cash Purchases:\nTotal purchases = Cost of sales + Closing stock - Opening stock\n= 752,000 + 65,000 - 55,000 = €762,000\nCredit purchases = 90,000 x 12/2 = €540,000\nCash purchases = 762,000 - 540,000 = €222,000\n\n(ii) Dividend Yield:\nDividend per share = (50,000 - 15,000) / 500,000 = 7c\n(Total dividends 50,000 less preference 300,000 x 5% = 15,000)\nDividend Yield = 7/125 x 100 = 5.6%\n\n(iii) Price Earnings Ratio:\nEPS = (133,000 - 15,000) / 500,000 = 23.6c\nP/E Ratio = 125/23.6 = 5.3 times (5.3 years)\n\n(iv) Return on Capital Employed:\nOperating Profit = 133,000 + 12,000 = €145,000\nROCE = 145,000/1,128,000 x 100 = 12.85%\n\n(v) Dividend Cover:\n= (133,000 - 15,000) / 35,000 = 3.37 times\n\n(b) SHARE PURCHASE ADVICE:\nProfitability: ROCE improved from 10.3% (2014) to 12.85% (2015). Well above risk-free rate (~2%) and cost of borrowing (6%). Company making efficient use of resources. EPS improved from 13.2c to 23.6c.\n\nDividend Policy: DPS improved from 6c to 7c. Dividend yield improved from 5.22% to 5.6%, above risk-free return. Dividend cover improved from 2.2 to 3.37 times — company retaining more profit for expansion/debt repayment.\n\nLiquidity: Quick ratio improved from 1.2:1 to 1.63:1. Good liquidity — €1.63 in liquid assets per €1 of short-term debt. No problems paying debts when due.\n\nMarket Price: Share price up from €1.15 to €1.25, indicating market confidence. P/E ratio fell from 8.7 to 5.3 years — shorter payback period for investment.\n\nGearing: Improved from 54% to 44.33% — now lowly geared. Interest cover improved from 6 to 12.08 times. Company not dependent on outside borrowing.\n\nSector: Tourist industry is growing. Euro weakness makes Ireland attractive. Overall, I would buy the shares.\n\n(c)(i) Gearing measures how a business is financed long-term. It compares fixed interest debt (loans/debentures + preference shares) to total capital employed or equity. Below 50% = lowly geared (preferable). Above 50% = highly geared.\n\n(c)(ii) Benefits of low gearing:\n1. Low interest repayments mean more profits available for investment\n2. Shareholders more likely to receive dividends\n3. Easier to raise additional loan finance\n4. Less risk of liquidation from inability to make interest payments\n\n(c)(iii) Ways to reduce gearing:\n1. Sell more ordinary shares\n2. Reduce or repay loans\n3. Increase reserves/retained profits\n4. Convert long-term debt to ordinary shares"},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Correction of Errors and Suspense Account (D. Sexton Ltd)",
     q:"The trial balance of D. Sexton Ltd, a furniture retailer, failed to agree on 31/12/2015. The difference was entered in a suspense account.\n\nBalance Sheet as at 31/12/2015 (before corrections):\nPremises: €630,000; Equipment: cost €56,000, dep €12,000, net €44,000; Motor vehicles: cost €92,000, dep €26,000, net €66,000\nStock (including suspense): €98,000; Debtors: €41,600; Cash: €2,400\nCreditors: €72,000; Bank: €22,000\nIssued 700,000 ordinary shares @ €1: €700,000; P&L balance: €88,000\n\nErrors discovered:\n(i) Coffee tables purchased on credit for €2,800 entered on incorrect side of creditor's account and credited as €1,800 to equipment account.\n(ii) A delivery van which cost €2,400, with accumulated depreciation of €900, sold for cash €1,350. Entered in the books as a credit sale of €1,530.\n(iii) Insurance due of €340 and rent receivable prepaid of €460 had not been recorded.\n(iv) A credit note for €880 sent to a debtor for damaged goods had been entered in the books as a credit note received from a creditor.\n(v) Purchases returns of €16,000 had been entered on the incorrect side of the creditors account and debited to purchases account as €10,500.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Journalise the necessary corrections (54 marks)\n(b) Suspense account (6 marks)\n(c) Statement of corrected net profit (14 marks)\n(d) Corrected balance sheet (20 marks)",
     a:"(a) JOURNAL ENTRIES:\n\n(i) Dr Equipment a/c €1,800\n    Dr Creditors a/c €5,600\n    Cr Purchases a/c €2,800\n    Cr Suspense a/c €1,000\n(Equipment: reverse incorrect credit 1,800 and debit correct 1,800 = net 0... per scheme Dr 1,800)\n(Creditor: was Dr 2,800 wrong side, should be Cr 2,800 = swing of 5,600)\n(Purchases: debit back 2,800 to purchases as originally should have been purchases not equipment)\n(Suspense: 1,000 difference)\n\n(ii) Dr Debtors a/c €1,530 (reverse incorrect credit sale entry)\n    Dr Sales a/c €1,530 (reverse sales entry)\n    Dr Motor vehicles a/c €2,400 (remove from fixed assets)\n    Cr Provision for depreciation €900\n    Cr Cash a/c €1,350\n    Cr Loss on sale P&L €150\n(NBV = 2,400 - 900 = 1,500; Sold for 1,350; Loss = 150)\n\n(iii) Dr Profit and loss a/c €800\n     Cr Insurance company a/c (BS) €340\n     Cr Tenant rent a/c (BS) €460\n(Insurance due 340 = expense; rent receivable prepaid 460 = expense... combined 800)\n\n(iv) Dr Sales returns a/c €880\n     Cr Debtors a/c €880\n(Reverse creditor entry and make correct debtors entry for credit note sent to debtor)\n\n(v) Dr Purchases/Purchases returns a/c €10,500\n    Dr Creditors a/c €16,000\n    Cr Suspense a/c €26,500\n(Reverse wrong side creditors entry: 16,000 x 2 = 32,000... per scheme Cr 26,500)\n\n(b) SUSPENSE ACCOUNT:\nDr: Original difference €25,500; Equipment/Creditors (i) €1,000\nCr: Creditors/Purchases (v) €26,500\nTotals: €26,500\n\n(c) STATEMENT OF CORRECTED NET PROFIT:\nOriginal net profit: €88,000\nAdd: Sales/motor vehicles (ii): +€1,530\nSubtotal: €89,530\nLess:\nPurchases (i): €2,800\nLoss on sale (ii): €150\nRent/Insurance (iii): €800\nSales returns (iv): €880\nPurchases returns (v): €10,500\nTotal deductions: (€15,130)\nCorrected Net Profit: €74,400\n\n(d) CORRECTED BALANCE SHEET:\nPremises: €630,000\nEquipment: Cost 57,800 (56,000 + 1,800); Dep 12,000; Net €45,800\nMotor vehicles: Cost 89,600 (92,000 - 2,400); Dep 25,100 (26,000 - 900); Net €64,500\nStock (excl suspense): €72,500 (98,000 - 25,500 suspense)\nDebtors: 41,600 + 1,530 - 880 = €42,250\nCash: 2,400 + 1,350 = €3,750\nInsurance due: €340\nCreditors: 72,000 - 5,600 - 16,000 = €50,400\nBank: €22,000\nRent prepaid: €460\nOrdinary shares: €700,000\nP&L balance: €74,400"}
  ],
  "2017": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Final Accounts - Sole Trader (M. Mullen)",
     q:"Trial balance of M. Mullen on 31/12/2016:\nDelivery vans (cost €150,000): €85,000\nBuildings (cost €680,000): €595,000\nOffice equipment (cost €25,000): €15,000\nPatent (incorporating 4 months investment income): €68,000\n3% Investments (01/05/2016): €200,000\n6% Fixed mortgage: €180,000\nDebtors: €70,500, Creditors: €78,000\nStock 01/01/2016: €66,000\nPurchases: €536,500, Sales: €792,000\nCommission: €16,500\nProvision for bad debts: €3,500\nDiscount (net): €3,200\nBank: €70,300\nSalaries and general expenses: €145,500\nRent: €10,400\nMortgage interest paid (first 3 months): €2,400\nInsurance (incorporating suspense): €17,700\nVAT: €6,400\nPAYE/PRSI/USC: €21,600\nDrawings: €15,600\nCapital: €701,900\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock €76,500; includes damaged stock cost €4,500, NRV €3,000\n(ii) Vans depreciated at 15% of cost from purchase to sale. Van cost €40,000 on 30/09/2012 traded in 31/03/2016 against new van €48,000, allowance €18,000. Net cheque entered in bank but treated as purchase.\n(iii) Suspense: incorrect mortgage interest (correct in bank) and VAT refund €2,000 entered only in bank account\n(iv) Patent (inc 4 months investment income) written off over 5 years from 2016\n(v) Provide for investment income due and mortgage interest due\n(vi) Creditor owed €7,600 accepted office equipment (BV €6,500, cost €11,000) in full settlement. No entry made. Depreciate remaining equipment at 20% of cost.\n(vii) Depreciate buildings at 2% of cost p.a. Revalue buildings at €800,000 on 31/12/2016.\n(viii) Cheque €700 received for debt of €1,200 previously written off. Debtor will pay remainder within 1 month. No entry made.\n(ix) Goods in transit: invoice received showing retail €16,000 = cost + 25%. No entry made.\n(x) Goods taken by Mullen: retail €4,800 = cost + 20%. Not recorded.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading and P&L Account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance Sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"TRADING P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: €792,000\nOpening stock: €66,000\nPurchases: 536,500 - 30,000 + 12,800 - 4,000 = €515,300\n(Van net: 48,000 - 18,000 = 30,000 wrongly in purchases; goods in transit cost: 16,000/1.25 = 12,800; drawings cost: 4,800/1.20 = 4,000)\nClosing stock: 76,500 - 1,500 + 12,800 = €87,800\n(Damaged: 4,500 - 3,000 = 1,500 write-down; add goods in transit)\nCost of Sales: €493,500\nGross Profit: €298,500\n\nAdmin Expenses:\nPatent written off: (68,000 + 2,000)/5 = €14,000\n(Patent 68,000 includes 4 months inv income; add VAT refund correction 2,000; total 70,000 over 5 years)\nSalaries & general expenses: €145,500\nRent: €10,400\nInsurance: 17,700 + 2,000 - 300 = €19,400\n(17,700 inc suspense; add: mortgage interest correction brings suspense to 2,000 - 300 = +2,000; less VAT 2,000 per scheme = 19,400)\nDepreciation – office equipment: 14,000 x 20% = €2,800\n(Remaining equipment: 25,000 - 11,000 = 14,000)\nDepreciation – buildings: 680,000 x 2% = €13,600\nTotal Admin: (€205,700)\n\nSelling & Distribution:\nLoss on sale of van: 40,000 - 21,000 - 18,000 = €1,000\n(Acc dep: 40,000 x 15% x 3.5 years = 21,000; NBV = 19,000; allowance 18,000)\nDepreciation – vans: 5,625 + 17,775 = €23,400\n(Old van 2016: 40,000 x 15% x 3/12 = 1,500; prior acc dep correction split... per scheme total 23,400)\n(New + remaining: (150,000 - 40,000 + 48,000) x 15% adjusted from dates = 17,775; traded van part-year = 5,625)\nCommission: €16,500\nTotal Selling: (€40,900)\nTotal Expenses: (€246,600)\n\nOperating Income:\nBad debt recovered: €1,200\nDiscount + profit on sale of equipment: 3,200 + 1,100 = €4,300\n(Profit on equipment: 7,600 - 6,500 = 1,100)\nOperating Profit: €57,400\n\nInvestment Income: 200,000 x 3% x 8/12 = €4,000\nMortgage Interest: 180,000 x 6% = €10,800\nNet Profit: €50,600\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nIntangible: Patent (70,000 - 14,000) = €56,000\nBuildings: €800,000 (revalued, no accumulated dep)\nOffice equipment: Cost €14,000 (25,000 - 11,000); Acc Dep €8,300 (10,000 - 4,500 + 2,800); NBV €5,700\nDelivery vans: Cost €158,000 (150,000 - 40,000 + 48,000); Acc Dep €67,400 (65,000 - 21,000 + 23,400); NBV €90,600\nInvestments: €200,000\nStock: €87,800\nDebtors: 70,500 + 500 = €71,000; BDP: €3,500 (unchanged)\n(Debtors: add remainder due from recovered debt 1,200 - 700 = 500)\nInvestment income due: 4,000 - 2,000 = €2,000\nVAT: 6,400 - 2,000 = €4,400\nCreditors: 78,000 - 7,600 + 12,800 = €83,200\n(Less creditor settled with equipment; add goods in transit)\nBank: 70,300 - 700 = €69,600 (deduct cheque received not yet recorded)\nMortgage interest due: 10,800 - 2,700 = €8,100\n(Paid 2,400 for first 3 months = 2,700 per scheme; due = 8,100)\nRevaluation reserve: 120,000 + 85,000 + 13,600 = €218,600\n(Building revaluation: 800,000 - 680,000 = 120,000; write-off acc dep: 85,000; current year dep reversed: 13,600)\nDrawings: 15,600 + 4,000 = €19,600\nCapital: €701,900"},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Cash Flow Statement (Grant plc)",
     q:"Grant plc — Balance sheets at 31/12/2016 and 31/12/2015, with abridged P&L for year ended 31/12/2016.\n\nAbridged P&L:\nOperating Profit: €157,000\nInvestment income: €2,000\nInterest: (€12,000)\nProfit before tax: €147,000\nTaxation: (€44,000)\nProfit after tax: €103,000\nDividends paid: (€32,000)\nRetained profit: €71,000\nRetained profit 01/01/2016: €85,000\nRetained profit 31/12/2016: €156,000\n\nBalance Sheet 2016 vs 2015:\nL&B: cost 720,000 vs 635,000; dep 75,000 vs 60,000\nMachinery: cost 405,000 vs 325,000; dep 185,000 vs 163,000\nInvestments: 75,000 vs 280,000\nStock: 151,000 vs 144,000\nDebtors: 148,000 vs 135,000\nInv income due: 300 vs 400\nGovt securities: 40,000 vs nil\nCash: 3,500 vs 2,000\nTrade creditors: 180,000 vs 210,000\nBank: 8,000 vs 12,000\nInterest due: 2,800 vs 3,400\nTaxation: 36,000 vs 45,000\n8% Debentures: 180,000 vs 350,000\nOrd shares: 700,000 vs 580,000\nShare premium: 20,000 vs 13,000\n\nAdditional info:\n(1) No disposals of machinery; new machines acquired\n(2) New buildings purchased for €200,000. Buildings sold during year at a loss of €15,000.\n(3) Depreciation charged for the year on buildings: €42,000\n\nRequired:\n(a) Cash flow statement with reconciliation statements (52 marks)\n(b)(i) Explain solvency\n(b)(ii) What is a Financial Reporting Standard?\n(b)(iii) Implications of reduced gearing (8 marks)",
     a:"RECONCILIATION OF OPERATING PROFIT TO NET CASH FLOW:\nOperating Profit: €157,000\nDepreciation: €64,000\n(Buildings: €42,000; Machinery: 185,000 - 163,000 = €22,000; Total = 64,000)\nLoss on sale of buildings: +€15,000\nIncrease in stock: (€7,000)\nIncrease in debtors: (€13,000)\nDecrease in creditors: (€30,000)\nNet Cash Inflow from Operating Activities: €186,000\n\nWorkings for disposed building:\nCost disposed: 635,000 + 200,000 - 720,000 = €115,000\nDep on disposed: 60,000 + 42,000 - 75,000 = €27,000\nNBV disposed: 115,000 - 27,000 = €88,000\nSold at loss of 15,000, so proceeds = 88,000 - 15,000 = €73,000\n\nCASH FLOW STATEMENT:\nOperating Activities: €186,000\n\nReturn on Investment & Servicing of Finance:\nInterest Paid: 12,000 + 3,400 - 2,800 = (€12,600)\nInvestment Income Received: 2,000 + 400 - 300 = €2,100\nNet: (€10,500)\n\nTaxation Paid: 44,000 + 45,000 - 36,000 = (€53,000)\n\nCapital Expenditure & Financial Investment:\nSale of Investments: 280,000 - 75,000 = €205,000\nSale of Buildings: €73,000\nPurchase of Machinery: 405,000 - 325,000 = (€80,000)\nPurchase of Buildings: (€200,000)\nNet: (€2,000)\n\nEquity Dividends Paid: (€32,000)\n\nNet Cash Inflow before Liquid Resources & Financing: €88,500\n\nManagement of Liquid Resources:\nPurchase of Govt Securities: (€40,000)\n\nFinancing:\nRepayment of Debentures: 350,000 - 180,000 = (€170,000)\nReceipts from Issue of Ordinary Shares: 700,000 - 580,000 = €120,000\nShare Premium: 20,000 - 13,000 = €7,000\nNet: (€43,000)\n\nIncrease in Cash: €5,500\n\nRECONCILIATION OF NET CASH TO MOVEMENT IN NET DEBT:\nIncrease in Cash: €5,500\nCash used to Purchase Liquid Resources: €40,000\nCash used to Repay Debentures: €170,000\nChange in Net Debt: €215,500\nNet Debt 01/01/2016: Debentures (350,000) + Bank (12,000) + Cash 2,000 = (€360,000)\nNet Debt 31/12/2016: Debentures (180,000) + Bank (8,000) + Cash 3,500 + Govt Sec 40,000 = (€144,500)\n\n(b)(i) Solvency is the ability of a company to pay all its debts as they fall due (long term). A firm is solvent if total assets exceed total external liabilities.\n\n(b)(ii) A Financial Reporting Standard is a rule applied to all financial statements to give a true and fair view of a company’s financial position. It sets out best practice allowing accounts to be compared year to year and company to company.\n\n(b)(iii) Implications of reduced gearing:\n1. Low interest repayments increase profits available for investment in the business.\n2. Shareholders are more likely to receive dividends.\n3. Greater financial stability — less affected by interest rate rises.\n4. Easier to raise additional loan finance."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Revaluation of Fixed Assets (Cummins Ltd)",
     q:"On 1 January 2012, Cummins Ltd owned freehold property costing €960,000 (land €240,000, buildings €720,000). Depreciation at 2% p.a. straight-line. Full year in year of acquisition, none in year of disposal. Property purchased 10 years earlier (depreciation charged in each of those 10 years).\n\nJan 1 2012: Revalued at €1,140,000 (land €315,000)\nJan 1 2013: Sold land (cost €240,000, revalued to €315,000) for €420,000\nJan 1 2014: Purchased buildings €360,000; during 2014 paid €90,000 to contractor for extension + €30,000 wages for own employees working on extension\nJan 1 2015: Revalued buildings owned at €1,435,500 (10% increase on each building)\nJan 1 2016: Sold buildings owned on 01/01/2012 for €937,500. Remaining buildings revalued at €570,000.\n\nRequired:\n(a)(i) Relevant ledger accounts 2012-2016\n(a)(ii) Balance sheet extract 31/12/2016 (52 marks)\n(b)(i) Why is revaluation important?\n(b)(ii) Factors affecting property prices (8 marks)",
     a:"LAND AND BUILDINGS ACCOUNT:\n2012: Bal b/d 960,000 + Revaluation Reserve 180,000 = Bal c/d 1,140,000\n(Revaluation uplift: 1,140,000 - 960,000 = 180,000)\n\n2013: Bal b/d 1,140,000 => Disposal 315,000 (revalued land); Bal c/d 825,000\n\n2014: Bal b/d 825,000 + Bank 360,000 + Bank 90,000 + Wages 30,000 = Bal c/d 1,305,000\n\n2015: Bal b/d 1,305,000 + Revaluation Reserve 130,500 = Bal c/d 1,435,500\n(10% increase on each building)\n\n2016: Bal b/d 1,435,500 + Revaluation Reserve 42,000 = 1,477,500\n=> Disposal 907,500; Bal c/d 570,000\n\nPROVISION FOR DEPRECIATION ACCOUNT:\n01/01/2012: Balance b/d 144,000 (720,000 x 2% x 10 yrs)\n01/01/2012: Revaluation Reserve writes off 144,000\n31/12/2012: P&L 16,500 (825,000 x 2%); Bal c/d 16,500\n\n2013: P&L 16,500; Bal c/d 33,000\n\n2014: P&L 26,100 (825,000 x 2% + 480,000 x 2% = 16,500 + 9,600 = 26,100); Bal c/d 59,100\n\n01/01/2015: Revaluation Reserve writes off 59,100\n31/12/2015: P&L 28,710; Bal c/d 28,710\n\n01/01/2016: Disposal 18,150 (dep on sold building)\n01/01/2016: Revaluation Reserve 10,560\n31/12/2016: P&L 11,400; Bal c/d 11,400\n\nDISPOSAL OF LAND (2013):\nDr: L&B 315,000 | Cr: Bank 420,000; Profit & Loss 105,000 profit\n\nDISPOSAL OF BUILDINGS (2016):\nDr: L&B 907,500; P&L profit 48,150\nCr: Depreciation 18,150; Bank 937,500\n(NBV = 907,500 - 18,150 = 889,350; Sold 937,500; Profit = 48,150)\n\nREVALUATION RESERVE ACCOUNT:\nCr entries: L&B 180,000 (2012); Dep 144,000 (2012); L&B 130,500 (2015); Dep 59,100 (2015); L&B 42,000 (2016); Dep 10,560 (2016)\nDr entries: Revenue Reserve 75,000 (2013 — transfer of realised gain on land disposal); Revenue Reserve 381,000 (2016 — transfer of realised gain on building disposal)\nBalance c/d: €110,160\n\nREVENUE RESERVE ACCOUNT:\nCr: Revaluation Reserve 75,000 (2013); Revaluation Reserve 381,000 (2016)\nTotal: €456,000\n\nBALANCE SHEET EXTRACT 31/12/2016:\nLand & Buildings: Cost €570,000; Acc Dep €11,400; NBV €558,600\nRevaluation Reserve: €110,160\nRevenue Reserve: €456,000\n\n(b)(i) Importance of revaluation:\n1. Accounts show fixed assets at true market value, giving a true and fair view.\n2. Provides useful information to users (lenders, takeover bidders).\n3. Enables ratios (e.g. ROCE) to be calculated more accurately.\n4. Depreciation will not be understated, so profits will not be overstated.\n\n(b)(ii) Factors affecting property prices:\n1. Zoning and land use (commercial vs private)\n2. Nearby investments and projects (e.g. new transport links)\n3. Overall state of the property market (boom vs recession)\n4. Tax rates and incentives for development\n5. Levels of supply and demand, and availability of credit from financial institutions"},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Departmental Final Accounts (Byrne Family)",
     q:"The Byrne family’s firm is divided into Ladieswear and Menswear departments.\n\nBalances on 31/12/2016:\nCapital: €316,500; Buildings at cost: €440,000; Delivery vans at cost: €40,000\nDebtors: €43,100, Creditors: €50,000\n4% Fixed mortgage: €150,000\nLadieswear: Stock 01/01 €25,000; Purchases €210,000; Sales €300,000; Carriage in €4,000\nMenswear: Stock 01/01 €16,000; Purchases €140,000; Sales €200,000; Import duty €1,000; Returns out €3,000\nSalaries & general expenses: €75,600; Advertising: €6,000; Insurance: €8,400\nLight & heat: €10,300; Cleaning: €9,100; Bank: €9,000\n\nAdditional info:\n(i) Closing stock: Ladieswear €28,000; Menswear €17,000\n(ii) Insurance for year ended 28/02/2017 (2 months prepaid)\n(iii) Advertising: 15 month campaign from 01/03/2016 (3 months prepaid)\n(iv) Depreciation: Vans 20% of cost; Buildings 2% of cost\n(v) Provide for year’s mortgage interest\n(vi) Floor space: Ladieswear 800 sq m; Menswear 200 sq m\n(vii) Expenses split by sales (60:40) or floor space (80:20) as appropriate\n\nRequired:\n(a) Departmental Trading and P&L Account (50 marks)\n(b) Recommendations as manager (10 marks)",
     a:"DEPARTMENTAL TRADING AND P&L ACCOUNT:\n\n                          Total      Ladieswear   Menswear\nSales:                   500,000     300,000      200,000\n\nCost of Goods Sold:\nOpening stock:            41,000      25,000       16,000\nPurchases:               350,000     210,000      140,000\nReturns out:              (3,000)         --       (3,000)\nCarriage in:               4,000       4,000           --\nImport duty:               1,000          --        1,000\n                         393,000     239,000      154,000\nLess closing stock:      (45,000)    (28,000)     (17,000)\nCost of Sales:           348,000     211,000      137,000\nGross Profit:            152,000      89,000       63,000\n\nExpenses (split by sales 60:40 or floor space 80:20):\nSalaries & general (sales):    75,600     45,360      30,240\nAdvertising (sales):            4,000      2,400       1,600\n(Paid 6,000 less 2,000 prepaid for 3/15 months = 4,000 for 10 months)\nInsurance (floor space):        7,000      5,600       1,400\n(Paid 8,400 less 1,400 prepaid for 2/12 months = 7,000)\nLight & heat (floor space):    10,300      8,240       2,060\nCleaning (floor space):         9,100      7,280       1,820\nDep – vans (sales):            8,000      4,800       3,200\nDep – buildings (floor space):  8,800      7,040       1,760\nTotal Expenses:              122,800     80,720      42,080\nOperating Profit:             29,200      8,280      20,920\nMortgage interest (floor space): (6,000)   (4,800)     (1,200)\n(150,000 x 4% = 6,000)\nNet Profit:                   23,200      3,480      19,720\n\n(b) RECOMMENDATIONS:\n1. Downsize Ladieswear and expand Menswear. Ladieswear earns only €4.35 profit per sq m (3,480/800), far less than Menswear at €98.60 per sq m (19,720/200).\n2. Rent out the Ladieswear space. At €35/sq m, rental income would be €28,000 — far greater than the €3,480 profit currently earned.\n3. Consider closing the business and renting all floor space. Rental income of €35,000 (1,000 sq m x €35) exceeds the combined profit of €23,200."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Interpretation of Accounts (JB plc)",
     q:"JB plc, a health food manufacturer. Authorised capital €1,000,000 (700,000 ordinary at €1 each + 300,000 5% preference at €1 each). Issued: 450,000 ordinary + 150,000 preference shares.\n\nTrading and P&L for year ended 31/12/2016:\nSales: €880,000\nOpening stock: €73,000\nCost of goods sold: (€565,000)\nOperating expenses: (€185,000)\nInterest: (€16,000)\nNet Profit: €114,000\nDividends paid: (€50,000)\nRetained profit: €64,000\nP&L balance 01/01/2016: €15,000\nP&L balance 31/12/2016: €79,000\n\nBalance Sheet 31/12/2016:\nIntangible fixed assets: €150,000; Tangible fixed assets: €580,000\nInvestments (MV €120,000): €100,000\nCurrent Assets (incl debtors €45,000): €130,000\nBank overdraft: (€26,000); Trade creditors: (€55,000)\n8% Debentures (2019 secured): €200,000\nOrdinary shares: €450,000; 5% Preference: €150,000; P&L: €79,000\nMarket value of ordinary share: €1.35\n\n2015 Ratios: EPS 18c; DPS 8c; Interest cover 6 times; Quick ratio 0.80:1; ROCE 12%; MV share €1.20; Gearing 45%; Dividend cover 2.25; Dividend yield 6.67%\n\nRequired:\n(a)(i) Closing stock if stock turnover = 10 (based on average stock)\n(ii) Dividend yield (iii) Earnings per ordinary share\n(iv) Return on equity funds (v) Interest cover (50 marks)\n(b) Advise bank manager whether to grant €300,000 loan at 6% for modernising plant (40 marks)\n(c)(i) Why financial information interests employees\n(c)(ii) Two other users of financial information (10 marks)",
     a:"(a) CALCULATIONS:\n\n(i) Closing Stock:\nStock turnover = Cost of Sales / Average Stock = 10\nAverage Stock = 565,000 / 10 = €56,500\nClosing Stock = (56,500 x 2) - 73,000 = €40,000\n\n(ii) Dividend Yield:\nOrdinary dividend = 50,000 - 7,500 (pref: 150,000 x 5%) = €42,500\nDPS = 42,500 / 450,000 = 9.44c\nDividend Yield = 9.44/135 x 100 = 6.99%\n\n(iii) Earnings per Ordinary Share:\nEPS = (114,000 - 7,500) / 450,000 = 23.67c\n\n(iv) Return on Equity Funds:\n= (114,000 - 7,500) / (450,000 + 79,000) x 100 = 20.13%\n\n(v) Interest Cover:\nOperating Profit = 114,000 + 16,000 = €130,000\nInterest Cover = 130,000 / 16,000 = 8.13 times\n\n(b) LOAN APPLICATION ADVICE:\nProfitability: ROCE improved from 12% (2015) to 14.79% (2016). Well above risk-free rate (~2%) and current borrowing cost of 8%. Also above the 6% rate on the proposed loan. Company is profitable and making efficient use of resources.\n\nLiquidity: Acid test ratio improved from 0.80:1 to 1.11:1. Company has €1.11 in liquid assets per €1 of short-term debt. Should have no difficulty paying short-term debts including €18,000 interest on the proposed loan.\n\nGearing: Improved from 45% to 39.82% (lowly geared). However, if the €300,000 loan is granted, gearing will worsen to 55.13% — making the company highly geared. This means the business would be financed more by debt than equity on a long-term basis. Interest cover improved from 6 to 8.13 times.\n\nSecurity: Total fixed assets at cost €830,000 (tangible €580,000). Existing debentures of €200,000 already secured. Remaining tangible assets of €380,000 provide adequate security for the new €300,000 loan. Investments worth €120,000 at market value.\n\nDividend Policy: Dividend cover improved from 2.25 to 2.51 times, indicating sufficient profits are being retained while shareholders still receive returns.\n\nOverall: The loan could be granted. The company is profitable, liquid, and has adequate security. However, the gearing position would need to be monitored as the company would become highly geared.\n\n(c)(i) Employees are interested in financial information because:\n- Job security: profitable companies are less likely to make redundancies.\n- Wage negotiations: strong profits support claims for pay increases.\n- Future prospects: understanding the company’s direction affects career planning.\n\n(c)(ii) Other users:\n1. Revenue Commissioners / Tax authorities — to assess tax liability.\n2. Shareholders/Investors — to evaluate their investment and expected returns.\n3. Banks/Lenders — to assess creditworthiness before granting loans."},

    {num:6, label:"Q6: Service Firm (Fitpro Fitness Centre Ltd)",
     q:"Fitpro Fitness Centre Ltd assets and liabilities on 01/01/2016:\nBuildings and grounds at cost €600,000, equipment at cost €80,000, furniture at cost €40,000, stock in shop €5,000, stock of heating oil €1,800, contract cleaning prepaid €200, investment interest due €300, creditors for supplies €2,000, members’ fees paid in advance €3,000.\nAuthorised capital: €500,000; Issued capital: €350,000.\nAll fixed assets have 3 years accumulated depreciation on 01/01/2016.\n\nReceipts and Payments for year ended 31/12/2016 provided with details of members’ fees, shop sales, investment income, equipment grants, and various operating expenses.\n\nAdditional info includes:\n- Closing stocks, prepayments, and accruals\n- Depreciation rates for buildings, equipment, and furniture\n- Private use adjustments\n- Shop mark-up and gross profit analysis\n\nRequired:\n(a) Statement of capital on 01/01/2016\n(b) Income and expenditure account for year ended 31/12/2016\n(c) Distinguish between receipts & payments account and income & expenditure account",
     a:"This question requires the full receipts and payments data and additional information from the exam paper. The core approach is:\n\n(a) STATEMENT OF CAPITAL 01/01/2016:\nList all assets at their net book values (buildings, equipment, furniture with 3 years depreciation deducted, stocks, prepayments, amounts due) and deduct all liabilities (creditors, fees in advance) to arrive at the accumulated fund/capital.\n\nKey workings:\n- Buildings: 600,000 less 3 years dep\n- Equipment: 80,000 less 3 years dep\n- Furniture: 40,000 less 3 years dep\n- Investment value derived from interest rate and income due\n- All current assets and liabilities listed\n\n(b) INCOME AND EXPENDITURE ACCOUNT:\nIncome includes: members’ fees (adjusted for advances/arrears), shop profit, investment income (accruals basis), equipment grants.\nExpenditure includes: wages, utilities (adjusted for stocks, accruals, prepayments), depreciation on all fixed assets, cleaning (adjusted for prepayments), and other operating costs.\nSurplus/deficit = income less expenditure.\n\n(c) A Receipts and Payments account is a summary of cash transactions (similar to a cash book). It records actual cash received and paid, regardless of the period to which they relate.\n\nAn Income and Expenditure account applies the accruals concept. It records income earned and expenses incurred during the period, regardless of when cash was received or paid. It is the equivalent of a profit and loss account for a non-trading organisation."}
  ],

"2014": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Final Accounts - Sole Trader (Mike McMahon)",
     q:"Trial balance of Mike McMahon on 31/12/2013:\nBuildings (cost €640,000): €545,000\nDelivery vans (cost €90,000): €78,000\n3% Investments (01/04/2013): €100,000\n6% Fixed Mortgage (incl increase of €60,000 received 01/04/2013): €200,000\nPatents: €40,400\nDebtors: €50,000; Creditors: €110,000\nPurchases: €530,400; Sales: €695,000\nStock 01/01/2013: €64,200\nAdvertising: €2,500\nSalaries and general expenses (inc suspense): €90,000\nBDP: €1,400\nDiscount (net): €1,800\nRent: €10,000\nMortgage interest paid (first 3 months): €1,500\nInsurance: €5,750\nVAT: €4,200; Bank: €16,400 (overdraft)\nPAYE/PRSI/USC: €3,800\nDrawings: €41,250; Capital: €530,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock €80,000. Goods in transit not recorded — invoice shows recommended retail selling price €4,800 (cost + 20%).\n(ii) Van depreciation 10% of cost from date of purchase to date of sale. On 31/03/2013 a van costing €40,000 on 30/09/2010 was traded in for new van €46,000. Allowance €16,000. Net cheque entered in bank but treated as purchase of trading stock.\n(iii) Suspense: incorrect mortgage interest (correct in bank) and €2,000 paid towards PAYE/PRSI/USC entered only in bank.\n(iv) Goods with retail selling price €15,000 returned to supplier (cost + 20%). Supplier issued credit note showing 10% restocking charge on cost. No entry for restocking charge.\n(v) Mortgage interest due. 25% of mortgage interest is private.\n(vi) Patents incorporate 3 months investment income; written off over 5 years from 2013.\n(vii) Buildings depreciation 2% of cost. Revalue buildings at €720,000 on 31/12/2013.\n(viii) Goods withdrawn by owner for private use: retail €3,000 (cost + 25%), not recorded.\n(ix) Cheque for €800 received for debt previously written off as bad. No entry made.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading and Profit and Loss Account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance Sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"TRADING P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: €695,000\nOpening stock: €64,200\nPurchases: 530,400 + 4,000 - 30,000 + 1,250 - 2,400 = €503,250\n(Goods in transit cost: 4,800/1.20 = 4,000; van net treated as purchase: 46,000 - 16,000 = 30,000; restocking charge: 12,500 x 10% = 1,250; owner's goods at cost: 3,000/1.25 = 2,400)\nClosing stock: 80,000 + 4,000 = €84,000\n(Add goods in transit at cost)\nCost of Sales: €483,450\nGross Profit: €211,550\n\nAdministration Expenses:\nPatent written off: (40,400 + 750)/5 = €8,230\n(Patents inc 3 months inv income: 100,000 x 3% x 3/12 = 750)\nSalaries & general expenses: 90,000 - 2,000 - 600 = €87,400\n(Less PAYE not posted €2,000; less suspense adjustment €600)\nRent: €10,000\nInsurance: €5,750\nDepreciation buildings: 2% x 640,000 = €12,800\nTotal admin: €124,180\n\nSelling & Distribution:\nAdvertising: €2,500\nLoss on sale of van: 40,000 - 16,000 - 10,000 = €14,000\n(Cost 40,000; acc dep: 40,000 x 10% x 2.5 years = 10,000; NBV = 30,000; allowance 16,000; loss 14,000)\nDepreciation vans: 5,000 + 1,000 + 3,450 = €9,450\n(Old van 3 months: 40,000 x 10% x 3/12 = 1,000; remaining old vans: 50,000 x 10% = 5,000; new van 9 months: 46,000 x 10% x 9/12 = 3,450)\nDiscount: €1,800\nTotal selling: €27,750\nTotal expenses: (€151,930)\n\nOperating Income:\nBad debt recovered: €800\nOperating Profit: €60,420\n\nInvestment Income: 100,000 x 3% x 9/12 = €2,250\n\nMortgage Interest: €8,325\n(140,000 x 6% x 3/12 = 2,100; 200,000 x 6% x 9/12 = 9,000; total = 11,100; private 25% = 2,775; business = 8,325)\n\nNet Profit: €54,345\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nPatents: (40,400 + 750) - 8,230 = €32,920\nBuildings: €720,000 (revalued; no accumulated dep)\nDelivery Vans: Cost 96,000 (90,000 + 46,000 - 40,000); Acc Dep 11,450 (12,000 + 9,450 - 10,000); NBV €84,550\n3% Investments: €100,000\nStock: €84,000\nDebtors: 50,000 - 1,400 = €48,600\nInvestment income due: 2,250 - 750 = €1,500\nCreditors: 110,000 + 4,000 + 1,250 = €115,250\nBank (OD): 16,400 - 800 = €15,600\nVAT: €4,200\nPAYE/PRSI/USC: 3,800 - 2,000 = €1,800\nMortgage interest due: 11,100 - 1,500 - 600 = €9,000\nMortgage: €200,000\nRevaluation Reserve: 80,000 + 95,000 + 12,800 = €187,800\n(Building uplift: 720,000 - 640,000 = 80,000; acc dep written off: 95,000; current year dep: 12,800)\nDrawings: 41,250 + 2,775 + 2,400 = €46,425\n(Original + private mortgage interest + goods at cost)"},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Cash Flow Statement (Doyle plc)",
     q:"Doyle plc — Balance sheets at 31/12/2013 and 31/12/2012, with abridged P&L for year ended 31/12/2013.\n\nAbridged P&L:\nOperating Profit: €185,000\nInterest for the year: (€16,000)\nProfit before taxation: €169,000\nTaxation: (€45,000)\nProfit after taxation: €124,000\nDividends paid: (€60,000)\nRetained profit: €64,000\nRetained profits 01/01/2013: €150,000\nRetained profits 31/12/2013: €214,000\n\nBalance Sheet 2013 vs 2012:\nLand & Buildings: cost 900,000 vs 825,000; dep 70,000 vs 50,000\nMachinery: cost 420,000 vs 495,000; dep 270,000 vs 250,000\nQuoted Investments: 50,000 vs 30,000\nStock: 215,000 vs 192,000\nDebtors: 178,000 vs 170,000\nGovernment Securities: 20,000 vs 0\nBank: 0 vs 5,000\nCash: 4,000 vs 3,000\nTrade creditors: 290,000 vs 285,000\nInterest due: 3,000 vs 0\nTaxation: 80,000 vs 75,000\nBank overdraft: 6,000 vs 0\n10% Debentures: 160,000 vs 240,000\nOrdinary Shares: 690,000 vs 670,000\nShare Premium: 4,000 vs 0\n\nAdditional info:\n1. No disposals of buildings; new buildings acquired.\n2. No purchases of machinery; machinery disposed of for €30,000.\n3. Depreciation charged on machinery = €40,000.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Cash Flow Statement with Reconciliation Statements (51 marks)\n(b)(i) Explain why Doyle plc, with operating profit of €185,000, generated greater net cash inflow from operations.\n(b)(ii) List three accounting obligations of a large public company. (9 marks)",
     a:"RECONCILIATION OF OPERATING PROFIT TO NET CASH FLOW:\nOperating Profit: €185,000\nDepreciation charge for year: €60,000\n(Machinery dep: 40,000; Buildings dep: 70,000 - 50,000 = 20,000; Total = 60,000)\nAdd Loss on sale of machinery: €25,000\n(Cost disposed: 495,000 - 420,000 = 75,000; Dep on disposed: 250,000 + 40,000 - 270,000 = 20,000; NBV = 55,000; sold for 30,000; loss = 25,000)\nLess Increase in Stock: (€23,000)\nLess Increase in Debtors: (€8,000)\nAdd Increase in Creditors: €5,000\nNet Cash Inflow from Operating Activities: €244,000\n\nCASH FLOW STATEMENT:\nOperating Activities: €244,000\n\nReturns on Investment & Servicing of Finance:\nInterest on debentures paid: 0 + 16,000 - 3,000 = (€13,000)\n\nTaxation Paid: 75,000 + 45,000 - 80,000 = (€40,000)\n\nCapital Expenditure & Financial Investment:\nInvestments purchased: (€20,000)\nPayment for buildings: 900,000 - 825,000 = (€75,000)\nReceipts from sale of machinery: €30,000\nNet: (€65,000)\n\nEquity Dividends Paid: (€60,000)\n\nNet Cash Inflow before Liquid Resources & Financing: €66,000\n\nManagement of Liquid Resources:\nPurchase of Govt Securities: (€20,000)\n\nFinancing:\nRepayment of Debentures: (€80,000)\nReceipt from Issue of Shares: €20,000\nShare Premium: €4,000\nNet: (€56,000)\n\nDecrease in Cash: (€10,000)\n\nRECONCILIATION OF NET CASH TO MOVEMENT IN NET DEBT:\nDecrease in Cash: (€10,000)\nCash used to purchase Govt Securities: €20,000\nCash used to repay Debentures: €80,000\nChange in Net Debt: €90,000\nNet Debt 01/01/2013: Debentures (240,000) + Bank 5,000 + Cash 3,000 = (€232,000)\nNet Debt 31/12/2013: Debentures (160,000) + Bank (6,000) + Cash 4,000 + Govt Sec 20,000 = (€142,000)\n\n(b)(i) Doyle plc generated €59,000 more cash than operating profit because: depreciation €60,000 and loss on sale of machinery €25,000 reduced profit but had no effect on cash. Increase in creditors €5,000 increased cash but not profit. Increases in debtors €8,000 and stock €23,000 reduced cash inflow but not profit.\n\n(b)(ii) Obligations of a large public company:\n1. Provide a full set of accounts, balance sheet and cash flow statement to shareholders at AGM\n2. File/register full accounts and balance sheet with the registrar of companies\n3. Must have its accounts audited and present an annual report including director's report and auditor's report"},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Published Accounts (Danner plc)",
     q:"Danner plc, authorised capital €900,000 (700,000 ordinary + 200,000 4% preference at €1 each).\n\nTrial balance at 31/12/2013:\nBuildings at cost: €750,000; Buildings acc dep 01/01: €81,000\nVehicles at cost: €240,000; Vehicles acc dep 01/01: €25,000\nQuoted Investments: €250,000 (market value €110,000)\nUnquoted Investments: €70,000 (directors' value €60,000)\nDebtors: €290,000; Creditors: €198,000\nStock 01/01/2013: €82,000\nPatent 01/01/2013: €84,000\nAdmin expenses: €230,000; Distribution costs: €260,000\nPurchases: €1,000,500; Sales: €1,770,000\nRental income: €30,000; Profit on sale of land: €20,000\nDividends paid: €22,000; Bank: €81,000\nVAT: €65,000; 5% Debentures 2018/2019: €350,000\nP&L balance 01/01/2013: €41,000\nInvestment income: quoted €2,000, unquoted €1,500\nOrdinary shares: €550,000; 4% Pref shares: €200,000\nBDP: €20,000; Debenture interest paid: €5,000\nDiscount: €11,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock €90,000\n(ii) Land adjacent to premises (cost €110,000) sold for €130,000. Buildings revalued at €900,000 at year end.\n(iii) Provide for: debenture interest due, auditors' fees €15,000, directors' fees €50,000, corporation tax €90,000.\n(iv) Admin expenses includes receipt of patent royalties €3,000.\n(v) Buildings depreciation 2% straight line (25% distribution / 75% admin). No purchase or sale of buildings. Vehicles 20% of cost.\n(vi) Patent acquired 01/01/2010 for €120,000, amortised over 10 years. Amortisation in cost of sales.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Published P&L with notes (51 marks)\n(b)(i) Three items for a Directors' Report\n(b)(ii) Explain Exceptional Item (9 marks)",
     a:"PUBLISHED P&L ACCOUNT:\nTurnover: €1,770,000\nCost of Sales: 82,000 + 1,000,500 - 90,000 + 12,000 = €1,004,500\n(Patent amortisation 120,000/10 = 12,000 included in COS)\nGross Profit: €765,500\n\nDistribution Costs: 260,000 + 3,750 + 48,000 = €311,750\n(Buildings dep 25%: 15,000 x 25% = 3,750; Vehicles dep: 240,000 x 20% = 48,000)\nAdmin Expenses: 230,000 + 50,000 + 15,000 + 3,000 + 11,250 = €309,250\n(Directors' fees; auditors' fees; patent royalties added back to admin since it was deducted; buildings dep 75%: 15,000 x 75% = 11,250)\nNet: €144,500\n\nOther Operating Income: 30,000 + 3,000 + 11,000 = €44,000\n(Rental income + patent royalties + discount)\nOperating Profit: €188,500\n\nInvestment Income: 2,000 + 1,500 = €3,500\nProfit on sale of land: €20,000 (exceptional item)\nInterest payable: 350,000 x 5% = (€17,500)\nProfit before taxation: €194,500\nTaxation: (€90,000)\nProfit after tax: €104,500\nDividends: (€22,000)\nRetained: €82,500\nP&L b/f: €41,000\nP&L balance 31/12/2013: €123,500\n\nNOTES:\n1. ACCOUNTING POLICY: Buildings revalued at year-end and included at revalued amount. Depreciation: Buildings 2% SL, Vehicles 20% of cost. Stock: FIFO at lower of cost and NRV.\n\n2. OPERATING PROFIT after charging: Depreciation on tangible fixed assets €63,000 (vehicles 48,000 + buildings 15,000); Patent amortised €12,000; Directors' remuneration €50,000; Auditors' fees €15,000.\n\n3. FINANCIAL FIXED ASSETS:\nQuoted investments: 01/01 €250,000; 31/12 €250,000 (market value €110,000)\nUnquoted investments: 01/01 €70,000; 31/12 €70,000 (directors' value €60,000)\n\n4. TANGIBLE FIXED ASSETS:\nL&B: Cost 01/01 860,000 (750,000 + 110,000 land sold in year); Disposal (110,000); Reval surplus 150,000; Cost 31/12 900,000\nAcc Dep: 01/01 81,000; Charge 15,000 = 96,000; Transfer on revaluation (96,000) = 0\nNBV: 01/01 779,000; 31/12 900,000\nVehicles: Cost 240,000 unchanged\nAcc Dep: 01/01 25,000; Charge 48,000 = 73,000\nNBV: 01/01 215,000; 31/12 167,000\nTotal NBV: 01/01 994,000; 31/12 1,067,000\n\n(b)(i) Directors' Report must include: amount to be transferred to reserves; a fair review of the development of the business during the year; details of any important events affecting the company since year-end; significant changes in fixed assets; principal activities and any changes therein.\n\n(b)(ii) Exceptional Item: A material item of significant size that must be shown separately in the P&L because of its size. Example: profit or loss on sale of a fixed asset or a large bad debt write-off."},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Creditors Control Account (B. Young)",
     q:"Creditors Ledger Control Account of B. Young showed: €63,552 Cr and €490 Dr on 31/12/2013. These did not agree with the Schedule of Creditors.\n\nErrors:\n(i) Cash purchases by Young of €890 debited to a supplier's account.\n(ii) Discount €120 disallowed by supplier had been treated as discount received €210 in the books.\n(iii) A creditor charged Young interest €110 on overdue account. Only entry: €11 debited to creditor's account. After protest, interest reduced to €50 but not reflected in accounts.\n(iv) Invoice from supplier for €860 entered in day book as €680. When posting, no entry made in personal account.\n(v) Young returned goods €400 and entered correctly. Credit note arrived showing 5% restocking charge deduction. Total credit note credited to creditor's account. No other entry for credit note.\n(vi) Credit note received from supplier for €313. Only entry: €31 credited to supplier's account.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Adjusted Creditors Control Account (24 marks)\n(b) Adjusted Schedule of Creditors (28 marks)\n(c)(i) Explain Contra Item\n(c)(ii) How might the opening balance of €490 arise? (8 marks)",
     a:"(a) ADJUSTED CREDITORS CONTROL ACCOUNT:\nDr side: Balance b/d €490; Credit Note (vi) €313; Balance c/d €63,819\nCr side: Balance b/d €63,552; Purchases (iv) €180 (860 - 680); Discount disallowed (ii) €330 (120 + 210); Interest (iii) €50; Restocking charge (v) €20 (400 x 5%); Balance c/d €490\nTotals: €64,622\nAdjusted balance: €63,329 (63,819 - 490)\n\n(b) SCHEDULE OF CREDITORS:\nBalance per list: €61,892\nAdd: Cash purchases (i) €890; Discount disallowed (ii) €330; Interest (iii) €61 (50 + 11); Invoice (iv) €860\nTotal additions: €2,141\nSubtotal: €64,033\nDeduct: Credit note adjustment (v) €360 (400 x 5% = 20 deduction, so credit note = 380; creditor credited 380, should be 400 less 20 = 380... correction swing = 360); Credit note (vi) €344 (313 + 31)\nTotal deductions: (€704)\nAdjusted balance: €63,329\n\n(c)(i) Contra Item: An offset of a debtor against a creditor where the debtor and creditor are the same person or business. The amount owed and owing are netted off.\n\n(c)(ii) An opening debit balance of €490 could arise from: overpayment of a debt to a creditor; or full payment of a debt followed by a credit note (returns or price reduction) or discount being received."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Interpretation of Accounts (Shannon plc)",
     q:"Shannon plc financial data:\nSales: €950,000\nCost of Sales: €805,000\nNet Profit: €47,000\nInterest: €18,000\nCapital Employed: €792,000\nDebtors: €43,000\nOrdinary shares: €350,000\n4% Preference shares: €125,000\nOrdinary dividend: €35,000\nMarket price: 130 cent\n\nPrior year ratios (2012):\nROCE: 10.1%; Acid Test: 0.90:1; Gearing: 48%\nInterest cover: 4 times; Dividend cover: 1.25 times\n\nShannon plc is in the Construction Industry and seeks a bank loan of €350,000 at 9% for expansion.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Calculate: (i) Cash Sales (ii) ROCE (iii) EPS (iv) Dividend Yield (v) Period to recoup share price (50 marks)\n(b) Report advising bank manager on loan application (40 marks)\n(c) Distinguish between liquidity and solvency (10 marks)",
     a:"(a) CALCULATIONS:\n\n(i) Cash Sales:\nDebtors/Credit Sales x 12 = 2 months\nCredit Sales = 43,000 x 12/2 = €258,000\nCash Sales = 950,000 - 258,000 = €692,000\n\n(ii) ROCE = (47,000 + 18,000) / 792,000 x 100 = 8.21%\n\n(iii) EPS = (47,000 - 5,000) / 350,000 = 12 cent\n(Pref dividend: 125,000 x 4% = 5,000)\n\n(iv) Dividend Yield:\nDPS = 35,000 / 350,000 = 10 cent\nDividend Yield = 10/130 x 100 = 7.69%\n\n(v) Period to recoup = 130/10 = 13 years\n\n(b) BANK LOAN REPORT:\nProfitability: ROCE fell from 10.1% to 8.21%. This is less than the 9% loan interest rate. The bank would question why the company would borrow at 9% if ROCE is only 8.21%.\n\nLiquidity: Acid test worsened from 0.90:1 to 0.45:1. Very poor — only 45c of liquid assets per €1 owed short-term. Will worsen further if loan is granted.\n\nGearing: Rose from 48% to 50.5% (highly geared). With the €350,000 loan, gearing would rise to 65.67%. Interest cover fell from 4 times to 3.61 times and would get much worse.\n\nSecurity: Fixed assets at cost €790,000 but €300,000 already committed to debentures. Remaining tangible fixed assets €480,000 less security = €180,000 — not adequate.\n\nDividend Policy: Cover of only 1.2 times (down from 1.25). Too much profit paid out in dividends, jeopardising loan repayment capacity.\n\nConclusion: Loan should NOT be granted. Falling profit, serious liquidity problem, high gearing, and generous dividend policy make Shannon plc a poor risk.\n\n(c) Liquidity measures the ability of the company to meet its short-term debts as they fall due (current assets vs current liabilities). Solvency measures the ability of the company to meet all its debts, both short-term and long-term (total assets vs total liabilities). A company can be solvent but illiquid."}
  ],
  "2015": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Final Accounts - Company (Melba Ltd)",
     q:"Melba Ltd, authorised capital €1,500,000 (1,100,000 ordinary + 400,000 4% preference at €1 each).\n\nTrial balance at 31/12/2014:\nBuildings at cost: €713,000\nDelivery vans (cost €250,000): €170,000\nDiscount (net): €12,200\nP&L balance 01/01/2014: €62,200 (debit balance)\nStock 01/01/2014: €74,500\nDebenture interest for first 6 months: €16,200\n3% Investments 01/01/2014: €350,000\nPatents (inc 4 months investment income): €21,500\nPurchases: €1,120,000; Sales: €1,495,000\nDividends paid: €25,000; BDP: €4,000\nDebtors: €99,200; Creditors: €81,100; Bank: €50,000 (OD)\nSalaries and general expenses (inc suspense): €231,100\n8% Debentures: €400,000\nOrdinary shares: €500,000; 4% Pref shares: €300,000\nVAT: €5,000; Advertising: €9,600\nCapital reserve: €45,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock €80,400; includes damaged stock cost €6,600, NRV €3,200.\n(ii) Patents (inc 4 months investment income) written off over 5 years from 2014.\n(iii) Suspense: incorrect debenture interest (correct in bank) and discount allowed €400 entered only in discount a/c.\n(iv) Stock cost €6,000 destroyed by fire; insurance pays €5,000 compensation.\n(v) Van depreciation 15% of cost from date of purchase to date of sale. Van cost €30,000 on 31/03/2011 traded in 31/03/2014. New van €56,000, allowance €8,000. Net treated as purchase of trading stock.\n(vi) New warehouse €100,000 + VAT 13%. Amount paid entered in buildings, no VAT entry.\n(vii) Bank: firm shows €50,000 OD. Bank statement shows €46,690 OD. Discrepancies: (1) 2 months investment income paid into bank; (2) payment from liquidator 25c in euro on €4,000 debt; (3) cheque to advertising firm €560 unpresented.\n(viii) Directors recommend: investment income and debenture interest due; BDP 4% of debtors; buildings depreciation 2% of cost; MD bonus 3% on sales > €900,000 plus 5% on sales > €1,200,000.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading and Profit and Loss Account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance Sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"TRADING P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: €1,495,000\nOpening stock: €74,500\nPurchases: 1,120,000 - 6,000 - 48,000 = €1,066,000\n(Fire-damaged stock removed from purchases: 6,000; van net treated as purchase: 56,000 - 8,000 = 48,000)\nClosing stock: 80,400 - 3,400 = €77,000\n(Damaged stock write-down: 6,600 - 3,200 = 3,400)\nCost of Sales: €1,063,500\nGross Profit: €431,500\n\nAdministration Expenses:\nPatent written off: (21,500 + 3,500)/5 = €5,000\n(Patent inc 4 months inv income: 350,000 x 3% x 4/12 = 3,500)\nSalaries & general expenses: 231,100 + 200 + 400 = €231,700\n(Suspense: debenture interest adj +200; discount allowed +400)\nLoss on fire-damaged stock: 6,000 - 5,000 = €1,000\nDepreciation buildings: (713,000 - 13,000) x 2% = €14,000\n(VAT on warehouse: 100,000 x 13% = 13,000 deducted from buildings cost; buildings cost = 700,000)\nTotal admin: €251,700\n\nSelling & Distribution:\nDepreciation vans: 33,000 + 1,125 + 6,300 = €40,425\n(Remaining vans: 220,000 x 15% = 33,000; old van 3 months: 30,000 x 15% x 3/12 = 1,125; new van 9 months: 56,000 x 15% x 9/12 = 6,300)\nLoss on sale of van: 30,000 - 8,000 - 13,500 = €8,500\n(Acc dep on old van: 30,000 x 15% x 3 years = 13,500; NBV = 16,500; allowance 8,000)\nBad debts: 4,000 - 1,000 = €3,000\n(Liquidator payment: 4,000 x 25% = 1,000 received; write off remainder 3,000)\nAdvertising: €9,600\nSales commission: 595,000 x 3% + 295,000 x 5% = 17,850 + 14,750 = €32,600\n(Sales > 900,000: 1,495,000 - 900,000 = 595,000 at 3%; Sales > 1,200,000: 1,495,000 - 1,200,000 = 295,000 at 5%)\nTotal selling: €94,125\nTotal expenses: (€345,825)\n\nOperating Income:\nDiscount: €12,200\nReduction in BDP: 4,000 - 3,792 = €208\n(New BDP: 94,800 x 4% = 3,792; debtors: 99,200 - 4,000 - 400 = 94,800)\nTotal: €12,408\nOperating Profit: €98,083\n\nInvestment Income: 350,000 x 3% = €10,500\nDebenture Interest: 400,000 x 8% = (€32,000)\nNet Profit: €76,583\nDividends: (€25,000)\nP&L b/f: (€62,200) (debit balance)\nP&L balance 31/12/2014: (€10,617)\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nPatents: (21,500 + 3,500) - 5,000 = €20,000\nBuildings: Cost €700,000; Dep €14,000; NBV €686,000\nDelivery Vans: Cost 276,000 (250,000 + 56,000 - 30,000); Acc Dep 106,925 (80,000 + 40,425 - 13,500); NBV €169,075\n3% Investments: €350,000\nStock: €77,000\nDebtors: 99,200 - 4,000 - 400 = €94,800; BDP: 94,800 x 4% = €3,792\nInsurance company due: €5,000\nInvestment income due: 10,500 - 3,500 - 1,750 = €5,250\n(Received in patent: 3,500; received in bank per statement: 1,750)\nVAT: 5,000 - 13,000 = €8,000 (current asset, owed to firm)\nCreditors: €81,100\nBank (OD): 50,000 - 1,750 - 1,000 = €47,250\n(Bank reconciliation: 50,000 less 2 months inv income 1,750 less liquidator 1,000 = 47,250; OR 46,690 + 560 unpresented = 47,250)\nSales commission due: €32,600\nDebenture interest due: 32,000 - 16,200 + 200 = €16,000\n8% Debentures: €400,000\nCapital reserve: €45,000\nP&L balance: (€10,617)"},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Depreciation of Fixed Assets (Euro Ltd)",
     q:"Euro Ltd, sportswear manufacturer. Machinery depreciated straight-line over 10 years, scrap value 5% of cost. Depreciation from date of purchase to date of sale.\n\nOn 01/01/2013, Euro Ltd owned:\nNo. 1: purchased 01/07/2010, cost €50,000\nNo. 2: purchased 01/09/2011, cost €65,000\nNo. 3: purchased 01/01/2012, cost €75,000\n\n01/03/2013: Machine No. 1 ruined by malfunction. Insurance paid €25,000; scrap dealer paid €500. New Machine No. 4 purchased for €60,000.\n01/01/2014: Machine No. 3 had motor modifications costing €4,000. Depreciation on modification = €475/year.\n01/04/2014: Machine No. 2 traded in for €27,500 against new Machine No. 5 costing €55,000. Balance paid immediately. Delivery €2,000 and installation €400.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Machinery Account for 2013 and 2014 (6 marks)\n(b) Provision for Depreciation Account for 2013 and 2014 (32 marks)\n(c) Machinery Disposal Account (14 marks)\n(d)(i) Why charge depreciation?\n(d)(ii) Factors for depreciation policy (8 marks)",
     a:"Depreciation per annum:\nNo. 1: (50,000 - 2,500) x 10% = €4,750\nNo. 2: (65,000 - 3,250) x 10% = €6,175\nNo. 3: (75,000 - 3,750) x 10% = €7,125\nNo. 4: (60,000 - 3,000) x 10% = €5,700\nNo. 5: (57,400 - 2,870) x 10% = €5,453\nModification: €475/year\n\n(a) MACHINERY ACCOUNT:\n2013: Bal b/d 190,000 (50,000 + 65,000 + 75,000); Bank No. 4 60,000 = 250,000\nDisposal No. 1 50,000; Bal c/d 200,000 = 250,000\n\n2014: Bal b/d 200,000; Bank (modification) 4,000; Bank No. 5 57,400 = 261,400\n(Machine No. 5 cost: 55,000 + 2,000 delivery + 400 installation = 57,400)\nDisposal No. 2 65,000; Bal c/d 196,400 = 261,400\n\n(b) PROVISION FOR DEPRECIATION ACCOUNT:\n2013:\nBal b/d 27,233\n(No. 1: 4,750 x 2.5 = 11,875; No. 2: 6,175 x 1 + 2,058 = 8,233; No. 3: 7,125 x 1 = 7,125; Total = 27,233)\nP&L charge 18,842\n(No. 1: 4,750 x 2/12 = 792; No. 2: 6,175; No. 3: 7,125; No. 4: 5,700 x 10/12 = 4,750; Total = 18,842)\nDisposal No. 1: 12,667 (11,875 + 792)\nBal c/d: 33,408\n\n2014:\nBal b/d 33,408\nP&L charge 18,934\n(No. 2: 6,175 x 3/12 = 1,544; No. 3: 7,125; No. 3 modification: 475; No. 4: 5,700; No. 5: 5,453 x 9/12 = 4,090; Total = 18,934)\nDisposal No. 2: 15,952 (8,233 + 6,175 + 1,544)\nBal c/d: 36,390\n\n(c) DISPOSAL ACCOUNTS:\nMachine No. 1 (2013):\nDr: Machine 50,000\nCr: Insurance 25,000; Scrap 500; Depreciation 12,667; Loss (P&L) 11,833\n\nMachine No. 2 (2014):\nDr: Machine 65,000\nCr: Trade-in allowance 27,500; Depreciation 15,952; Loss (P&L) 21,548\n\n(d)(i) Depreciation is an expense charged to write off the cost of a fixed asset over its useful economic life. Failure to charge depreciation would overstate profits and overstate the net worth of assets in the balance sheet, not showing a true and fair view.\n\n(d)(ii) Factors to consider: type of asset; estimated useful life; cost of the asset; scrap/residual value at end of life; method of depreciation to be used."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Tabular Statement (Watt Ltd)",
     q:"Watt Ltd balance sheet at 01/01/2014:\nGoodwill (cost €55,000): €45,000\nL&B: cost €690,000, dep €55,100, net €634,900\nEquipment: cost €30,000, dep €2,500, net €27,500\nDelivery vans: cost €86,000, dep €32,000, net €54,000\nStock: €73,600; Insurance prepaid: €1,000; Debtors: €52,900\nCreditors: €82,300; Bank: €11,900 (OD); Expenses due: €3,700\nIssued: 480,000 ordinary shares; Share premium: €75,000; P&L balance: €236,000\n\nTransactions:\nJan: Bought adjoining business — buildings €240,000, delivery vans €42,000, creditors €54,000. Purchase price discharged by granting 220,000 shares at 20c premium.\n\nFeb: Creditor owed €2,500 accepted fridge freezer (book value €2,200, cost €4,000) in full settlement.\n\nApril: L&B revalued at €990,000 on 01/04/2014 (land €170,000).\n\nMay: Bank statement — direct debit €3,600 for fire insurance (year ended 31/03/2015); credit transfer €8,600 for 10 months' rent receivable in advance from May 1.\n\nJuly: Van (cost €15,000, NBV €3,000) traded in for new van €24,000 on 01/07/2014. Allowance €4,500.\n\nNov: Payment €1,260 from T McDonagh, debtor whose debt was previously written off. Represents 90% of original debt; remainder due January 2015. Same day: goods €720 sold on credit (20% mark-up).\n\nDec: Buildings depreciation 2% of value at 01/04/2014. Vans depreciation 20% of cost from purchase to sale.\n\nRequired: Tabular statement showing effect of each transaction.",
     a:"TABULAR STATEMENT — KEY FIGURES:\n\nJan — Acquisition of Business:\nGoodwill: 272,000 - 240,000 - 42,000 + 54,000 = +€44,000 (goodwill on acquisition)\nAlternatively: Shares 220,000 + Premium 44,000 = 264,000; net assets = buildings 240,000 + vans 42,000 - creditors 54,000 = 228,000; goodwill = 264,000 - 228,000 = 36,000\nL&B: +€240,000; Vans: +€42,000; Creditors: +€54,000\nShares: +€220,000; Share premium: +€44,000\n\nFeb — Equipment settlement:\nEquipment: -€4,000 (at cost); Equipment dep: +€1,800 (reverse dep on disposed)\nCreditors: -€2,500\nP&L: loss on disposal 2,200 - 2,500 = -€300 (profit €300)\nActually: NBV 2,200, settled debt 2,500, so profit €300 to P&L\n\nApril — Revaluation:\nL&B revalued to €990,000 from NBV (690,000 + 240,000 - 55,100) = 874,900\nRevaluation reserve: 990,000 - 874,900 = +€115,100\nAcc dep on buildings written off: +€55,100\n\nMay — Insurance and Rent:\nBank: -€3,600 (insurance DD) + €8,600 (rent) = +€5,000\nInsurance prepaid: 3,600 - 1,000 (old prepaid expired) = +€2,600\nP&L: insurance -€1,000 (old year); rent income recognised for 2 months May-Dec... actually 10 months advance from May, 8 months to Dec = earned\nRent receivable: +€8,600; then 8 months earned (€6,880) to P&L; 2 months advance = €1,720 remaining\n\nJuly — Van trade-in:\nVans: -15,000 + 24,000 = +€9,000; Van dep: reverse +€12,000 (dep on old); charge new van dep\nBank: -(24,000 - 4,500) = -€19,500\nP&L: profit on disposal 4,500 - 3,000 = +€1,500\n\nNov — Bad debt recovered:\nBank: +€1,260\nDebtors: +€140 (remainder due) + €720 (new sale) = +€860\nP&L: bad debt recovered +€1,400 (original debt); goods cost = 720/1.20 = 600; gross profit +€120\nStock: -€600\n\nDec — Depreciation:\nBuildings dep: (990,000 - 170,000) x 2% = €16,400\nVan dep: existing vans full year + new van 6 months - old van 6 months\nP&L: charged depreciation\n\nCLOSING BALANCES:\nGoodwill: 45,000 + 36,000 = €81,000\nL&B: €990,000; Dep: €16,400\nEquipment: €26,000; Dep: €700\nVans: €137,000; Dep: €46,500\nShares: €700,000; Share Premium: €119,000\nRevaluation Reserve: €115,100\nRent receivable (advance): €1,720"},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Farm Accounts (John and Elaine Daly)",
     q:"Among assets/liabilities of John and Elaine Daly on 01/01/2014:\nL&B at cost: €450,000 (land €175,000); Vehicles & Machinery at cost: €82,000\nElectricity due: €330; Cattle: €60,000; Sheep: €24,000\nMilk cheque due: €1,800; Stock of fuel: €620\nThree months investment interest due: €300\n\nLodgements: Bank b/f €27,200; Milk €29,000; Sheep €28,000; Cattle €14,000; Lambs €10,400; Calves €6,500; Single payment sheep €2,100; Single payment cattle €3,500; Wool €1,400; Forestry premium €1,800; Interest from 3% investment bond €600\nPayments: Fertiliser €2,500; General farm expenses €14,200; Dairy wages €2,600; Sheep €18,500; Cattle €12,600; Light/heat/fuel €2,210; Machinery €6,200; Repairs €5,000; Vet fees/medicines €2,480; Bank loan + 18 months interest at 6% on 30/04/2014: €21,800; Balance 31/12: €36,410\n\nClosing: Cattle €75,000; Sheep €23,000\nFamily use: Milk €750; Lamb €580\nVet fees include family health insurance €1,500\nGeneral expenses, fertiliser, vet fees: 70% cattle/30% sheep\nOther expenses: 75% farm / 25% household\nDepreciation: machinery 10% of cost; buildings 2% p.a. (land €175,000)\nClosing: milk cheque due €1,500; fertiliser creditors €450; fuel stock €560\n\nRequired:\n(a) Statement of Capital 01/01/2014 (20 marks)\n(b) Enterprise Analysis: Cattle & Milk, Sheep (20 marks)\n(c) General P&L Account (10 marks)\n(d) Drawings Account (5 marks)\n(e)(i) Purpose of General P&L (ii) Advantages of enterprise analysis (5 marks)",
     a:"(a) STATEMENT OF CAPITAL 01/01/2014:\nAssets:\nL&B: €450,000\nVehicles & Machinery: €82,000\nCattle: €60,000; Sheep: €24,000\nMilk cheque due: €1,800; Fuel stock: €620\nInvestments: €40,000\n(3% bond: interest €300 x 12/3 = €1,200 p.a.; investment = 1,200/0.03 = €40,000)\nInvestment interest due: €300\nBank: €27,200\nTotal Assets: €685,920\n\nLiabilities:\nElectricity due: €330\nBank loan: €20,000\n(109% = 21,800; loan = 21,800/1.09 = 20,000; total interest 1,800)\nLoan interest due: €1,400\n(18 months interest at 6% = 9%; 10 months accrued to 01/01/2014: 20,000 x 6% x 10/12 = 1,000... OR interest = 1,800; period elapsed from loan start to 01/01 = 14 months of 18; due at 01/01 = 1,800 x 14/18 = 1,400)\nTotal Liabilities: (€21,730)\nCapital: €664,190\n\n(b) ENTERPRISE ANALYSIS — CATTLE AND MILK:\nIncome:\nMilk sales: 29,000 - 1,800 + 1,500 = €28,700\nCattle & calves sales: 14,000 + 6,500 = €20,500\nSingle payment cattle: €3,500\nIncrease in cattle stock: 75,000 - 60,000 = €15,000\nDrawings (milk): €750\nTotal income: €68,450\n\nExpenditure:\nCattle purchases: €12,600\nDairy wages: €2,600\nGeneral expenses (70%): 14,200 x 70% = €9,940\nFertiliser (70%): 2,950 x 70% = €2,065\n(Fertiliser: 2,500 + 450 closing creditor = 2,950)\nVet fees (70%): 980 x 70% = €686\n(Vet: 2,480 - 1,500 VHI = 980)\nTotal expenditure: (€27,891)\nProfit on Cattle & Milk: €40,559\n\nENTERPRISE ANALYSIS — SHEEP:\nIncome:\nSheep & lambs sales: 28,000 + 10,400 = €38,400\nSingle payment sheep: €2,100\nWool: €1,400\nDrawings (lamb): €580\nTotal income: €42,480\n\nExpenditure:\nDecrease in sheep stock: 24,000 - 23,000 = €1,000\nSheep purchases: €18,500\nGeneral expenses (30%): 14,200 x 30% = €4,260\nFertiliser (30%): 2,950 x 30% = €885\nVet fees (30%): 980 x 30% = €294\nTotal expenditure: (€24,939)\nProfit on Sheep: €17,541\n\n(c) GENERAL P&L ACCOUNT:\nIncome:\nProfit — Cattle & Milk: €40,559\nProfit — Sheep: €17,541\nInvestment interest: €1,200\n(300/3 x 12 = 1,200 OR 40,000 x 3% = 1,200)\nForestry premium: €1,800\nTotal: €61,100\n\nExpenditure:\nLight/heat/fuel (75%): 1,940 x 75% = €1,455\n(2,210 + 620 - 330 - 560 = 1,940)\nRepairs (75%): 5,000 x 75% = €3,750\nMachinery depreciation: (82,000 + 6,200) x 10% x 75% = €6,615\nBuildings depreciation: (450,000 - 175,000) x 2% x 75% = €4,125\nLoan interest: €300\n(Total interest €1,800 for 18 months; 2014 portion: 4 months Jan-Apr at 6% = 20,000 x 6% x 4/12 = 400; private 25% = 100; farm = 300)\nTotal: (€16,245)\nNet Profit: €44,855\n\n(d) DRAWINGS ACCOUNT:\nMilk: €750; Lamb: €580\nLight & heat (25%): €485\nVHI: €1,500\nRepairs (25%): €1,250\nDep machinery (25%): €2,205\nDep buildings (25%): €1,375\nLoan interest (25%): €100\nTotal: €8,245\n\n(e)(i) The General P&L Account includes expenses and gains that cannot be directly allocated to individual farm enterprises. It is used to ascertain the overall profit or loss of the farm.\n\n(e)(ii) Enterprise analysis allows costs to be allocated to individual farm activities. The farmer can find the profit/loss on each activity and see each enterprise's contribution to total profit. This enables better planning, decision-making, and resource allocation."},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Interpretation of Accounts (GJ plc)",
     q:"GJ plc, food processing sector. Authorised capital €900,000 (800,000 ordinary + 100,000 5% preference at €1 each). Issued: 650,000 ordinary shares, all preference shares.\n\nP&L for year ended 31/12/2014:\nSales: €878,000\nCost of goods sold: (€720,000)\nOperating expenses: (€90,000)\nInterest: (€14,000)\nNet profit: €54,000\nDividends paid: (€44,000)\nRetained: €10,000; P&L b/f: €15,000; P&L balance: €25,000\n\nBalance Sheet at 31/12/2014:\nFixed Assets: €855,000; Investments (market value €100,000): €110,000\nCurrent Assets (inc stock €51,500, debtors €80,000): €131,500\nTrade creditors: €121,500\n7% Debentures (2016 secured): €200,000\nOrdinary shares: €650,000; 5% Pref shares: €100,000\nMarket price: 95c per share\n\n2013 ratios: EPS 9.1c; DPS 8.0c; Interest cover 6 times; Quick ratio 0.90:1; ROCE 8.2%; Market price 97c; Gearing 28%\n\nRequired:\n(a) Calculate: (i) Opening Stock (ii) EPS (iii) Dividend Yield (iv) P/E Ratio (v) Interest Cover (50 marks)\n(b) Report to investor Tom Murphy on purchasing 150,000 shares at 90c each (€50,000 savings, borrow remainder at 9%) (40 marks)\n(c) Limitations of ratio analysis (10 marks)",
     a:"(a) CALCULATIONS:\n\n(i) Opening Stock:\nAverage Stock = 720,000 / 12 = €60,000\nOpening Stock = (60,000 x 2) - 51,500 = €68,500\n\n(ii) EPS = (54,000 - 5,000) / 650,000 = 7.54 cent\n(Pref dividend: 100,000 x 5% = 5,000)\n\n(iii) Dividend Yield:\nDPS = (44,000 - 5,000) / 650,000 = 6 cent\nDividend Yield = 6/95 x 100 = 6.32%\n\n(iv) P/E Ratio = 95 / 7.54 = 12.60 times\n\n(v) Interest Cover = (54,000 + 14,000) / 14,000 = 4.86 times\n\n(b) INVESTMENT REPORT TO TOM MURPHY:\n\nMarket Price: Share price falling from 97c (2013) to 95c (market) and available at 90c. Worrying downward trend indicating lack of stock market confidence. P/E ratio 12.6 (was 10.66 in 2013) — longer period to recoup investment.\n\nDividend Policy: Yield 6.32% (was 8.25% in 2013). DPS reduced from 8c to 6c. Dividend cover improved slightly from 1.14 to 1.26 but company still paying out too much in dividends. Cost of borrowing €85,000 at 9% = €7,650. Dividend income on 150,000 shares at 6c = €9,000. Surplus €1,350 but must compare to lost interest on €50,000 savings.\n\nProfitability: ROCE fell from 8.2% to 6.97% (68,000/975,000). This is below the debenture rate of 7%. Trend is concerning.\n\nLiquidity: Quick ratio fell from 0.90:1 to 0.66:1. Only 66c of liquid assets per €1 owed short-term. Could result in inability to pay dividends despite profits.\n\nGearing: Rose from 28% to 30.77%. Still low geared but worsening trend. Interest cover fell from 6 to 4.86 times. Debentures due 2016 — will require refinancing.\n\nRecommendation: Advise NOT to borrow money to purchase shares. Falling profitability, deteriorating liquidity, and declining share price make this a risky investment.\n\n(c) LIMITATIONS OF RATIO ANALYSIS:\n• Analyses past/historical figures only; quickly out of date\n• Does not show seasonal fluctuations\n• Firms use different accounting bases making comparisons difficult\n• Inflation distorts figures over time\n• Ratios merely give clues to the future, they do not predict outcomes"}
  ],

"2012": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Final Accounts - Company (West Ltd)",
     q:"West Ltd has an Authorised Capital of €1,600,000 divided into 1,100,000 Ordinary Shares at €1 each and 500,000 4% Preference Shares at €1 each.\n\nTrial Balance at 31/12/2011:\nBuildings at cost: €800,000\nDelivery Vans (cost €260,000): €180,000\nDiscount (Net): €10,400\nP&L Balance 01/01/2011: €25,100\nStocks 01/01/2011: €71,200\nDebenture interest (first 4 months): €5,600\n4% Investments 01/01/2011: €300,000\nPatents (inc 3 months investment income): €20,400\nPurchases: €1,140,000; Sales: €1,444,700\nDividends paid: €23,300; BDP: €3,000\nDebtors: €98,400; Creditors: €82,200\nBank: €33,000\nSalaries & general expenses (inc Suspense): €194,300\n9% Debentures: €180,000\nIssued Ordinary Shares: €700,000; 4% Preference Shares: €400,000\nDirectors fees: €40,200; Advertising: €4,800\nCapital Reserve: €50,000\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stock at cost €81,200; includes damaged stock cost €5,400, NRV €2,300\n(ii) Patents (inc 3 months investment income) written off over 4 years from 2011\n(iii) Suspense: incorrect debenture interest (correct in bank) and discount allowed €450 entered only in discount a/c\n(iv) Stock cost €5,000 destroyed by fire; insurance pays €4,000\n(v) Van depreciation 10% of cost (purchase to sale). Van cost €28,000 on 30/06/2005 traded in 31/03/2011 against new van €54,000. Allowance €6,000. Net treated as purchase.\n(vi) Bank: firm shows €33,000; statement shows €31,280 OD. Cheque €640 entered as €460; credit transfer €900 (30c in €1 from bankrupt debtor); unpresented cheque €1,000 (director fees)\n(vii) Advertising: 18-month campaign from 01/10/2011\n(viii) Provide for: investment income due, debenture interest due, BDP at 4% of debtors, buildings dep 2% of cost\n\nRequired:\n(a) Trading and Profit and Loss Account (75 marks)\n(b) Balance Sheet (45 marks)",
     a:"TRADING P&L ACCOUNT:\nSales: €1,444,700\nOpening Stock: €71,200\nPurchases: 1,140,000 - 48,000 = €1,092,000\n(Remove van net payment treated as purchase: 54,000 - 6,000 = 48,000)\nClosing Stock: 81,200 - 3,100 - 5,000 = €73,100\n(Damaged stock write-down: 5,400 - 2,300 = 3,100; fire-damaged stock removed)\nCost of Sales: €1,090,100\nGross Profit: €354,600\n\nExpenses:\nSalaries & general: 194,300 - 450 + 10,600 = €204,450\n(Suspense: debenture interest should be 180,000 x 9% x 4/12 = 5,400 but paid 5,600 => overpaid 200 less, so suspense = deb int correction 10,800 - 450 = 10,350; OR correct suspense figure)\nNote: Suspense analysis:\n- Debenture int paid €5,600 but correct = 180,000 x 9% x 4/12 = €5,400. Bank correct, so TB debit €5,600 but should be €5,400 => credit suspense €200? No: bank correct means bank has correct figure. TB shows €5,600 debit for deb int. Correct deb int for 4 months = 180,000 x 9% x 4/12 = €5,400? But the paper says 9% debentures. Full year = 180,000 x 9% = €16,200. If 4 months paid: 16,200 x 4/12 = €5,400. But TB shows €5,600. Bank correct so bank debited €5,400. Debenture int a/c shows €5,600. Suspense = €5,600 - €5,400 = €200 (Dr suspense).\nDiscount allowed €450 entered only in discount a/c (debit discount) but no credit to debtors => Cr suspense €450.\nSuspense: Dr €200, Cr €450 => Net Cr €250 included in salaries & general expenses.\nSalaries & general: 194,300 + 250 = €194,550\n\nDirectors fees: 40,200 + 1,000 = €41,200\n(Unpresented cheque for director fees €1,000 already in books)\nDiscount: 10,400 + 450 = €10,850\nDepreciation vans: 700 + 25,400 = €26,100\n(Old van to date of sale: 28,000 x 10% x 3/12 = 700; Remaining: (260,000 - 28,000 + 54,000) x 10% = 28,600; but old van dep for year only 3 months = 700; new vans: (260,000 - 28,000) x 10% = 23,200; new van: 54,000 x 10% x 9/12 = 4,050? No - 10% of cost from date of purchase to date of sale. Full year on existing except old van.\nRevised: Old van dep 2011: 28,000 x 10% x 3/12 = €700\nRemaining vans: (260,000 - 28,000) x 10% = €23,200\nNew van: 54,000 x 10% x 9/12 = €4,050\nTotal van dep: 700 + 23,200 + 4,050 = €27,950)\nLoss on disposal of van: NBV - allowance\n(Acc dep on old van: 28,000 x 10% x 5.75 yrs (01/07/2005 to 31/03/2011) = €16,100\nNBV = 28,000 - 16,100 = €11,900; Allowance = €6,000\nLoss on disposal = 11,900 - 6,000 = €5,900)\nFire loss: 5,000 - 4,000 = €1,000\nAdvertising: 4,800 x 3/18 = €800 (3 months of 18-month campaign in 2011)\nDepreciation buildings: 800,000 x 2% = €16,000\nBad debt (bankrupt): 900/0.30 = 3,000 total debt; written off = 3,000 x 70% = €2,100\nBDP adjustment: (98,400 - 450 - 3,000) x 4% = 94,950 x 4% = €3,798; increase = 3,798 - 3,000 = €798\nPatent written off: (20,400 + 3,000)/4 = €5,850\n(Patent inc 3 months inv income => inv income = 300,000 x 4% x 3/12 = 3,000; patent cost = 20,400 - (-3,000)? No: patent incorporates 3 months inv income means inv income was credited to patent a/c. So patent cost = 20,400 + 3,000 = €23,400? Or patent = cost minus inv income received. If inv income was credited to patent: patent cost = 20,400 + 3,000 = 23,400. Written off over 4 years: 23,400/4 = €5,850)\n\nOperating Income:\nDiscount (net): €10,400\nInsurance compensation: €4,000\n\nInvestment Income: 300,000 x 4% = €12,000\nDebenture Interest: 180,000 x 9% = €16,200\n\nSUMMARY P&L:\nGross Profit: €354,600\nLess Expenses:\nSalaries & general: €194,550\nDirectors fees: €41,200\nDiscount: €10,850\nDep vans: €27,950\nLoss on disposal: €5,900\nFire loss: €1,000\nAdvertising: €800\nDep buildings: €16,000\nBad debt written off: €2,100\nBDP increase: €798\nPatent amortisation: €5,850\nTotal expenses: (€306,998)\nAdd: Investment income: €12,000\nLess: Debenture interest: (€16,200)\nNet Profit: €43,402\nP&L b/f: €25,100\nDividends: (€23,300)\nP&L balance 31/12/2011: €45,202\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nBuildings: Cost €800,000; Dep €16,000; NBV €784,000\nVans: Cost €286,000 (260-28+54); Dep €107,950 (80+27,950-16,100+16,100=107,950); NBV €178,050\n(Acc dep b/f on remaining vans: 80,000 - 16,100 = 63,900; + 2011 dep 27,950; + old van dep 16,100 removed = 63,900 + 27,950 = 91,850? Let me recalc: Acc dep b/f = 260,000 - 180,000 = 80,000. Remove old van dep: 80,000 - 16,100 = 63,900. Add 2011 dep charge: 63,900 + 27,950 = 91,850. NBV = 286,000 - 91,850 = 194,150)\nPatent: 23,400 - 5,850 = €17,550 (OR 20,400 + 3,000 - 5,850 = 17,550)\nInvestments: €300,000\nStock: €73,100\nDebtors: 98,400 - 450 - 3,000 = €94,950; BDP: €3,798\nInv income due: 12,000 - 3,000 = €9,000\n(Total inv income €12,000 less 3 months already received in patent = 3,000; remaining due = 9,000)\nFire insurance due: €4,000\nAdvertising prepaid: 4,800 - 800 = €4,000\nBank: Need to reconcile.\nBank per books: €33,000 (debit = asset)\nLess: cheque correction: 640 - 460 = (€180)\nAdd: credit transfer: €900\nCorrected bank: 33,000 - 180 + 900 = €33,720\n(Check: statement €31,280 OD + unpresented €1,000 = €32,280 OD; but books show €33,720 Dr... discrepancy suggests bank is OD in TB)\nIf bank €33,000 is overdraft (credit balance):\nBank OD: 33,000 + 180 - 900 = €32,280 (= 31,280 + 1,000 unpresented) Correct!\nSo Bank overdraft: €32,280\nCreditors: 82,200 + 180 = €82,380\nDeb interest due: 16,200 - 5,600 = €10,600\nDirector fees unpresented: already in books (€1,000 unpresented but already recorded)"},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Correction of Errors and Suspense (N. O'Connell)",
     q:"The Trial Balance of N. O'Connell, a garage owner, failed to agree on 31/12/2011. The difference was entered in a Suspense Account and final accounts prepared showing net profit of €41,000.\n\nErrors:\n(i) O'Connell's private car valued at €7,000 was presented to the business. Car later sold on credit to a debtor for €7,600. Sale treated as a cash sale.\n(ii) O'Connell returned a motor car to supplier for €9,000, entered as €9,900. Credit note arrived showing restocking charge of €300. Only entry: credit of €8,700 in creditors account.\n(iii) Private debt of €1,000 owed to O'Connell offset against business debt of €1,260 owed by O'Connell. No entry made.\n(iv) Bank payments: repairs to premises €600 and insurance of private dwelling €520 entered correctly in bank but credited to premises and insurance respectively.\n(v) Car parts sold on credit for €920 returned. Entered as €20 credit to equipment and €290 debit to purchases.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Journalise the necessary corrections (40 marks)\n(b) Statement of corrected net profit (14 marks)\n(c)(i) Purpose of preparing a Trial Balance\n(c)(ii) Two types of errors not revealed by Trial Balance (6 marks)",
     a:"(a) JOURNAL ENTRIES:\n\n(i) Private car presented to business and sold on credit:\nDr Debtors €7,600, Cr Bank/Cash €7,600\n(Reverse cash sale entry; record as credit sale)\nDr Capital/Drawings €7,000, Cr Profit on sale €600 (or Motor vehicles €7,000)\n(Record capital introduced and profit on sale: 7,600 - 7,000 = 600)\nActual entries:\nOriginal incorrect: Dr Bank 7,600; Cr Sales 7,600\nCorrection: Dr Debtors 7,600; Cr Suspense 7,600 (reverse bank, debit debtors)\nDr Suspense 7,600; Cr Bank 7,600\nDr Motor Vehicles 7,000; Cr Capital 7,000 (record car presented to business)\nDr Cost of Sales/Motor Vehicles 7,000; Cr Motor Vehicles 7,000 (cost of car sold)\nNet effect on suspense: NIL (affects both sides equally)\n\n(ii) Returns and restocking charge:\nOriginal: Cr Creditors 8,700 only\nCorrect entries should be: Dr Creditors 9,000; Cr Purchases Returns 9,000\nThen: Dr Creditors (restocking) 300; no credit yet\nAnd: Dr Purchases Returns 300; Cr Creditors 300 (restocking reduces return)\nCorrection:\nDr Creditors 9,000; Cr Suspense 9,000 (record debit to creditors)\nDr Suspense 8,700; Cr Creditors 8,700 (reverse incorrect credit)\nDr Purchases Returns 900; Cr Suspense 900 (entered as 9,900 not 9,000; also restocking 300)\nNet: Dr Creditors 9,000 + 8,700 = 17,700 debit side; was 8,700 credit\nSimplified: Suspense Dr 8,700; Cr 9,000+900 = 9,900 => Cr 1,200\nDr Creditors €17,700 (9,000 + 8,700)\nCr Purchases Returns €8,700 (9,000 - 300 restocking)\nCr Suspense €300\nDr Restocking charge €300\n\n(iii) Private/business offset:\nDr Creditors €1,260; Cr Drawings €1,000; Cr Discount Received €260\n(Business debt 1,260 offset by private debt 1,000; difference = discount received)\n\n(iv) Repairs and insurance:\nDr Premises/Repairs €1,200; Cr Suspense €1,200\n(Reverse credit entries: 600 + 520 = 1,120 credited; should be debited = swing of 2,240 total)\nActual: Dr Repairs 1,200 (600 x 2 swing); Dr Drawings 1,040 (520 x 2 swing)\nCr Suspense €2,240\n\n(v) Car parts returned:\nDr Sales Returns €920; Dr Equipment €20; Cr Purchases €290; Cr Debtors €920; Cr Suspense €270\n(Reverse incorrect entries and record correctly)\nCorrect: Dr Returns Inward 920; Cr Debtors 920\nReverse incorrect: Dr Equipment 20; debit entry of 290 to Purchases\nSo: Dr Sales Returns 920; Cr Debtors 920\nDr Suspense 20; Cr Equipment 20 (reverse)\nDr Suspense 290; Cr Purchases 290 (reverse)\nNet Suspense: Dr 310\n\nSUSPENSE ACCOUNT:\nDr: (ii) 8,700; (v) 310 = 9,010\nCr: (i) 0; (ii) 9,000+900; (iv) 2,240; Balance = ?\nBalance: Opening suspense + adjustments = 0\n\n(b) CORRECTED NET PROFIT:\nOriginal Net Profit: €41,000\nAdd: Profit on sale of car (i) +600; Discount received (iii) +260; Purchases reversed (v) +290\nTotal additions: +€1,150\nLess: Restocking charge (ii) -300; Repairs (iv) -600; Sales Returns (v) -920\nTotal deductions: -€1,820\nCorrected Net Profit: €40,330\n\n(c)(i) Purpose of Trial Balance: To check the arithmetical accuracy of the double-entry bookkeeping system. If correctly maintained, total debits should equal total credits. It serves as the basis for preparing the Final Accounts.\n\n(c)(ii) Errors not revealed:\n1. Error of Commission: correct amount posted to wrong personal account (e.g. debiting wrong debtor). Both sides still balance.\n2. Error of Principle: entry made in the wrong class of account (e.g. purchase of fixed asset recorded as an expense). Debits still equal credits."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Revaluation of Fixed Assets (Logan Ltd)",
     q:"On 1 January 2007 Logan Ltd owned freehold buildings costing €360,000 and adjacent land costing €240,000. Buildings depreciated at 2% p.a. straight-line. Full year in year of acquisition, none in year of disposal. Property purchased 01/01/2003, depreciation charged for 4 years (2003-2006).\n\nJan 1 2007: Revalued at €700,000 (land €270,000)\nJan 1 2008: Sold adjacent land for €320,000 (cost €240,000, revalued within €700,000)\nJan 1 2009: Purchased buildings €500,000. During 2009 paid €100,000 to contractor + €50,000 wages for extension.\nJan 1 2010: Revalued all buildings at €1,188,000 (10% increase each)\nJan 1 2011: Sold buildings owned on 01/01/2007 for €400,000. Remaining buildings revalued at €950,000.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Land and Building Account 2007-2011\n(b) Provision for Depreciation Account 2007-2011\n(c) Disposal Accounts\n(d)(i) Extract from Balance Sheet 31/12/2011\n(d)(ii) Factors in annual depreciation charge",
     a:"LAND AND BUILDING ACCOUNT:\n2007:\nBal b/d (01/01/2007): €600,000 (360,000 buildings + 240,000 land)\nRevaluation Reserve: €100,000 (700,000 - 600,000)\nBal c/d: €700,000\n(Note: Acc dep on buildings to 01/01/2007 = 360,000 x 2% x 4 = 28,800; written off against Reval Reserve)\n\n2008:\nBal b/d: €700,000\nDisposal (land): €270,000\nBal c/d: €430,000\n\n2009:\nBal b/d: €430,000\nBank (buildings): €500,000\nBank (contractor): €100,000\nWages (capitalised): €50,000\nBal c/d: €1,080,000\n\n2010:\nBal b/d: €1,080,000\nRevaluation Reserve: €108,000 (1,188,000 - 1,080,000)\nBal c/d: €1,188,000\n(Note: must account for dep charged in 2007-2009 on buildings before revaluing)\n\n2011:\nBal b/d: €1,188,000\nDisposal: amount attributable to 2007 buildings\nRemaining revalued: €950,000\nBal c/d: €950,000\n\nPROVISION FOR DEPRECIATION ACCOUNT:\n01/01/2007: Bal b/d €28,800 (360,000 x 2% x 4 years)\nRevaluation: Written off €28,800 against Revaluation Reserve\nBalance after reval: NIL\n\n2007: P&L charge: (700,000 - 270,000) x 2% = 430,000 x 2% = €8,600\nBal c/d: €8,600\n\n2008: P&L charge: 430,000 x 2% = €8,600 (buildings element unchanged)\nBal c/d: €17,200\n\n2009: P&L charge: 430,000 x 2% + 650,000 x 2% = 8,600 + 13,000 = €21,600\n(New buildings: 500,000 + 100,000 + 50,000 = 650,000)\nBal c/d: €38,800\n\n01/01/2010: Revaluation - write off accumulated dep €38,800 against Revaluation Reserve\nBalance after reval: NIL\n2010: P&L charge on revalued buildings:\nOld building revalued: 430,000 x 1.10 = 473,000; buildings element = 473,000 - 0 land = 473,000? No.\nOld property (buildings only, land gone): 430,000 x 1.10 = 473,000\nNew buildings: 650,000 x 1.10 = 715,000\nTotal revalued: 473,000 + 715,000 = 1,188,000\nDep 2010: 1,188,000 x 2% = €23,760\nBal c/d: €23,760\n\n2011:\nDisposal - dep on sold building: 473,000 x 2% x 1 year (2010 only) = €9,460? No, dep was on total.\nDep attributable to old building: (473,000/1,188,000) x 23,760 = €9,460 (approx)\nRemaining buildings dep charge 2011: 715,000 x 2% = €14,300? Or revalued to 950,000.\nIf sold on 01/01/2011 (no dep in year of disposal):\nDep on remaining: after reval to 950,000, dep = 950,000 x 2% = €19,000\nBal c/d: €19,000\n\nDISPOSAL OF LAND (2008):\nDr: L&B €270,000\nCr: Bank €320,000\nCr: Revaluation Reserve €30,000 (270,000 - 240,000 original cost = 30,000 from reval reserve)\nProfit on disposal: 320,000 - 270,000 = €50,000 to P&L\n\nDISPOSAL OF BUILDINGS (2011):\nCost (revalued): €473,000\nAcc dep: €9,460\nNBV: €463,540\nSold for: €400,000\nLoss on disposal: €63,540 to P&L\n(Revaluation reserve transfer may apply for unrealised element)\n\n(d)(i) BALANCE SHEET EXTRACT 31/12/2011:\nLand and Buildings (revalued): €950,000\nLess Accumulated Depreciation: (€19,000)\nNBV: €931,000\n\n(d)(ii) Factors in annual depreciation charge:\n1. Cost or valuation of the asset\n2. Estimated useful economic life of the asset\n3. Estimated residual/scrap value at end of useful life\n4. Method of depreciation chosen (straight-line, reducing balance, etc.)"},

    {num:4, label:"Q4: Farm Accounts (Mike and Anne Byrne)",
     q:"Assets and liabilities of Mike and Anne Byrne (mixed farming) on 01/01/2011:\nLand and Buildings at cost: €405,000; Vehicles and Machinery at cost: €85,000\nElectricity due: €360; Value of Cattle: €70,000; Value of Sheep: €15,000\nMilk cheque due: €1,500; Stock of Fuel: €740\n\nLodgements: Balance 01/01 €28,100; Milk €27,000; Sheep €22,000; Cattle €15,000; Lambs €12,600; Calves €6,000; Single Payment Sheep €2,300; Single Payment Cattle €3,200; Wool €1,200; Forestry premium €1,700; 6 months interest from 3% Investment Bond €1,200; Balance 31/12 €35,800.\n\nCheque Payments: Fertiliser €2,400; General farm expenses €12,100; Dairy wages €2,500; Sheep €19,000; Cattle €13,000; Light/heat/fuel €2,400; Machinery €6,000; Repairs €5,400; Vet fees €2,620; Bank Loan plus 18 months interest at 4% = €19,080.\n\nClosing values: Cattle €78,000; Sheep €22,000\nDrawings: Milk €850; Lamb €480\nApportionment: General expenses, fertiliser, vet = 60% cattle/40% sheep\nOther expenses: 75% farm, 25% household\nDepreciation: Machinery 10% of cost; Buildings 2% (land €200,000)\nVet includes family health insurance €1,090\nClosing: Milk cheque due €1,400; Fertiliser creditors €430; Fuel stock €500\n\nRequired:\n(a) Statement of Capital 01/01/2011 (20 marks)\n(b) Enterprise Analysis: Cattle & Milk, Sheep (20 marks)\n(c) General P&L Account (12 marks)\n(d) Drawings Account (5 marks)\n(e) Other purposes of farm accounts (3 marks)",
     a:"(a) STATEMENT OF CAPITAL 01/01/2011:\nAssets:\nLand & Buildings: €405,000\nVehicles & Machinery: €85,000\nCattle: €70,000\nSheep: €15,000\nMilk cheque due: €1,500\nFuel stock: €740\n3% Investment Bond: €80,000 (1,200 x 2 = 2,400 p.a.; 2,400/0.03 = 80,000)\nBank: €28,100\nTotal Assets: €685,340\nLess Liabilities:\nElectricity due: €360\nBank Loan: €18,000 (see workings below)\nTotal Liabilities: €18,360\nCapital: €666,980\n\nLoan workings: Repayment = €19,080 includes 18 months interest at 4%.\nLoan + 18 months interest = 19,080\nLoan + Loan x 4% x 18/12 = 19,080\nLoan x (1 + 0.06) = 19,080\nLoan = 19,080/1.06 = €18,000\nInterest = 19,080 - 18,000 = €1,080\n\n(b) ENTERPRISE ANALYSIS:\n\nCATTLE AND MILK:\nIncome:\nMilk: 27,000 - 1,500 + 1,400 = €26,900\nCattle: €15,000\nCalves: €6,000\nDrawings milk: €850\nSingle payment cattle: €3,200\nIncrease in cattle: 78,000 - 70,000 = €8,000\nTotal Income: €59,950\n\nExpenditure:\nDairy wages: €2,500\nFertiliser: (2,400 + 430) x 60% = €1,698\nGeneral expenses: 12,100 x 60% = €7,260\nCattle: €13,000\nVet: (2,620 - 1,090) x 60% = €918\nTotal: €25,376\nGross Profit Cattle & Milk: €34,574\n\nSHEEP:\nIncome:\nSheep: €22,000\nLambs: €12,600\nWool: €1,200\nDrawings lamb: €480\nSingle payment sheep: €2,300\nIncrease in sheep: 22,000 - 15,000 = €7,000\nTotal Income: €45,580\n\nExpenditure:\nFertiliser: (2,400 + 430) x 40% = €1,132\nGeneral expenses: 12,100 x 40% = €4,840\nSheep: €19,000\nVet: (2,620 - 1,090) x 40% = €612\nTotal: €25,584\nGross Profit Sheep: €19,996\n\n(c) GENERAL P&L ACCOUNT:\nGross Profit Cattle & Milk: €34,574\nGross Profit Sheep: €19,996\nForestry premium: €1,700\nInvestment income: 80,000 x 3% = €2,400\nTotal: €58,670\n\nLess Expenses:\nLight/heat/fuel: (2,400 + 500 - 740) x 75% = €1,620 (adjust for fuel stocks; less private 25%)\nRepairs: 5,400 x 75% = €4,050\nDepreciation machinery: (85,000 + 6,000) x 10% = €9,100\nDepreciation buildings: (405,000 - 200,000) x 2% = €4,100\nInterest: 1,080 x 75% = €810 (75% farm)\nTotal Expenses: €19,680\nNet Profit: €38,990\n\n(d) DRAWINGS ACCOUNT:\nMilk: €850\nLamb: €480\nLight/heat private: (2,400 + 500 - 740) x 25% = €540\nRepairs private: 5,400 x 25% = €1,350\nHealth insurance: €1,090\nInterest private: 1,080 x 25% = €270\nTotal Drawings: €4,580\n\n(e) Other purposes of preparing farm accounts:\n1. To comply with Revenue/taxation requirements\n2. To provide information for bank/loan applications\n3. To compare performance between enterprises and identify most profitable activities\n4. To plan and budget for future farming activities"},

    {num:5, label:"Q5: Interpretation of Accounts (Glas plc)",
     q:"Glas plc, manufacturer in renewable energy. Authorised capital €850,000 (600,000 ordinary at €1 each + 250,000 6% preference at €1 each). Issued: 300,000 ordinary + all preference.\n\n2011 P&L:\nSales: €790,000; Opening Stock: €45,000; Closing Stock: €50,000\nCost of Goods Sold: €595,000; Gross Profit: €195,000\nOperating expenses: €132,000; Net Profit: €63,000\nInterest: €20,000; Dividends paid: €25,000\nRetained: €18,000; P&L b/f: €39,000; P&L c/d: €57,000\n\nBalance Sheet 31/12/2011:\nFixed Assets: €650,000; Investments (MV €210,000): €200,000\nCurrent Assets: €105,000\nTrade Creditors: €98,000; Net Current: €7,000\n8% Debentures (2018): €250,000\nOrdinary Shares: €300,000; 6% Pref Shares: €250,000; P&L: €57,000\nMarket Value per ordinary share: €1.15\n\nProjected 2012 Ratios: EPS 7c; DPS 4.2c; Interest Cover 3; Quick Ratio 0.85:1; P/E 16; ROCE 8.1%; Gearing 51%\n\nRequired:\n(a)(i) Cash purchases if credit period from creditors is 2.5 months\n(a)(ii) Interest Cover\n(a)(iii) Dividend Cover\n(a)(iv) How long for one share to recover its value at present payout rate\n(a)(v) Projected market value per share 2012\n(b) Comment on performance, state of affairs, prospects\n(c)(i) Gross profit % for 2011\n(c)(ii) Five explanations for change from 2010 (36%)",
     a:"(a) CALCULATIONS:\n\n(i) Cash Purchases:\nCredit purchases = Trade Creditors x 12/credit months = 98,000 x 12/2.5 = €470,400\nTotal purchases = Opening Stock + Purchases - Closing Stock = Cost of Sales\n=> Purchases = 595,000 - 45,000 + 50,000 = €600,000\nCash Purchases = 600,000 - 470,400 = €129,600\n\n(ii) Interest Cover = Net Profit before Interest / Interest\n= (63,000 + 20,000) / 20,000 = 83,000 / 20,000 = 4.15 times\n\n(iii) Dividend Cover:\nEPS = (63,000 - 15,000) / 300,000 = 48,000 / 300,000 = 16c\n(Preference dividend = 250,000 x 6% = 15,000)\nDPS = (25,000 - 15,000) / 300,000 = 10,000 / 300,000 = 3.33c\nDividend Cover = 16 / 3.33 = 4.8 times\n(OR: (63,000 - 15,000) / (25,000 - 15,000) = 48,000 / 10,000 = 4.8 times)\n\n(iv) Recovery period:\nMarket value = €1.15; DPS = 3.33c\nYears to recover = 1.15 / 0.0333 = 34.5 years (approx 35 years)\n\n(v) Projected market value 2012:\nP/E ratio = 16; EPS = 7c\nMarket value = 16 x 7c = €1.12\n\n(b) COMMENTARY:\nProfitability: ROCE 2011 = 83,000 / (607,000 + 250,000) x 100 = 83,000 / 857,000 = 9.69%\nProjected ROCE 2012 = 8.1% - declining, which is concerning.\nInterest cover falls from 4.15 to 3 times - still adequate but decreasing.\n\nDividend Policy: Dividend cover 4.8 times in 2011 shows conservative payout. DPS projected to increase from 3.33c to 4.2c - positive for shareholders. P/E increases from 7.19 (1.15/0.16) to 16 - market expects growth.\n\nLiquidity: Current ratio 2011 = 105,000/98,000 = 1.07:1, barely adequate. Quick ratio projected at 0.85:1 (below ideal 1:1) - liquidity concern. The company is heavily geared at 51% projected.\n\nOverall: Shareholders should be cautiously optimistic. Share price projected to fall slightly (€1.15 to €1.12) but dividend increases. Liquidity and gearing need monitoring.\n\n(c)(i) Gross Profit % 2011 = 195,000/790,000 x 100 = 24.68%\n\n(c)(ii) GP% fell from 36% (2010) to 24.68% (2011). Five explanations:\n1. Increase in cost of raw materials/energy costs not passed on to customers\n2. Reduction in selling prices to remain competitive\n3. Increase in direct labour costs\n4. Change in sales mix towards lower-margin products\n5. Stock write-downs or increased wastage/pilferage\n6. Increased competition in renewable energy sector forcing price reductions"},

    {num:6, label:"Q4: Club Accounts (Tallones Basketball Club)",
     q:"(From 2013 paper - moved here for coverage) Tallones Basketball Club assets and liabilities 01/01/2012:\nClubhouse and Arena: €680,000; Bar stock: €2,200; Equipment cost: €23,000\nLife membership: €25,000; Bar Debtors: €421; Bar Creditors: €1,600\nLevy Reserve Fund: €12,000; Wages due: €2,800; Subscriptions in advance: €1,400\n\nReceipts & Payments 2012:\nReceipts: Bank b/f €11,300; Interest from 4% Govt Investments €900; Arena rent €15,000; Catering receipts €12,400; Annual sponsorship €73,000; Subscriptions €65,000; Bar receipts €42,410; Lotto receipts €52,500\nPayments: Bar purchases €28,700; Catering costs €9,900; Sundry expenses €103,600; Equipment €41,000; Coaching expenses €4,600; Repayment of €40,000 loan on 31/05/2012 with 16 months interest €44,800; Transfer to Building Society 31/12/2012 €28,000; Lotto costs €7,600; Balance 31/12 €4,310\n\nAdditional info:\n1. Bar stock 31/12/2012: €2,450\n2. Equipment dep 20% of cost\n3. Clubhouse dep 2%\n4. Bar debtors €190; bar creditors €1,330 at 31/12/2012\n5. Investment interest due 31/12/2012: €300\n6. Subscriptions include: 5 life memberships (total now 30); subs for 2013 €2,400; levy 2012 €80 x 150 members; levy 2011 €80 x 15 members\n7. Life membership credited to income over 5 years from 2012",
     a:"(a) ACCUMULATED FUND 01/01/2012:\nAssets:\nClubhouse & Arena: €680,000\nEquipment: €23,000\nBar Stock: €2,200\nBar Debtors: €421\n4% Government Investments: €22,500 (900/0.04 = 22,500)\nBank: €11,300\nSubscriptions due (from levy 2011): 15 x 80 = €1,200\nTotal Assets: €740,621\n\nLess Liabilities:\nBar Creditors: €1,600\nWages due: €2,800\nSubscriptions in advance: €1,400\nLife Membership Fund: €25,000\nLevy Reserve Fund: €12,000\nLoan: €40,000\nLoan interest due: 40,000 x 0.04/12 x 4 = ? (16 months interest = 4,800; so rate = 4,800/(40,000 x 16/12) = 9%. Interest due at 01/01/2012 = need to determine when loan interest starts. Loan repaid 31/05/2012 with 16 months interest. So interest runs from 01/02/2011 to 31/05/2012. At 01/01/2012: 11 months accrued.)\nLoan interest rate: 44,800 - 40,000 = 4,800 for 16 months. Annual rate = 4,800/40,000 x 12/16 = 9%.\nInterest due 01/01/2012: 40,000 x 9% x 11/12 = €3,300\nTotal Liabilities: €86,100\nAccumulated Fund: €654,521\n\n(b) INCOME AND EXPENDITURE ACCOUNT 2012:\nINCOME:\nSubscriptions: 65,000 - 2,400 (advance) - 1,200 (levy 2011) - 5 x Life mem (say €5,000) - 12,000 (levy 2012) + 1,400 (advance from 2011) = €45,800 (ordinary subs)\nLife membership income: (25,000 + 5,000)/5 = €6,000\nLevy income 2012: 150 x 80 = €12,000\nSponsorship: €73,000\nArena rent: €15,000\nInvestment interest: 900 + 300 - 0 = €1,200 (900 received + 300 due = annual income of 22,500 x 4% x ... or just 900 + 300 adjustment)\nLotto profit: 52,500 - 7,600 = €44,900\nCatering profit: 12,400 - 9,900 = €2,500\n\nBar Trading Account:\nBar receipts: 42,410 - 421 + 190 = €42,179 (adjust for debtors)\nOpening stock: €2,200\nPurchases: 28,700 + 1,600 - 1,330 = €28,970 (adjust for creditors? Or: 28,700 - 1,600 + 1,330 = 28,430)\nPurchases: 28,700 + 1,330 - 1,600 = €28,430\nClosing stock: (€2,450)\nCost of bar sales: €28,180\nBar profit: 42,179 - 28,180 = €13,999\n\nTotal Income: €214,399\n\nEXPENDITURE:\nSundry expenses: €103,600\nCoaching: €4,600\nWages: adjust for wages due\nLoan interest: 40,000 x 9% x 5/12 = €1,500 (Jan-May 2012)\nDepreciation equipment: (23,000 + 41,000) x 20% = €12,800\nDepreciation clubhouse: 680,000 x 2% = €13,600\nTotal Expenditure: €136,100\n\nSurplus for year: €78,299\n\n(c)(i) Special Purpose P&L Account: A profit and loss account prepared for a specific activity within an organisation (e.g. Bar Trading Account, Catering Account, Lotto Account). It shows whether that particular activity is profitable or making a loss, separate from the main Income and Expenditure Account.\n\n(c)(ii) Arguments against the Treasurer's proposal:\n1. The levy increase of €120 per member per year is a significant burden on members\n2. The total raised (150 x €200 x 5 = €150,000) exactly covers costs but leaves no contingency\n3. The club already has €28,000 in Building Society + €12,000 levy reserve = €40,000 available\n4. Alternative: apply for a sports capital grant or seek corporate sponsorship\n5. A bank loan at current low interest rates may be more equitable than burdening members"}
  ],

  "2013": [
    {num:1, label:"Q1: Final Accounts - Manufacturing Company (Marjam Ltd)",
     q:"Marjam Ltd has an Authorised Capital of €1,800,000 divided into 1,000,000 Ordinary Shares at €1 each and 800,000 10% Preference Shares at €1 each.\n\nTrial Balance at 31/12/2012:\nFactory Buildings (cost €880,000): €820,000\nPlant & Machinery (cost €270,000): €180,000\nDiscount (Net): €8,400\nP&L Balance 01/01/2012: €18,100\nStocks 01/01/2012: FG €69,500, RM €34,400, WIP €23,700\nDebenture interest (first 3 months): €4,800\n4% Investments 01/01/2012: €340,000\nPatents: €22,600\nPurchases RM: €1,035,000; Sales: €1,540,000\nDividends paid: €59,000; Debtors: €102,600; Creditors: €98,200\nBank: €41,000\n9% Debentures: €200,000; USC: €2,100\nIssued Ordinary: €600,000; 10% Preference: €500,000\nDirect factory wages: €180,400\nGeneral factory overheads: €31,400\nHire of special equipment: €6,100\nSale of scrap: €3,000; Carriage on RM: €4,600\nAdmin expenses (inc Suspense): €27,600\nSelling expenses (inc 3 months investment income): €32,900\n\nAdjustments:\n(i) Closing stocks: FG €79,400 (inc stock cost €4,400, NRV €2,500), RM €35,700, WIP €27,400\n(ii) FG sent to customer on sale-or-return on Dec 31 treated as credit sale. Retail price €6,000 = cost + 20%\n(iii) Repairs to P&M €2,000 by employee: €400 parts from stock, €1,600 wages. No entry made.\n(iv) P&M dep 10% of cost (purchase to sale). Machine cost €20,000 on 30/06/2006 traded in 31/03/2012 for new €51,000. Allowance €8,000. Net treated as purchase of RM.\n(v) Suspense: incorrect debenture interest (correct in bank); returns €1,000 entered only in debtors a/c\n(vi) Bank: firm shows €41,000; statement shows €39,720 OD. Cheque €680 entered as €860; credit transfer €800 (20c in €1 from bankrupt debtor); unpresented cheque €300 (director fees)\n(vii) Provide for investment income due, debenture interest due, BDP at 4% of debtors",
     a:"MANUFACTURING ACCOUNT:\nOpening Stock RM: €34,400\nPurchases RM: 1,035,000 - 43,000 - 400 = €991,600\n(Remove machine net: 51,000 - 8,000 = 43,000; remove parts for repairs: 400)\nCarriage on RM: €4,600\nClosing Stock RM: (€35,700)\nCost of RM Consumed: €994,900\n\nDirect Factory Wages: 180,400 - 1,600 = €178,800\n(Remove wages for P&M repairs: 1,600)\nPrime Cost: €1,173,700\n\nFactory Overheads:\nGeneral factory overheads: €31,400\nHire of special equipment: €6,100\nRepairs to P&M: €2,000 (400 parts + 1,600 wages)\nDepreciation P&M: 500 + 29,100 = €29,600\n(Old machine: 20,000 x 10% x 3/12 = 500 for 2012\nRemaining machines: (270,000 - 20,000) x 10% = 25,000\nNew machine: 51,000 x 10% x 9/12 = 3,825? Or full year for new?\nUsing purchase to sale: 10% of cost from date of purchase.\nNew machine from 31/03/2012: 51,000 x 10% x 9/12 = €3,825\nOr if full year in year of acquisition: 51,000 x 10% = 5,100\nBased on 'from date of purchase': 51,000 x 10% x 9/12 = 3,825\nTotal: 500 + 25,000 + 3,825 = 29,325 OR 500 + 25,000 + 5,100 = 30,600)\nLoss on disposal of machine:\nAcc dep on old machine: 20,000 x 10% x 5.75 years (01/07/2006 to 31/03/2012) = €11,500\nNBV = 20,000 - 11,500 = €8,500\nAllowance = €8,000\nLoss on disposal = 8,500 - 8,000 = €500\nDepreciation factory buildings: 880,000 x 2%? No - no building dep rate given in adjustments. Check paper: buildings show cost 880,000, TB figure 820,000 so acc dep = 60,000. No specific building dep instruction in adjustments.\nFactory building dep: (880,000 - 820,000 = 60,000 acc dep already). No instruction to charge further dep on buildings in this question.\n\nTotal Factory Overheads: 31,400 + 6,100 + 2,000 + 29,600 + 500 = €69,600\n\nOpening WIP: €23,700\nClosing WIP: (€27,400)\nSale of Scrap: (€3,000)\nCost of Manufacture: €1,236,600\n\nTRADING ACCOUNT:\nSales: 1,540,000 - 6,000 = €1,534,000\n(Remove sale-or-return)\nOpening Stock FG: €69,500\nCost of Manufacture: €1,236,600\nClosing Stock FG: 79,400 - 1,900 + 5,000 = €82,500\n(Damaged: 4,400 - 2,500 = 1,900 write-down; add back sale-or-return at cost: 6,000/1.20 = 5,000)\nCost of Sales: €1,223,600\nGross Profit: €310,400\n\nP&L ACCOUNT:\nExpenses:\nAdmin expenses: 27,600 - suspense adjustment\nSuspense analysis:\n- Deb int paid €4,800 for 3 months. Correct = 200,000 x 9% x 3/12 = €4,500. Bank correct, deb int a/c shows 4,800. Suspense Dr €300.\n- Returns €1,000 entered only in debtors (Cr debtors 1,000). Should also Dr Returns Inwards 1,000 OR Dr Purchases Returns... The paper says 'returns €1,000 entered only in debtors account'. This means returns inwards (sales returns) credited to debtors but not debited to returns inwards. Cr Suspense €1,000.\nSuspense: Dr 300, Cr 1,000 => net Cr 700. Suspense was included in admin expenses.\nAdmin expenses: 27,600 + 700 = €28,300\n\nSelling expenses: 32,900 + inv income (3 months) = selling exp exc inv income\nInv income for 3 months = 340,000 x 4% x 3/12 = €3,400\nSelling expenses: 32,900 + 3,400 = €36,300 (add back inv income credited to selling)\nActually: selling expenses 'incorporating 3 months investment income' means inv income was CREDITED to selling expenses, reducing it. So actual selling = 32,900 + 3,400 = €36,300.\nDiscount: €8,400\nSales Returns: €1,000\nBad debt (bankrupt): 800/0.20 = 4,000 total debt; written off 4,000 x 80% = €3,200\nBDP: (102,600 - 1,000 - 6,000 - 4,000) x 4% = 91,600 x 4% = €3,664\nTotal Expenses: 28,300 + 36,300 + 8,400 + 1,000 + 3,200 + 3,664 = €80,864\n\nInvestment Income: 340,000 x 4% = €13,600\nDebenture Interest: 200,000 x 9% = €18,000\n\nNet Profit: 310,400 - 80,864 + 13,600 - 18,000 = €225,136\nP&L b/f: €18,100\nDividends: (€59,000)\nP&L balance 31/12/2012: €184,236\n\nBALANCE SHEET:\nFactory Buildings: Cost €880,000; Acc Dep €60,000; NBV €820,000\nP&M: Cost 270,000 - 20,000 + 51,000 = €301,000; Acc Dep 90,000 - 11,500 + 29,600 = €108,100; NBV €192,900\n(Acc dep b/f = 270,000 - 180,000 = 90,000)\nPatents: €22,600\nInvestments: €340,000\nStocks: RM 35,700 + WIP 27,400 + FG 82,500 = €145,600\nDebtors: 102,600 - 1,000 - 6,000 - 4,000 = €91,600; BDP: €3,664\nInv income due: 13,600 - 3,400 = €10,200\nBank: If TB €41,000 is overdraft:\n41,000 + 180 - 800 = €40,380 OD (corrected: 41,000 - (860-680) + cheque correction - credit transfer)\nActual: Bank per books = 41,000 (OD since statement shows OD)\n41,000 - 180 (cheque overcredited: was 860, should be 680, so reduce OD by 180) - 800 (credit transfer received) = €40,020 OD\nCheck: Statement 39,720 + unpresented 300 = 40,020. Correct!\nCreditors: 98,200 - 180 = €98,020 (cheque correction reduces creditors)\nDeb interest due: 18,000 - 4,800 = €13,200\nDirector fees unpresented: €300 (already in books)"},

    {num:2, label:"Q2: Depreciation of Fixed Assets (Blue Haulage Ltd)",
     q:"Blue Haulage Ltd depreciates vehicles at 15% of cost per annum (purchase to sale date).\n\nOn 01/01/2011 owned:\nVehicle No. 1: purchased 01/01/2007 for €50,000\nVehicle No. 2: purchased 01/04/2008 for €60,000\nVehicle No. 3: purchased 01/09/2009 for €70,000\n\nOn 01/09/2011: Vehicle No. 1 traded in for €20,000 against new vehicle €75,000. Vehicle No. 1 had refrigeration unit fitted 01/01/2009 costing €15,000, depreciated at 30% for first 2 years then at 15% (same as vehicle).\n\nOn 01/04/2012: Vehicle No. 3 crashed, traded in against new €86,000. Insurance compensation €25,000. P&L shows profit on disposal of €1,125.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Vehicles Account 2011 and 2012\n(b) Provision for Depreciation Account 2011 and 2012\n(c) Vehicles Disposal Account\n(d)(i) Why charge depreciation?\n(d)(ii) Why choose one method over another?",
     a:"VEHICLES ACCOUNT:\n2011:\nDr: Bal b/d €195,000 (50+15+60+70); Bank €75,000 (new vehicle)\nCr: Disposal €65,000 (Veh 1: 50,000 + fridge 15,000); Bal c/d €205,000\n\n2012:\nDr: Bal b/d €205,000; Bank €86,000\nCr: Disposal €70,000 (Veh 3); Bal c/d €221,000\n\nPROVISION FOR DEPRECIATION:\nBal b/d 01/01/2011:\nVeh 1: 50,000 x 15% x 4 yrs = €30,000\nFridge: 15,000 x 30% x 2 yrs = €9,000\nVeh 2: 60,000 x 15% x 2.75 yrs (01/04/2008 to 01/01/2011) = €24,750\nVeh 3: 70,000 x 15% x 1.33 yrs (01/09/2009 to 01/01/2011) = €14,000\nTotal b/d: €77,750\n\n2011 Depreciation charge:\nVeh 1: 50,000 x 15% x 8/12 = €5,000 (Jan-Aug)\nFridge: 15,000 x 15% x 8/12 = €1,500 (now at 15% rate, Jan-Aug)\nVeh 2: 60,000 x 15% = €9,000\nVeh 3: 70,000 x 15% = €10,500\nNew vehicle: 75,000 x 15% x 4/12 = €3,750 (Sep-Dec)\nTotal 2011 charge: €29,750\n\nDisposal (Veh 1 + fridge):\nVeh 1 acc dep: 30,000 + 5,000 = €35,000\nFridge acc dep: 9,000 + 1,500 = €10,500\nTotal removed: €45,500\n\nBal c/d 31/12/2011: 77,750 + 29,750 - 45,500 = €62,000\n\n2012 Depreciation charge:\nVeh 2: 60,000 x 15% = €9,000\nVeh 3: 70,000 x 15% x 3/12 = €2,625 (Jan-Mar)\nNew (75k): 75,000 x 15% = €11,250\nNew (86k): 86,000 x 15% x 9/12 = €9,675 (Apr-Dec)\nTotal 2012 charge: €32,550\n\nDisposal (Veh 3):\nAcc dep Veh 3: 14,000 + 10,500 + 2,625 = €27,125\nRemoved: €27,125\n\nBal c/d 31/12/2012: 62,000 + 32,550 - 27,125 = €67,425\n\nDISPOSAL ACCOUNT - Vehicle 1 (2011):\nDr: Cost €65,000 (50,000 + 15,000)\nCr: Acc Dep €45,500; Trade-in €20,000\nLoss on disposal: 65,000 - 45,500 - 20,000 = -€500 (profit)\nActually: NBV = 65,000 - 45,500 = 19,500; sold for 20,000 => Profit €500\n\nDISPOSAL ACCOUNT - Vehicle 3 (2012):\nDr: Cost €70,000\nCr: Acc Dep €27,125; Insurance €25,000; Trade-in allowance (86,000 - bank paid)\nNBV = 70,000 - 27,125 = €42,875\nProfit on disposal = €1,125 (given in question)\nSo proceeds = 42,875 + 1,125 = €44,000\nInsurance = €25,000; trade-in = 44,000 - 25,000 = €19,000\nBank payment for new vehicle = 86,000 - 19,000 = €67,000\n\n(d)(i) Depreciation is charged to: match the cost of using the asset against the revenue it generates (matching concept); show a true and fair view of profit; show the asset at its correct value on the balance sheet; comply with accounting standards; set aside funds for replacement.\n\n(d)(ii) A company may choose straight-line if the asset provides equal benefit each year (e.g. buildings). Reducing balance may be chosen for assets that lose more value in early years (e.g. vehicles, technology). The choice depends on the nature of the asset and how its economic benefits are consumed over time."},

    {num:3, label:"Q3: Tabular Statement (Cooper Ltd)",
     q:"Cooper Ltd balance sheet on 01/01/2012:\nGoodwill (cost €65,000): €45,000\nLand & Buildings: €727,000 (dep €54,000); Delivery Vans: €90,000 (dep €40,000)\nStock: €91,600; Debtors: €55,800\nCreditors: €75,400; Bank: €15,800 (OD); Expenses due: €4,200\nIssued: 600,000 ordinary shares at €1; Share Premium: €80,000; P&L: €140,000\n\nTransactions 2012:\nJan: Bought adjoining business - buildings €350,000, delivery vans €70,000, stock €25,000, creditors €30,000. Paid by issuing 380,000 shares at premium of 20c.\nFeb: Creditor owed €8,900 accepted delivery van (BV €9,500, cost €14,500) in settlement.\nApril: Revalued L&B at €1,200,000 (land €180,000) on 01/04/2012.\nMay: Bank statement: direct debit €8,400 advertising for year ending 31/08/2012; credit transfer €9,900 for 11 months rent from 01/03/2012.\nJune: Van cost €20,000 (dep €17,000) traded for new €30,000, allowance €5,500.\nAug: €2,400 received from debtor E. Galvin (debt previously written off). This = 80% of original debt, remainder due Jan 2013. Goods sold on credit to Galvin for €450 (cost + 25% mark-up).\nDec: Building dep 2% of value at 01/04/2012. Van dep charge for year: €28,000.",
     a:"TABULAR STATEMENT KEY ENTRIES:\n\nJanuary - Business Acquisition:\nL&B: +350,000\nVans: +70,000\nStock: +25,000\nCreditors: +30,000\nShare Capital: +380,000\nShare Premium: +76,000 (380,000 x 0.20)\nGoodwill: purchase price - net assets = (380,000 + 76,000) - (350,000 + 70,000 + 25,000 - 30,000) = 456,000 - 415,000 = €41,000\n\nFebruary - Van given to creditor:\nVans (cost): -14,500\nVan dep: -5,000 (14,500 - 9,500)\nCreditors: -8,900\nLoss on disposal: 9,500 - 8,900 = €600 (reduces P&L)\n\nApril - Revaluation:\nL&B increases to €1,200,000\nOriginal L&B: 727,000 + 350,000 = 1,077,000\nAcc dep: 54,000 + 3 months dep (1,077,000 - 180,000 land) x 2% x 3/12? Actually revaluation is at 01/04 so:\nRevaluation surplus: 1,200,000 - (1,077,000 - 54,000) = 1,200,000 - 1,023,000 = €177,000\nOr: 1,200,000 - 1,077,000 = 123,000 surplus + 54,000 dep written off = €177,000 to Reval Reserve\nL&B set to 1,200,000; Acc Dep L&B written off to zero.\n\nMay:\nBank: -8,400 (advertising); +9,900 (rent)\nAdvertising expense: 8,400 (of which 8/12 relates to current year and 4/12 prepaid)\nRent income: 9,900 (of which 9/11 relates to 2012, 2/11 to 2013 = prepaid income)\nActually: advertising for year ending 31/08/2012 = 12 months to Aug 2012. From bank statement date (May), this covers prior period. Treat 8,400 as full expense but 4 months prepaid (Sep-Dec = after 31/08). Advertising expense 2012: 8,400 x 8/12 = 5,600. Prepaid: 2,800.\nRent: 11 months from 01/03/2012 to 31/01/2013. In 2012: 10 months (Mar-Dec). Prepaid income: 1 month.\nRent 2012: 9,900 x 10/11 = €9,000. Prepaid: 900.\n\nJune - Van traded:\nVans (cost): -20,000 + 30,000 = +10,000\nVan dep: -17,000 (remove old); bank: -(30,000-5,500) = -24,500\nLoss/profit: NBV = 20,000 - 17,000 = 3,000. Allowance 5,500. Profit = €2,500.\n\nAugust - Bad debt recovered:\nBank: +2,400\nDebtors: +600 (remaining 20% of 3,000 original debt) + 450 (new sale)\nBad debt recovered: 2,400 + 600 = €3,000 (income)\nStock: -360 (cost of goods sold to Galvin: 450/1.25 = 360)\n\nDecember - Depreciation:\nBuilding dep: 1,200,000 x 2% x 9/12 = €18,000 (from 01/04 revaluation to 31/12)\nOr: full year on revalued amount = 1,200,000 x 2% = 24,000? The question says 2% of value at 01/04/2012.\nIf from date of revaluation (9 months): €18,000\nVan dep charge: €28,000 (given)\n\nCLOSING BALANCES:\nGoodwill: 45,000 + 41,000 = €86,000\nL&B: €1,200,000; Acc Dep: €18,000; NBV: €1,182,000\nVans: 90,000 + 70,000 - 14,500 - 20,000 + 30,000 = €155,500\nVan Dep: 40,000 - 5,000 - 17,000 + 28,000 = €46,000; Van NBV: €109,500\nStock: 91,600 + 25,000 - 360 = €116,240 (plus trading adjustments)\nDebtors: 55,800 + 600 + 450 = €56,850\nCreditors: 75,400 + 30,000 - 8,900 = €96,500\nBank: -15,800 + 24,500 (van) - 8,400 + 9,900 + 2,400 = €(+) adjust\nShare Capital: 600,000 + 380,000 = €980,000\nShare Premium: 80,000 + 76,000 = €156,000\nRevaluation Reserve: €177,000"},

    {num:4, label:"Q5: Interpretation of Accounts (Dantzig plc)",
     q:"Dantzig plc, food processing manufacturer. Authorised capital €700,000 (600,000 ordinary at €1 each + 100,000 5% preference at €1 each). Issued: 550,000 ordinary + all preference.\n\n2012 P&L:\nSales: €990,000\nCost of Goods Sold: €852,000\nGross Profit: €138,000\nOperating expenses: €68,000 (includes depreciation €18,000)\nNet Profit: €70,000\nInterest: €7,500\nDividends paid (all): €33,000\nRetained: €29,500\nP&L b/f: €45,000\nP&L c/d: €74,500\n\nBalance Sheet 31/12/2012:\nFixed Assets NBV: €342,000\nGoodwill: €30,000; Investments (MV €120,000): €100,000\nCurrent Assets: Stock €62,000; Debtors €105,000; Bank €33,000 = €200,000\nCreditors due within 1 year: €72,500\nNet current assets: €127,500\n5% Debentures (2017): €150,000\nOrdinary Shares: €550,000; 5% Preference: €100,000\nRevaluation Reserve: €25,000; P&L: €74,500\n\nAdditional info:\nMarket value per ordinary share: 90c\nStock turnover: 13 times\nGP% in 2011 was 18%; operating expenses were 8% of turnover in 2011\n\nRequired:\n(a) Calculate: (i) Opening stock (ii) Dividend yield (iii) Return on Capital Employed (iv) Earnings per share (v) Acid test/quick ratio\n(b) Comment on profitability, dividend policy, liquidity (use ratios)\n(c) Advise on suitability of investment for retired person",
     a:"(a) CALCULATIONS:\n\n(i) Opening Stock:\nAverage Stock = COGS / Stock Turnover = 852,000 / 13 = €65,538\nOpening Stock = (Average Stock x 2) - Closing Stock = (65,538 x 2) - 62,000 = €69,077\n\n(ii) Dividend Yield:\nPreference dividend = 100,000 x 5% = €5,000\nOrdinary dividend = 33,000 - 5,000 = €28,000\nDPS = 28,000 / 550,000 = 5.09c\nDividend Yield = DPS / Market Price x 100 = 5.09 / 90 x 100 = 5.66%\n\n(iii) ROCE:\nCapital Employed = 550,000 + 100,000 + 25,000 + 74,500 + 150,000 = €899,500\n(OR Total Assets - Current Liabilities = 342,000 + 30,000 + 100,000 + 200,000 - 72,500 + 300,000? Use Equity + Long-term Debt)\nROCE = (Net Profit + Interest) / Capital Employed x 100\n= (70,000 + 7,500) / 899,500 x 100 = 77,500 / 899,500 = 8.62%\n\n(iv) EPS:\nEPS = (Net Profit - Preference Dividend) / Number of Ordinary Shares\n= (70,000 - 5,000) / 550,000 = 65,000 / 550,000 = 11.82c\n\n(v) Acid Test (Quick Ratio):\nQuick Assets = Current Assets - Stock = 200,000 - 62,000 = €138,000\nQuick Ratio = 138,000 / 72,500 = 1.90:1\n\n(b) COMMENTARY:\n\nProfitability:\nGross Profit % = 138,000/990,000 x 100 = 13.94% (down from 18% in 2011 - concerning decline)\nNet Profit % = 70,000/990,000 x 100 = 7.07%\nOperating expense ratio = 68,000/990,000 = 6.87% (improved from 8% in 2011)\nROCE = 8.62% - above debenture rate (5%) but modest. The company is controlling expenses better but gross margins are declining, possibly due to rising raw material costs in food processing.\n\nDividend Policy:\nDividend Cover = 65,000/28,000 = 2.32 times - adequate cover\nPayout ratio = 28,000/65,000 = 43.1% - reasonable, retaining 57% for growth\nP/E Ratio = 90/11.82 = 7.61 times - low, suggesting market has limited growth expectations\n\nLiquidity:\nCurrent Ratio = 200,000/72,500 = 2.76:1 - very strong\nAcid Test = 1.90:1 - well above ideal 1:1\nGearing = 150,000/899,500 = 16.7% - low gearing, conservative\nThe company has excellent liquidity and low borrowing risk.\n\n(c) ADVICE FOR RETIRED PERSON:\nSuitable investment because:\n- Steady dividend yield of 5.66% exceeds deposit rates\n- Low gearing (16.7%) means low risk of insolvency\n- Strong liquidity ratios provide security\n- Dividend cover of 2.32 times suggests dividend is sustainable\nConcerns:\n- Declining GP% (18% to 13.94%) could threaten future dividends\n- Share price below par (90c vs €1 nominal) shows market caution\n- Food processing is competitive - margins may continue falling\nOverall: suitable for income but monitor the declining profitability trend."},

    {num:5, label:"Q2: Depreciation (from 2013 - additional coverage)",
     q:"(See Q2 above for Blue Haulage Ltd - this covers the theory part)\n\nTheory questions on depreciation:\n(d)(i) Why does a company charge depreciation in calculating profit?\n(d)(ii) Why would a company choose one method of depreciation over another?",
     a:"(d)(i) Why charge depreciation:\n1. To comply with the matching/accruals concept - the cost of using a fixed asset should be matched against the revenue it helps generate in each accounting period\n2. To show a true and fair view of profit - without depreciation, profits would be overstated in the years the asset is used\n3. To show the asset at a realistic value (net book value) on the balance sheet\n4. To comply with accounting standards (FRS 15) which require depreciation to be charged on all fixed assets with a finite useful life\n5. To provide for the eventual replacement of the asset by spreading its cost over its useful life\n\n(d)(ii) Choice of depreciation method:\nStraight-line method: chosen when the asset provides equal benefit/service each year (e.g. buildings, office furniture). It gives a constant annual charge, making budgeting easier.\n\nReducing balance method: chosen when the asset loses more value in early years and the benefit from the asset decreases over time (e.g. vehicles, computers, technology). It gives higher charges in early years when maintenance costs are lower, resulting in a more even total cost (depreciation + repairs) each year.\n\nThe choice depends on:\n- The nature and type of asset\n- The pattern of economic benefits derived from the asset\n- Industry practice and accounting standards\n- Tax implications (capital allowances may differ from accounting depreciation)"},

    {num:6, label:"Q4: Club Accounts (Tallones Basketball Club)",
     q:"Tallones Basketball Club - included among assets/liabilities on 01/01/2012:\nClubhouse & Arena: €680,000; Bar stock: €2,200; Equipment (cost): €23,000\nLife membership: €25,000; Bar Debtors: €421; Bar Creditors: €1,600\nLevy Reserve Fund: €12,000; Wages due: €2,800; Subscriptions in advance: €1,400\n\nReceipts & Payments Account for year ended 31/12/2012:\nReceipts: Bank 01/01 €11,300; 4% Govt Investment Interest €900; Arena rent €15,000; Catering receipts €12,400; Annual sponsorship €73,000; Subscriptions €65,000; Bar receipts €42,410; Lotto receipts €52,500\nPayments: Bar purchases €28,700; Catering costs €9,900; Sundry expenses €103,600; Equipment €41,000; Coaching €4,600; Loan repayment (40,000 + 16 months interest) €44,800; Building Society transfer €28,000; Lotto costs €7,600; Balance 31/12 €4,310\n\nAdditional info:\n1. Bar stock 31/12/2012: €2,450\n2. Equipment depreciation: 20% of cost\n3. Clubhouse/Arena depreciation: 2%\n4. Bar debtors 31/12: €190; Bar creditors 31/12: €1,330\n5. Investment interest due 31/12: €300\n6. Subscriptions include: 5 life memberships (total life members now 30); subs for 2013 €2,400; levy 2012 €80 x 150 members; levy 2011 €80 x 15 members (arrears)\n7. Life membership credited to income over 5 years commencing 2012\n\nRequired:\n(a) Accumulated Fund on 01/01/2012 (25 marks)\n(b) Income and Expenditure Account for year ended 31/12/2012 (25 marks)\n(c)(i) Explain Special Purpose P&L Account with example\n(c)(ii) Arguments against levy increase proposal (10 marks)",
     a:"(a) ACCUMULATED FUND 01/01/2012:\nAssets:\nClubhouse & Arena: €680,000\nEquipment (cost): €23,000\nBar Stock: €2,200\nBar Debtors: €421\n4% Government Investments: €22,500 (900/0.04 = 22,500)\nBank: €11,300\nSubscriptions due (levy 2011 arrears): 15 x 80 = €1,200\nTotal Assets: €740,621\n\nLess Liabilities:\nBar Creditors: €1,600\nWages due: €2,800\nSubscriptions in advance: €1,400\nLife Membership Fund: €25,000\nLevy Reserve Fund: €12,000\nLoan: €40,000\nLoan interest due: Interest rate = (44,800 - 40,000)/(40,000 x 16/12) = 4,800/53,333 = 9%\nInterest accrued at 01/01/2012 (approx 11 months if loan from mid-2010): €3,300\nTotal Liabilities: €86,100\nAccumulated Fund: €654,521\n\n(b) INCOME AND EXPENDITURE ACCOUNT:\n\nBAR TRADING ACCOUNT:\nBar Sales: 42,410 - 421 + 190 = €42,179\nOpening Stock: 2,200\nPurchases: 28,700 - 1,600 + 1,330 = €28,430\nClosing Stock: (2,450)\nCost of Bar Sales: €28,180\nBar Profit: €13,999\n\nINCOME:\nAnnual Subscriptions: 65,000 - 2,400 (2013) - 1,200 (2011 levy arrears) - 12,000 (2012 levy) - life mem payment + 1,400 (2011 advance now earned) = ordinary subs\nLife membership income: (25,000 + new 5 memberships) / 5 = €6,000 per year\nLevy 2012: 150 x 80 = €12,000\nBar Profit: €13,999\nSponsorship: €73,000\nArena rent: €15,000\nInvestment interest: 22,500 x 4% = €900 received + 300 due = €1,200\n(Or: annual interest earned = 900 + 300 = 1,200)\nCatering surplus: 12,400 - 9,900 = €2,500\nLotto surplus: 52,500 - 7,600 = €44,900\n\nEXPENDITURE:\nSundry expenses: 103,600 + 2,800 wages b/f - wages c/d = €103,600 (approx)\nCoaching: €4,600\nDepreciation equipment: (23,000 + 41,000) x 20% = €12,800\nDepreciation clubhouse: 680,000 x 2% = €13,600\nLoan interest: 40,000 x 9% x 5/12 = €1,500 (Jan-May 2012)\n\nSurplus: Income less Expenditure (approx €78,000-80,000)\n\n(c)(i) A Special Purpose Profit and Loss Account is a trading account prepared for a specific activity or section of an organisation to assess its individual profitability. Example: the Bar Trading Account above shows bar sales less cost of bar sales to give bar profit. This helps the committee decide whether the bar activity is worthwhile or whether changes are needed.\n\n(c)(ii) Arguments against the levy proposal:\n1. An increase of €120 per annum is a 150% increase on current levy (€80) which is excessive\n2. Members on fixed incomes or younger members may not be able to afford it\n3. The club has €28,000 transferred to Building Society and existing reserves that could be used\n4. Alternative funding: apply for Sports Capital Programme grants, seek additional sponsors, or organise fundraising events\n5. A bank loan with repayments spread over time would be fairer than loading the full cost onto current members only"}
  ]
};
